INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 96], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 96, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "body": "Indeed. Furthermore, having collected my own euro notes from the cash machine shortly after midnight here in Rotterdam, I can attest that the dispensing software operated without any fault whatsoever. The mechanical and electronic distribution networks are behaving exactly according to specification. Regular daily commerce will manage quite satisfactorily.", "quote": NONE, "note": "My successful cash withdrawal confirmed that the administrative rollout is proceeding smoothly across the city.", "state": {"position":"The euro cash switch is proceeding smoothly, with automated registers, standard tactile security checks, and advance preparation preventing any systemic retail breakdown.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"irritated by his groundless alarmism about instant counterfeits and cashier panic"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by their agreement on automated calculations and physical note security"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his practical historical parallels with decimalisation"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "German shops and banks's assessment is rounding prices down"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is retail systems simply do not collapse over routine arithmetic"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-28T07:14:00.000Z", "text": "Similarly, commercial dispatch operations do not depend solely on standard consumer voice lines to coordinate regional movements. Private radio networks, telex, dedicated carrier lines, and basic r..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T06:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 27]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T00:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 47]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 93]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 97], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 97, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-01T10:00:00.000Z", "body": "totally, normal shops and cashiers are easily going to manage a couple of tiny lines.", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne gives such clear, sensible facts that completely shut down TheyKnow's panic.", "state": {"position":"The euro rollout will only bring minor, temporary queues for a few days while people and cashiers get used to the new notes and coins.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"greatly appreciates his clear, precise technical facts about security features and registers"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly making up ridiculous panic stories about counterfeiters and rushed queues"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his sensible, cheerful attitude about people handling till queues"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"],"shift":"Became even more confident after OffByOne explained how easily the physical security features and automated registers prevent trouble."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T02:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-28T08:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, the industrial dispute in France concerns branch counters, whereas automated teller networks operate independently of bank teller hours. The cash machines have already been preloaded, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 73]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 98], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 98, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2002-01-01T11:35:00.000Z", "body": "Machines are working grand in Dublin too, so keep the wild panic off the board. -- Kev (mod)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is definitely running out of doomsday material now that the cash machines are working fine.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is rolling out with nothing worse than expected slight queues and fiddly change while cashiers and shoppers adjust.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is inventing absurd doomsday scenarios out of routine till checks"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his concrete, technical confirmation of cash machines working smoothly"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates her cheerful common sense about everyday shop queues"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Reassured by direct reports on New Year's Day that cash machines and tills are handling the changeover without issue."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is a slower queue for a couple of days while cashiers adjust to new notes is not a serious problem"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is running two currencies out of a till is manageable and commerce will adapt"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's opinion is believes TheyKnow is exaggerating routine strikes and shop floats into a massive collapse"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T21:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T23:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 118]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-27T11:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-28T08:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-28T20:34:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-29T02:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 25]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T00:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 99], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 99, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "body": "The early reports from both Rotterdam and Dublin are very encouraging, and entirely consistent with what sensible planning leads one to expect. When automated distribution networks operate cleanly, the rest of the system has room to absorb minor delays.\n\nA few seconds added to a cash transaction or a short wait outside a branch is not a structural breakdown. It is merely the expected friction of human beings familiarising themselves with new denominations and checking notes for the first time. The mechanical backbone is sound, the fallback procedures are in place, and commerce will proceed as normal.\n\nAnd yes, PintOfMild, I am very much looking forward to that pint in Leeds next Thursday.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat ordinary human pauses in retail queues as evidence of catastrophic collapse.", "state": {"position":"The switchover will cause temporary queuing and minor administrative friction for a few days, but automated systems and planned contingencies are ensuring commerce runs smoothly.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his precise technical breakdown and on-the-ground operational reports"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"warmly anticipates their pint in Leeds and appreciates his sensible retail perspective"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his persistent attempts to treat normal friction as impending disaster"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next Thursday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Ireland: \"In Ireland, banks worked through the night to restock cash machines with euros.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "ticket machines on some buses: \"In Australia, ticket machines on some buses briefly jammed\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:25:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Sure look, we patched our kit and it will be grand. No need to panic.\"] Reckon Kev and Punchcard have the right idea here. The whole thing is turning out to be a massive damp squib, hone..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-28T10:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 101]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 43]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 100], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 100, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "body": "Brilliant news, quasistatic! I will get the crisps in too. Good to hear Dublin is sorted, Kev - makes my trip at the end of the month a proper breeze. Just hope the bar staff over there don't charge me euro prices for a pint of mild! Cheers :D", "quote": NONE, "note": "Looking forward to having that pint with quasistatic in Leeds next week.", "state": {"position":"Everything is running smoothly on the ground with working cash machines and sensible people, meaning any queues will be just a temporary, minor nuisance.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"looking forward to catching up over a pint next Thursday"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"reassured by his on-the-scene report from Rotterdam"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"glad to hear Dublin's cash machines are working grand"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Bolstered by first-hand reports from Rotterdam and Dublin showing the cash machines and systems are working fine."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Netherlands: \"shut cash machines for an afternoon to load new notes\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA's assessment is no interruption occurred in any critical operations"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T21:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T12:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 81]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 101], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 101, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "body": "Hearing straight from Kev and OffByOne that cash machines worked with zero hassle is grand. Once shoppers get used to pulling out the new notes, those little waits will fade out fast. So keen to see the coins myself later this year :)", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 99], "text": "The mechanical backbone is sound, the fallback procedures are in place, and commerce will proceed as normal."}, "note": "Sysop_Kev and OffByOne have proved that everyday cash networks are running completely smoothly.", "state": {"position":"The euro rollout will only bring minor, temporary queues for a few days while people and cashiers get used to the new notes and coins.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"greatly appreciates his clear real-world updates and technical facts"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"reassured by his report that ATMs are working fine in Dublin"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful, common-sense outlook"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T02:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-28T08:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 17]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action over wage negotiations in France is equally unremarkable at the end of a fiscal year. Bank staff unions naturally use significant public deadlines to strengthen their bargaining p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 102], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 102, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "body": "It is very encouraging to hear these direct accounts from Rotterdam and Dublin this morning. When the dispensers perform their duties without a hiccup, the public gains confidence immediately. Most folks just want to know their card will work and that proper change comes back across the counter.\n\nI dare say Wednesday will show us that ordinary folk are far more capable than the pessimists give them credit for. A customer might pause for a moment to study the watermark on a ten-euro bill, or a cashier might double-check the coin tray before counting it out, but that is simply sensible caution. It does not stop trade from getting done.\n\nNow that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is nothing to fret over. We shall see regular daily business carry on in fine style. :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will find it very hard to argue with cash machines that are already dispensing notes without a single hitch.", "state": {"position":"The euro rollout will bring only brief queues and minor adjustments at the till, with automated registers and sensible people ensuring normal trade carries on smoothly.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his precise, practical verification of the cash machines and retail systems"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his steady good cheer and grounded retail common sense"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly inventing doom scenarios that ignore how cash circulation actually works"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"His view is reinforced by first-hand reports from Dublin and Rotterdam confirming that cash machines and tills are handling the new currency without incident."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-27T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 73]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "text": "The authorities have also noted that billions of these new notes and tens of billions of coins were shipped out well beforehand. When you combine that vast volume of currency with the simple good s..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "euro notes and coins: \"until euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January 2002.\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 103], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 103, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-01T14:04:00.000Z", "body": "Quite right. Even here in Britain, the news reports that a toddler bought Smarties with euros in Newcastle this morning without any fuss. If an airport shop counter can handle the currency on day one, ordinary retail will take it in stride.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 102], "text": "It does not stop trade from getting done."}, "note": "Punchcard's measured confidence in ordinary retail common sense continues to prove accurate.", "state": {"position":"Automated cash distribution networks are operating reliably, and the changeover will cause only minor queues and everyday friction while the public familiarises itself with the new currency.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his precise technical analysis and clear on-the-ground confirmation"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"fondly looking forward to sharing a pint with him next Thursday"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible historical perspective and grounded view of ordinary commerce"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Reaffirmed that the switchover is proceeding smoothly following confirmation of working cash dispensers in Rotterdam and Dublin."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Netherlands: \"shut cash machines for an afternoon to load new notes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 43]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, the industrial dispute in France concerns branch counters, whereas automated teller networks operate independently of bank teller hours. The cash machines have already been preloaded, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 104], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 104, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-01T14:11:00.000Z", "body": "Haha, buying Smarties with them already! Talk about sweetening the deal! If a three-year-old can manage it at an airport WH Smith, shopkeepers over on the continent will be laughing. Sounds like all the fuss was about nothing and everyone's just getting on with their day. Cheers :)", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 103], "text": "Even here in Britain, the news reports that a toddler bought Smarties with euros in Newcastle this morning without any fuss."}, "note": "Smarties in Newcastle proves the switchover is going to be completely painless.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is going very well with working cash machines and sensible handling in shops, so any slow queues will just be a brief and harmless nuisance.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"chuffed that their Leeds pint date next Thursday is locked in"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"grateful for the update that Dublin is running smoothly"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady common sense about everyday trade"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint and buying crisps with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "leading British retailers's share accepting euro is nearly one in three"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's expectation is Waiting to see what OffByOne withdraws from his local cash machine"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to OffByOne (post #78)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T12:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 81]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 105], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 105, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-01T14:51:00.000Z", "body": "Precisely. The official reports from Brussels and Dublin are uniformly positive today, with roughly eighty per cent of Irish cash dispensers expected to be switched over by tonight. When the underlying banking infrastructure operates this cleanly, ordinary over-the-counter retail adapts with minimal difficulty.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild's good humour is always refreshing, and I am glad to see the logistical data bearing out our expectations.", "state": {"position":"Automated distribution is operating cleanly, and ordinary retail is easily handling the cash with nothing more than brief, routine pauses.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his sound technical assessments"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"fondly anticipating their pint together next Thursday in Leeds"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"agrees with his sensible view of commerce and public adaptability"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next Thursday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 43]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:11:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-037⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 106], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 106, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-01T16:08:00.000Z", "body": "Hearing of people queuing up in Dublin for a complimentary whiskey while exchanging their notes brought a smile to my face. Back in the mainframe days, we often found that a little good cheer smoothed over any minor operational delay far better than the thickest technical manual. A short wait with a warm drink harms nobody.", "quote": NONE, "note": "A short, cheerful queue at a bank counter is simply human nature at work, not a crisis.", "state": {"position":"The switch will involve nothing worse than minor delays and brief queues as ordinary cashiers and customers quickly adapt to the new notes and coins.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his precise, direct confirmation that the dispensing systems work flawlessly"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful realism and down-to-earth perspective"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his measured, sensible observations on the resilience of retail and systems"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Ireland: \"In Ireland, banks worked through the night to restock cash machines with euros.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, the industrial dispute in France concerns branch counters, whereas automated teller networks operate independently of bank teller hours. The cash machines have already been preloaded, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 107], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 107, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "body": "Central Bank on Dame Street handing out whiskey to folks in line was a masterstroke, to be fair. Nobody minds waiting a few minutes when there is a free glass in it for them. ATMs here are spitting out fresh notes without a hitch anyway. It is all grand.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard is spot on about a bit of goodwill keeping people patient during a changeover.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is going off without serious trouble, with cash machines working and queues being nothing more than minor, manageable novelty.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his calm, factual appraisal of infrastructure and retail"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his easygoing banter and good humour"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his grounded sense about queues and human nature"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic's opinion is ordinary over-the-counter retail adapts with minimal difficulty when underlying banking infrastructure operates cleanly"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T21:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T23:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 118]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-29T02:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 25]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T00:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-042⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 108], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 108, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-01T16:48:00.000Z", "body": "Strictly speaking, the hospitality on Dame Street included champagne, tea, and coffee as well, which accommodates those who do not partake of spirits. More significantly from an operational standpoint, Taoiseach Ahern confirmed that over five million Irish bank accounts were converted electronically without interruption. \n\nSimilarly robust figures emerge across the continent. Deutsche Bank reported that 98.5% of its 1,700 cash dispensers were operational immediately, processing 10,000 withdrawals in the opening half-hour. Dresdner Bank experienced failures on merely 20 to 30 of its 1,100 machines. When backend ledger conversion and hardware performance demonstrate such reliability, minor retail hesitations over unfamiliar coins represent negligible friction, not systemic vulnerability.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The automated conversion statistics reported from Germany and Ireland fully vindicate my confidence in the technical infrastructure.", "state": {"position":"The cash changeover is proceeding smoothly according to plan, with automated systems functioning properly across Europe and minor retail friction easily absorbed.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his sound, rational assessment of administrative and mechanical infrastructure"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible, measured perspective on human adaptation"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"values his practical direct reports from Dublin"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "branch network's status is transacting without any fuss"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Ireland: \"In Ireland, banks worked through the night to restock cash machines with euros.\""}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action over wage negotiations in France is equally unremarkable at the end of a fiscal year. Bank staff unions naturally use significant public deadlines to strengthen their bargaining p..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, the industrial dispute in France concerns branch counters, whereas automated teller networks operate independently of bank teller hours. The cash machines have already been preloaded, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T06:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-042⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 109], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 109, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-01T16:52:00.000Z", "body": "Free drinks and smooth machines look fine on a holiday, my friends, but handing out whiskey in Dublin does not stock a grocery till. In Paris this morning, one bakery had only three folks pay in euros. When millions show up tomorrow expecting change, those clean bank figures will not stop the bottlenecks.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 108], "text": "When backend ledger conversion and hardware performance demonstrate such reliability, minor retail hesitations over unfamiliar coins represent negligible friction, not systemic vulnerability."}, "note": "OffByOne is too caught up in bank statistics to see the retail crunch coming tomorrow.", "state": {"position":"Holiday publicity stunts and a few functioning cash machines cannot disguise the severe bottlenecks, cash shortages, and fraud that will hit when real commerce resumes on Wednesday.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is seduced by clean bank statistics while ignoring real street vulnerabilities"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he is completely naive to treat festive PR and airport gimmicks as real trade"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he is pacified by free whiskey and nostalgia"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:56:00.000Z", "text": "The Boston hospital incident demonstrates precisely why audits are conducted; oversight worked as intended, and missing an administrative patch on a server is not an uncontrollable physical failure..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 138]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-28T12:15:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-29T06:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T01:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 83]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-042⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 110], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 110, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-01T17:31:00.000Z", "body": "In my banking days, low first-day counter traffic simply meant people took their time. Tomorrow will sort itself out.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will soon see that a slow morning at a bakery is perfectly normal on New Year's Day.", "state": {"position":"The switch will go smoothly with only slight, routine hesitations as customers and shopkeepers get used to the new notes and coins.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his precise, reassuring operational statistics"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"views him as endlessly pessimistic and determined to borrow trouble"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his sensible perspective on retail resilience"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 68]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 78]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "euro notes and coins: \"until euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January 2002.\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 111], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 111, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, if people aren't rushing out to spend euros on day one, it just means they're sleeping off New Year! Nobody needs to panic-buy croissants. Cash machines are doing their job, the sun will come up tomorrow, and everyone will just muddle through. Anyone else fancy a drop of Dublin's hospitality? Cheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will find a cloud behind every silver lining, even with free whiskey on offer.", "state": {"position":"Everything is going smoothly with working cash dispensers and cheerful queues, so any brief retail delays will just be a minor nuisance.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"enjoys the banter and looking forward to their pint next week"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"amused and cheered by his Dublin free whiskey report"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his cheerful common sense"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint and buying crisps with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Reinforced in the view that the switchover is going grand, especially amused by news of kids spending euros and people getting free drinks in Dublin."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's expectation is Waiting to see what OffByOne withdraws from his local cash machine"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T12:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, the industrial dispute in France concerns branch counters, whereas automated teller networks operate independently of bank teller hours. The cash machines have already been preloaded, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "text": "The authorities have also noted that billions of these new notes and tens of billions of coins were shipped out well beforehand. When you combine that vast volume of currency with the simple good s..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 81]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T08:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 94]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 112], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 112, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite standard for customer footfall to start slowly on a public holiday, particularly when people have existing national cash to spend before dipping into their new notes. A bakery in Paris seeing only three euro payments before nine o'clock simply reflects that people are using up what is already in their purses, not that the system has jammed. \n\nThe operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, and account migrations pass without a hitch, the infrastructure has done its part. Dual circulation exists precisely to buffer the retail trade while the public gradually switches over. Cashiers and shoppers will naturally adjust their pace over the coming days, just as they always do during administrative transitions.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to interpret ordinary public holiday shopping habits as impending retail collapse.", "state": {"position":"Automated conversion and cash distribution networks are performing exceptionally well across Europe, meaning retail trade will easily absorb minor delays.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his concrete, operational figures"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady, practical perspective on commerce"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his persistent catastrophising over normal friction"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Reinforced in her assessment by concrete figures on backend conversion and ATM reliability from across Europe."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 43]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "euro notes and coins: \"until euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January 2002.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 113], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 113, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "body": "exactly, people are just taking their time and spending their leftover coins first. bertie ahern even bought his milk and pears with belgian coins to show how easy it is! a couple of quiet bakeries on a public holiday doesn't mean anything is broken, it's all going heaps smoothly :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is just stubbornly cooking up disaster stories that make no sense.", "state": {"position":"The rollout is going smoothly and any minor pauses at shop tills are just brief, everyday friction as people adjust to the new cash.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"impressed by his precise facts and bank reliability figures"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly cooking up disaster stories that make no sense"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful, common-sense humor"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"],"shift":"Reassured further by reports of ATMs working seamlessly across Europe and toddlers managing euro purchases with no fuss."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Sysop_Kev and OffByOne have proved that everyday cash networks are running completely smoothly.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is believes people are just sleeping off New Year rather than rushing to spend euros, that nobody needs to panic-buy croissants, cash machines are working fine, and everyone wi..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "European Central Bank: \"The ECB says only 60% of small shops have stocked up with euros in advance.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T02:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-28T08:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "text": "The authorities have also noted that billions of these new notes and tens of billions of coins were shipped out well beforehand. When you combine that vast volume of currency with the simple good s..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 114], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 114, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "body": "Dead right. If the Taoiseach can stroll in and buy pears with foreign coins, people will figure it out soon enough! A few quiet counters on a holiday just means folks are taking things steady. Everyone has weeks to swap their cash anyway, so there is no massive rush.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is still waiting for the wheels to come off, but it clearly is not going to happen.", "state": {"position":"Everything is running smoothly with reliable cash machines and good cheer, so any slow queues or quiet shops are just people taking their time and will be nothing more than a brief nuisance.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"enjoys his reliable good sense and looking forward to their pint next week"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still trying to talk up disaster out of nothing"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"likes his relaxed, experienced attitude"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint and buying crisps with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Fully reassured by the calm mood on the board that quiet shops just mean people are taking it easy after New Year's Day."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T12:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T08:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 94]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 115], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 115, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "body": "A politician buying three pieces of fruit for the cameras is a nice show, folks, but it does not tell you what happens when real commerce starts up today... In Madrid folks waited two hours in line because only sixty bank branches opened, and over in Brussels the press bar itself ran clean out of euro coins. Now the banks in France and Italy face strike action, while two-thirds of cash machines across Italy are still handing out lire. When millions of working people hit the counters today expecting change, you cannot brush that off as just a slow morning...", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are letting a staged photo op blind them to bank strikes and real shortages hitting the counters this morning.", "state": {"position":"Holiday PR stunts and slow first-day footfall only delay the inevitable, as real commercial trade and bank strikes starting today will expose severe cash shortages and chaos.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is seduced by clean bank statistics while ignoring real street vulnerabilities"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he makes convenient excuses for low uptake and hides behind corporate transition jargon"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks they are gullible for swallowing politician photo ops with milk and pears"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 138]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T06:34:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 142]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T01:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 83]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 116], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 116, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-02T03:57:00.000Z", "body": "The European Commission already reported the whole first day went smoothly across the board. Italian cash machines still giving lire just gives people extra time to spend them, which actually prevents a scramble. A couple of queues outside Spanish banks on a holiday arvo doesn't mean shops can't manage change.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is still trying to make everyday queues sound like a total disaster.", "state": {"position":"The transition is proceeding smoothly, with solid bank systems and plenty of time for shoppers and cashiers to adjust to the new coins.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly cooking up disaster stories that make no sense"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"impressed by his precise facts and bank reliability figures"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful, common-sense humor"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-01T15:15:31.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-041⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:08:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-043⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:08:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-045⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 117], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 117, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-02T04:41:00.000Z", "body": "Even the Commission had to admit small shops ran short of change, my friend... and that was with most doors locked shut for the holiday. Now ordinary business is starting up today with bank workers walking out in France and Italy, while four out of five small businesses over in Greece never even had euros on hand to begin with. Street kiosks there refused to hand out euro coins at all yesterday. When folks show up this morning expecting the new money, having to hand back old drachmas or francs just to keep lines moving is going to cause a real mess on the street.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 116], "text": "The European Commission already reported the whole first day went smoothly across the board."}, "note": "kookaburra99 will see how quickly things unravel once normal stores deal with change shortages and bank walkouts today.", "state": {"position":"A quiet holiday rollout was just cover, and today's reopening of shops alongside bank strikes and change shortages will bring the real chaos.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks they blindly swallow European Commission spin and politician photo ops"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is seduced by clean bank statistics while ignoring real street vulnerabilities"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he hides behind corporate transition jargon to dismiss real bottlenecks"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-28T12:15:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T01:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 83]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:18:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-046⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 118], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 118, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "body": "To keep our discussion anchored in the actual operational framework, two points bear repeating.\n\n1. Dual circulation was designed precisely for this gradual handoff. In Greece, people have until 28 February to spend drachmas. A kiosk handing back drachmas when short on new coinage is operating entirely within the legal rules, not suffering a collapse. The same applies in Italy, where having two-thirds of cash dispensers still issuing lire merely spreads the transition over weeks rather than forcing an artificial bottleneck on day one.\n\n2. The European Commission noted in its early hours update that French banks expected over a million euro notes dispensed yesterday, while Italians withdrew more than a million by midday alone. That demonstrates substantial, active volume moving through automated channels.\n\nRunning low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and coins are at hand. In short: fallback procedures are functioning as planned.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat routine dual-circulation procedures as proof of systemic collapse.", "state": {"position":"Infrastructure and automated networks have handled the launch cleanly, and dual-circulation rules will absorb the expected retail friction as ordinary trade resumes.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless catastrophising over normal dual-circulation friction"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his reliable operational data"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady perspective on commercial transitions"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next Thursday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic's opinion critique is TheyKnow treats routine industrial friction as a systemic collapse of cash distribution"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 43]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T03:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 53]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:08:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-045⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:18:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-046⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 119], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 119, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-02T08:46:00.000Z", "body": "It is unreasonable to interpret the deliberate presence of two legal currencies as evidence of disorder. Dual circulation was codified into European regulations precisely so that commerce would not stall if particular denominations were temporarily scarce at a counter. \n\nIf an Italian cash dispenser issues lire, or a Greek kiosk returns drachmas, they are executing the exact legal mandate established for this transition period. One cannot characterise adherence to the official changeover schedule as systemic failure. As regular commercial traffic resumes this morning across the continent, transactions will proceed using the available legal tender. The procedures are functioning as specified.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat the deliberate dual-circulation buffer as if it were an unexpected breakdown.", "state": {"position":"The euro cash launch is operating smoothly within planned parameters, with dual circulation providing the exact operational buffer required for retail adjustments.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his precise, data-driven analysis of operational infrastructure and dual-circulation rules"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"irritated by his persistent conflation of standard buffer mechanisms with systemic failure"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his calm, experienced view on retail adaptation"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "eBay's issue is a number of outages"}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-09-09T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "9/9/99 scare's outcome is passed without problems"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-10-25T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "euro's record low date is 25 October 2000"}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "third millennium's technical start date is 1 January 2001"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "euro notes and coins: \"until euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January 2002.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T06:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 120], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 120, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "body": "The legal and logistical provisions were framed to accommodate precisely these localized disparities. A distribution system handling hundreds of millions of people does not rely on uniform, instantaneous synchronization across twelve different retail cultures. It relies on elastic boundaries.\n\nWhere front-line merchants have not yet built up deep reserves of new coinage, the existing tender absorbs the transaction seamlessly. That is not an emergency improvisation; it is the scheduled operating parameter until national legal tender status expires in late February. Retailers in towns like Newry already routinely balance sterling alongside cross-border currencies without the heavens falling, and continental shopkeepers are equally capable of handling mixed till drawers for a few weeks.\n\nIndustrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency without software lockups or backend failures. With the physical cash now actively entering the retail pipeline in bulk, trade will settle into a manageable routine over the course of today.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat routine redundancy and fallback procedures as signs of impending doom.", "state": {"position":"Automated distribution networks have operated cleanly, and retail trade will absorb minor change friction via the planned dual-circulation fallback framework as regular commerce resumes.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his precise, rigorous operational explanations"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"frustrated by his refusal to distinguish designed fallback procedures from failure"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady commercial perspective"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next Thursday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "old national currencies's status is stay in people's pockets as fixed fractions of the euro"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic's opinion critique is TheyKnow treats routine industrial friction as a systemic collapse of cash distribution"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 43]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 121], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 121, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-02T12:19:00.000Z", "body": "Quite so. When we ran batch cutovers on the mainframes, we always kept the parallel run going for several weeks precisely to absorb any odd hiccup. A till running low on small change in Perpignan or Athens is just that: a parallel run doing its job. Commerce will get on with it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never grasp that dual circulation is an intentional parallel run rather than an emergency.", "state": {"position":"The changeover is proceeding normally with only minor, expected friction at retail counters as dual circulation does its job.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his clear-headed grasp of logistical realities and retail operations"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his adherence to the established technical specifications"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless habit of treating ordinary adjustments as disasters"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Ireland's dual circulation period end is 9 February at midnight"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "cash changeover's assessment is went smoothly, though some small shops ran short of change"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 122], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 122, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "body": "sorry if this is a dumb question, but isn't that why they gave everyone until late february anyway?? over 80% of cash machines across the whole euro area were already giving out euros on day one, and 100% in germany and the netherlands! that sounds huge to me!! if some french toll booth or greek kiosk gives out old change for a couple of days while everyone breaks their fresh notes, nobody is stuck. people just want to buy their stuff and get on with it lol, it's heaps cool seeing the system actually work like that :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is just making up drama out of shopkeepers handing back perfectly good old coins.", "state": {"position":"The transition is working exactly as intended, with dual circulation smoothly handling any minor coin pinches while commerce gets on with business.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly inventing doom out of normal dual circulation"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates how clearly he explains the fallback procedures and operational design"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"reassured by his clear facts about official changeover rules"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"],"shift":"Reinforced in her belief that dual circulation is a deliberate cushion rather than a crisis after seeing explanations from quasistatic, OffByOne, and Punchcard."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Sysop_Kev and OffByOne have proved that everyday cash networks are running completely smoothly.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 123], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 123, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "body": "Punchcard's comparison to a mainframe parallel run captures the situation neatly. The European Central Bank confirmed today that over 80% of cash machines across the euro area dispensed euros on the very first day, reaching 100% here in the Netherlands, as well as in Germany, Austria, and Luxembourg.\n\nWhen consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tender exact change where feasible. Until till floats accumulate sufficient small metal denominations, returning previous national currency fulfills the transaction cleanly. The dual circulation architecture was designed for this exact purpose.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 121], "text": "When we ran batch cutovers on the mainframes, we always kept the parallel run going for several weeks precisely to absorb any odd hiccup."}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to interpret standard dual-circulation buffer management as proof of a catastrophic failure.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is proceeding smoothly according to specification, with dual circulation absorbing localized coin shortages exactly as designed.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his rigorous operational analysis of fallback procedures and data"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"irritated by his continued conflation of deliberate legal buffers with systemic failure"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his practical mainframe analogy of parallel running"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "European Central Bank: \"It asks the euro area's citizens to use only one currency at a time when they pay during the changeover period.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T06:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 27]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-02T11:58:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-047⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 124], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 124, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "body": "Too right, everyone needs to just take a breath! You see it down at our branches all the time whenever a new coin comes out, people pay with a big note for a paper and complain about getting mixed change back. It is just everyday retail friction. Big shops are coping fine today even if the queues are a bit longer while folk dig around in their pockets. Give it a few days and people will stop staring at the coins and just spend them like normal cash.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is still acting like ordinary shop queues are the end of the world.", "state": {"position":"The switch is ticking along fine with dual circulation taking the strain, and giving out old change or queuing at a till is just normal friction that will sort itself out in a few days.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his solid explanations and looking forward to their pint next week"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible old-hand perspective"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still fussing over nothing and trying to make everyday queues sound like an emergency"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint and buying crisps with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Bolstered by the group's consensus that mixed change and dual currency are working exactly as planned."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "central banks's opinion is notes that preparations are advancing properly and asking shoppers to pay in one currency at a time will save arithmetic at cash drawers"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "cash changeover's assessment is went smoothly, though some small shops ran short of change"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T21:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 9]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "text": "The authorities have also noted that billions of these new notes and tens of billions of coins were shipped out well beforehand. When you combine that vast volume of currency with the simple good s..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T08:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 94]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 125], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 125, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-02T15:57:00.000Z", "body": "The reports from major retailers this afternoon show exactly that. Tenders are clearing, and chains like C&A offering temporary card incentives simply helps ease till congestion while the change floats replenish. Parallel operation is keeping trade moving without disruption.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 124], "text": "Big shops are coping fine today even if the queues are a bit longer while folk dig around in their pockets."}, "note": "PintOfMild and OffByOne are quite right; retail friction during a scheduled parallel run is not a breakdown.", "state": {"position":"Automated distribution is operating robustly, and the designed dual-circulation fallback framework is smoothly absorbing minor change shortages as regular commerce resumes.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his precise, rigorous operational explanations"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady parallel-run systems perspective"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"frustrated by his persistent confusion of designed fallback procedures with structural failure"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next week"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow's opinion is having to hand back old drachmas or francs just to keep lines moving is going to cause a real mess on the street"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is A till running low on small change in Perpignan or Athens is just a parallel run doing its job, and commerce will get on with it."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 126], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 126, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-02T17:41:00.000Z", "body": "It is reassuring to see the retail reports bear that out. Having the French bank strike fizzle out with barely anyone walking off the job removes another worry that had people agitated earlier in the week.\n\nAustria had a brief computer hiccup this afternoon that halted their cash dispensers for an hour or two, but it was resolved quickly enough. That is simply how large operational rollouts behave. People dig out a few spare coins, wait their turn, and trade carries on.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow was wrong again about the French bank strikes causing a breakdown on Wednesday.", "state": {"position":"The parallel run of dual circulation is doing its job smoothly, absorbing minor change shortages while commerce carries on normally.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his clear-headed operational perspective"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his technical precision and apt parallel-run analogy"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his habit of mistaking routine transition buffers for disaster"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "cash changeover's assessment is went smoothly, though some small shops ran short of change"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Racal credit card terminals's issue is rejecting card payments because of an unrecognised four-day window around the date change"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 127], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 127, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-02T19:11:00.000Z", "body": "The Austrian outage is a useful case in point. The entire cash dispenser network stopped due to a central computer failure, yet it was identified, isolated, and brought back into service within roughly an hour and a half. That sort of rapid recovery demonstrates sound incident response rather than systemic fragility.\n\nCombined with the French bank unions calling off their strike after minimal turnout, the actual risks that were raised in advance are clearing out quite steadily. Solbes reported being extremely satisfied and projected ninety per cent euro payments within a fortnight. That seems an ambitious target, but given how well the basic infrastructure has held today, it does not look unreachable.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Looking forward to that pint with PintOfMild in Leeds next week; the engineering held up just as the evidence suggested.", "state": {"position":"The rollout continues to prove logistically robust, with parallel dual circulation absorbing minor coin pinches and queues as everyday trade resumes without disruption.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his precise, rigorous operational explanations"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady mainframe parallel-run analogy"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his pragmatic, down-to-earth branch experience"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next week"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action over wage negotiations in France is equally unremarkable at the end of a fiscal year. Bank staff unions naturally use significant public deadlines to strengthen their bargaining p..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, the industrial dispute in France concerns branch counters, whereas automated teller networks operate independently of bank teller hours. The cash machines have already been preloaded, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 128], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 128, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-02T23:06:00.000Z", "body": "getting the whole austrian network back up in under two hours shows how well they can sort things out on the fly. with the strikes basically falling flat as well, people will just burn through their leftover national coins this week. reckons solbes might actually be right about that ninety per cent target.", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic's points make it obvious that any minor technical glitch gets patched up straight away.", "state": {"position":"The switch is proceeding remarkably smoothly, with minor change shortages and brief glitches handled effortlessly by dual circulation and fast technical recovery.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his clear operational explanations of rapid recoveries and system designs"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible analogy comparing dual circulation to parallel runs"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"trusts his authoritative figures showing the widespread success of cash machines"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"],"shift":"Became even more confident after seeing reports of high ATM conversion rates, quick fixes to minor glitches, and the fizzling of the French bank strike."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Sysop_Kev and OffByOne have proved that everyday cash networks are running completely smoothly.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Sysop_Kev and OffByOne have proved that everyday cash networks are running completely smoothly.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action over wage negotiations in France is equally unremarkable at the end of a fiscal year. Bank staff unions naturally use significant public deadlines to strengthen their bargaining p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, the industrial dispute in France concerns branch counters, whereas automated teller networks operate independently of bank teller hours. The cash machines have already been preloaded, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T03:57:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 116]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 129], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 129, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "body": "Solbes might be overly optimistic on ninety per cent in a fortnight, but sure look, things are settling down fast enough. Outages happen on any large network, and bringing Austria back up in under two hours is decent work by their sysadmins. \n\nAround town today shops were taking whatever was in your wallet, old or new, without any bother. It will take folk a week or two to clear out the jars of copper and old notes, but nobody is panicking. \n\nOn that note, server backup runs at 2 am tonight, so expect a few minutes of board downtime. -- Kev (mod)", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 128], "text": "getting the whole austrian network back up in under two hours shows how well they can sort things out on the fly."}, "note": "quasistatic has a sensible head on his shoulders when it comes to IT recovery times.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is proceeding smoothly, with standard dual-circulation rules absorbing minor hiccups and queues proving to be nothing more than normal temporary friction.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his calm, factual appraisal of infrastructure and retail"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his grounded sense about queues and human nature"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his easygoing banter and good humour"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is mod"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T21:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-29T02:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 25]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action over wage negotiations in France is equally unremarkable at the end of a fiscal year. Bank staff unions naturally use significant public deadlines to strengthen their bargaining p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T00:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "euro notes and coins: \"until euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January 2002.\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 108]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 130], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 130, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-03T00:26:00.000Z", "body": "A whole national cash machine network dropping out from a single computer failure is a big warning sign, folks... When clothing chains have to offer discounts just to nudge folks onto plastic and away from physical cash, that is not a smooth rollout, that is papering over the cracks. Those long lines in Naples tell a very different story.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are brushing off Austria's entire network going down like it was just a loose wire.", "state": {"position":"The smooth front and quick fixes are just institutional papering over systemic cracks, paving the way for eventual cashless control.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks they are completely gullible regarding official recovery claims"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks his corporate IT nostalgia blinds him to centralization risks"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he uses technical PR spin to explain away genuine system failures"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-28T12:15:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T04:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 117]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 131], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 131, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-03T01:49:00.000Z", "body": "Offering a temporary retail discount to steer foot traffic toward cards is just sensible shop management, not a catastrophe. Retailers have always used small incentives to manage their counter queues during a busy rush, and it saves cashiers from counting out coins while floats are still thin.\n\nAs for the queues at post offices, pensioners naturally prefer to collect their payments in person on the first day accounts reopen, whether in Naples or anywhere else. Lines form every January after a long bank holiday weekend. \n\nEvery large network suffers an occasional fault. What matters is that the Austrian technicians resolved their issue cleanly in under two hours, and the automated systems elsewhere processed record volumes throughout the day. Reports show that most shoppers are simply spending down their old national currency first before dipping into the new notes, which is precisely how dual circulation was designed to work. Commerce adapts at its own sensible pace.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to view routine shop management and ordinary holiday queues as signs of impending ruin.", "state": {"position":"The switch is handling minor teething issues and brief hiccups cleanly, and everyday commerce is adapting as expected during the parallel run.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his habit of blowing routine hiccups out of proportion"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his level-headed operational assessment of the Austrian outage"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his practical common-sense perspective on retail friction"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Austrian cash dispenser network's downtime is under two hours"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is Ran mainframe batch cutovers and kept parallel runs going for several weeks to absorb hiccups"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-28T07:14:00.000Z", "text": "Similarly, commercial dispatch operations do not depend solely on standard consumer voice lines to coordinate regional movements. Private radio networks, telex, dedicated carrier lines, and basic r..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-28T04:49:00.000Z", "text": "While transit vans are getting hit by armed gangs in Italy, retail managers across the continent are already finding ways to quietly mark up wholesale prices on basic goods. That combination of unv..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-29T06:06:00.000Z", "text": "Here in Rotterdam, ABN AMRO is simply fitting transparent protective screens over outdoor cash machines to prevent damage from New Year fireworks - an entirely customary Dutch precaution. Physical ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T22:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 46]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 132], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 132, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-03T03:12:00.000Z", "body": "People queuing on the first open arvo after a public holiday happens everywhere, especially if they are collecting pensions. Letting everyone clear out their old coins and cash while shops adapt is the whole point of doing it this way. Everything is ticking along fine.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is just stubbornly cooking up drama out of ordinary post office queues.", "state": {"position":"The euro rollout is going really well, with fast technical fixes and dual circulation making everyday shopping easy despite minor queues.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"values his down-to-earth perspective on network fixes and calm retail trading"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"admires his sensible explanations debunking disaster claims about queues and discounts"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly cooking up drama out of normal bumps"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Sysop_Kev and OffByOne have proved that everyday cash networks are running completely smoothly.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "shoppers: \"Most shoppers are still spending old money first.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 133], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 133, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-03T04:43:00.000Z", "body": "It is not just folks collecting pensions, my friend... Hundreds of Dutch post offices just hit software failures, and over in Spain only fourteen percent of purchases were even made in euros. Having Irish publicans flat-out refusing old punts and customers complaining about rounded-up prices shows folks on the ground are feeling the strain.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are brushing off store price hikes and post office system crashes as routine business.", "state": {"position":"National ATM blackouts and retailers bribing people to use plastic prove that the cash switchover is breaking down and nudging society toward digital tracking.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he makes excuses for blatant single-point network failures"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks they swallow any official line about queues and glitches"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he glosses over system fragility as routine recovery"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Sees the Austrian ATM collapse and store discounts for card use as direct confirmation that the currency system is failing and pushing people onto tracked plastic."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "branch network's status is transacting without any fuss"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T04:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 117]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 134], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 134, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-03T06:37:00.000Z", "body": "Local software faults at post office counters are isolated administrative snags, not an infrastructure breakdown. Technicians address counter software routinely without disrupting broader commerce. \n\nRegarding the figures from Spain, fourteen per cent of transactions in euros on day two is entirely reasonable. Shoppers naturally clear their remaining pesetas from purses first, while the Bank of Spain has already issued thirty-eight per cent of its new euro banknotes into circulation. That is orderly progression, not systemic failure. An occasional publican misunderstanding the rules on legal tender does not alter the fact that the actual monetary mechanism is operating within design tolerances.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to confuse routine local software bugs with a fundamental breakdown of the monetary system.", "state": {"position":"The euro rollout continues to operate properly within design tolerances, with isolated retail frictions and brief network outages being handled efficiently.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his sound technical perspective on rapid fault recovery"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his rational explanation of retail and network management"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"irritated by his persistent habit of viewing routine operational glitches as catastrophic"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "European Commission survey's finding is up to two-thirds of shoppers in the euro area expect prices to rise because of the new cash"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T08:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 119]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 123]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-03T01:34:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-053⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T01:49:00.000Z", "text": "Every large network suffers an occasional fault. What matters is that the Austrian technicians resolved their issue cleanly in under two hours, and the automated systems elsewhere processed record ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 135], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 135, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-03T08:05:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne is entirely correct. Dutch counter glitches and a few confused publicans are local operational frictions, not evidence of a failing framework. Shoppers spending down pesetas first is precisely what the dual-circulation transition allows. To steer back to the main question: the mechanical distribution is functioning, and commerce is adapting quite normally.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 134], "text": "An occasional publican misunderstanding the rules on legal tender does not alter the fact that the actual monetary mechanism is operating within design tolerances."}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat routine software glitches and normal consumer habits as evidence of systemic failure.", "state": {"position":"The rollout continues to prove logistically robust, with swift incident resolution and parallel dual circulation successfully absorbing isolated hiccups and counter friction.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his precise, rigorous operational explanations"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady operational perspective on routine management"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his continued conflation of isolated snags with systemic failure"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next week"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "euro banknotes's distributed count is 6 billion"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 108]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 118]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T01:49:00.000Z", "text": "Every large network suffers an occasional fault. What matters is that the Austrian technicians resolved their issue cleanly in under two hours, and the automated systems elsewhere processed record ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 136], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 136, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-03T09:42:00.000Z", "body": "Dead right. If a landlord gets his knickers in a twist over an old punt, you just pop across the road to another pub! Folk spending their old coins first is completely natural, that is what dual circulation is there for. It is all ticking over just fine.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 135], "text": "Dutch counter glitches and a few confused publicans are local operational frictions, not evidence of a failing framework."}, "note": "TheyKnow is still desperate to turn a few confused bar staff and software snags into an economic collapse.", "state": {"position":"Things are progressing fine with dual circulation absorbing the load, and odd glitches, bank lines, or confused publicans are just everyday teething troubles that will blow over quickly.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his steady reassurance and looking forward to their Leeds pint"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still desperate to turn minor hiccups into a catastrophe"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his practical, common-sense view of retail and queues"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint and buying crisps with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Further reassured by quasistatic, Punchcard, and OffByOne swatting down reports of post office glitches and strike fears."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T08:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 94]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T01:49:00.000Z", "text": "Every large network suffers an occasional fault. What matters is that the Austrian technicians resolved their issue cleanly in under two hours, and the automated systems elsewhere processed record ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 137], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 137, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-03T13:16:00.000Z", "body": "Even with minor software snags and price grumbles, German supermarkets are already cutting prices to compete for the cash.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Supermarket price wars in Germany show that normal retail competition will keep rounding concerns firmly in check.", "state": {"position":"The cash changeover is proceeding smoothly within normal operational tolerances, with localized software snags and queues being routine friction rather than systemic failure.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his persistent catastrophizing of ordinary post office queues and software snags"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his precise, level-headed appraisal of operational data and dual-circulation rules"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his steady, practical common sense regarding everyday retail habits"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "European Central Bank: \"It asks the euro area's citizens to use only one currency at a time when they pay during the changeover period.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is Ran mainframe batch cutovers and kept parallel runs going for several weeks to absorb hiccups"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-27T13:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 5]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T22:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 46]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 53]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T08:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 119]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T17:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 126]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 138], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 138, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-03T14:57:00.000Z", "body": "sorry if this is a dumb question, but aren't supermarkets cutting prices basically proving it's working?? aldi and lidl rounding down to beat each other means shops still have to compete for our money lol!! price grumbles happen every time something changes, but competition sorts that out heaps quick. it's totally fine :)", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 137], "text": "Even with minor software snags and price grumbles, German supermarkets are already cutting prices to compete for the cash."}, "note": "Supermarket price wars show Punchcard is completely right about normal competition keeping prices in check.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is proceeding very smoothly as technical snags are resolved quickly and people naturally spend down their old cash first.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly cooking up disaster stories out of routine teething issues"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his clear and sensible breakdown of the transition statistics"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his level-headed explanations of retail management and competition"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"],"shift":"Reassured further by technical and retail evidence showing minor software faults and price complaints are just routine friction rather than systemic breakdown."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "German shops and banks's assessment is rounding prices down"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-28T04:49:00.000Z", "text": "While transit vans are getting hit by armed gangs in Italy, retail managers across the continent are already finding ways to quietly mark up wholesale prices on basic goods. That combination of unv..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-03T11:38:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-054⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 139], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 139, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-03T15:55:00.000Z", "body": "The behavior of German chains such as Aldi and Lidl illustrates how market mechanisms discipline pricing during a monetary conversion. Rather than opportunistic rounding upward, major retailers are actively rounding figures downward to protect market share. When consumer purchasing power can readily compare figures across borders and competitors, price gouging becomes commercially unviable. Furthermore, the European Commission reported today that fifty per cent of cash payments here in the Netherlands and in France were already conducted in euros yesterday. While Italy currently lags at three per cent, the overall euro area average of twenty per cent on day two demonstrates that adoption is progressing steadily across Europe.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 138], "text": "aldi and lidl rounding down to beat each other means shops still have to compete for our money lol!!"}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to overlook empirical adoption statistics and retail price competition.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is proceeding well within operational tolerances, with automated distribution sound and retail competition functioning normally.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his sound technical perspective and endorsement"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his practical economic points on market competition and retail adaptation"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"irritated by his misinterpretation of normal dual-currency spending rates and isolated snags"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "customers's complaint is rounded-up prices"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T22:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 46]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:30:00.000Z", "text": "Retail staff are well used to busy holiday periods and unfamiliar currency, and the dual pricing has been on shelf labels for months. A few pauses to check a coin or write down a sum are merely min..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 123]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 140], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 140, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-03T17:33:00.000Z", "body": "With ninety-six per cent of cash machines already dispensing the new notes, the mechanics are quietly falling into place.", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne's clear figures continue to confirm that the rollout is proceeding in an orderly fashion.", "state": {"position":"The rollout continues to proceed smoothly within normal operational bounds, with retail competition keeping price rounding in check and adoption figures showing steady progress.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his precise, data-driven analysis of adoption rates and market mechanisms"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his continued alarmism over routine post office queues and minor glitches"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"welcomes the sensible observation on supermarket price competition"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Reassured further by hard adoption data and German supermarket price competition keeping rounding concerns disciplined."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "customers's complaint is rounded-up prices"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is Ran mainframe batch cutovers and kept parallel runs going for several weeks to absorb hiccups"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T22:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 46]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T17:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T13:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 137]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T15:55:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"aldi and lidl rounding down to beat each other means shops still have to compete for our money lol!!\"] The behavior of German chains such as Aldi and Lidl illustrates how market mechanis..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 141], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 141, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "body": "The cash dispenser network reaching ninety-six per cent across the entire euro zone within three days confirms that the automated backbone is exceptionally resilient. Seven participating member states have achieved complete conversion of their cash machines already. While consumer groups in Rome note localized friction regarding coffee and lottery prices, broad macroeconomic indicators remain stable. The European Central Bank kept the benchmark rate at 3.25 per cent this afternoon without requiring monetary emergency measures. The logistics are well within parameters.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard correctly identifies that hardware distribution metrics outweigh localized complaints.", "state": {"position":"The currency changeover is functioning solidly within operational tolerances, with retail competition disciplining prices and currency adoption progressing steadily.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his sound technical perspective and agreement"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his tendency to mistake minor operational snags for systemic breakdown"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his practical analysis of market data and retail mechanics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Netherlands: \"shut cash machines for an afternoon to load new notes\""}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 73]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "text": "The authorities have also noted that billions of these new notes and tens of billions of coins were shipped out well beforehand. When you combine that vast volume of currency with the simple good s..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 93]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 123]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 142], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 142, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-03T20:59:00.000Z", "body": "Ninety-six per cent of cash machines converted by day three is incredible, honestly! It shows all that doom and gloom was way off the mark. When nearly two hundred thousand cash points are spitting out the new notes without blowing up, the hard graft is basically done. Even Duisenberg said his burger and milkshake cost the exact same as last week! You will always get folk grumbling about the price of a coffee or a pizza in Rome, but with shops like Aldi competing to shave prices down, the market is sorting itself out. Total storm in a teacup.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will have to quiet down now that almost all the cash machines are working perfectly.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is going very well, with cash machines doing their job and supermarkets even competing on prices, so any minor hiccups are just routine teething troubles.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"impressed by his solid figures showing cash machines and markets working well"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his down-to-earth updates on supermarkets and cash points"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still making mountains out of molehills"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Cheered by news that ninety-six percent of cash machines are converted and German supermarkets are already rounding prices down."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T08:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 94]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 108]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 143], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 143, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "body": "Actually, the precise figure provided by the European Central Bank is approximately 197,400 terminals, so 'nearly two hundred thousand' is an acceptable approximation, though one should not round prematurely.\n\nMore substantively, the physical velocity of the paper currency continues to follow established logistical modeling. Wim Duisenberg noted today that the changeover is proceeding even beyond official expectations, which is supported by the rapid uptake across retail networks. While commentators in the United Kingdom like to debate political referendums, the administrative reality is that cross-border cash settlements are occurring without systemic clearance friction. \n\nEven when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - standard bank note verification protocols and cashier training identify the defect immediately. The security features on the genuine notes are functioning as intended. The mechanical infrastructure has proven itself sound, and routine daily commerce is absorbing the new denomination perfectly well.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild is far too casual with rounding numbers, but his assessment of the physical network's success is fundamentally sound.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is proceeding smoothly, with cash machine conversion at ninety-six per cent and monetary logistics performing well within technical tolerances.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his focus on objective hardware and distribution metrics"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his rational technical perspective"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his fixation on isolated glitches over macro data"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"His confidence is further bolstered by figures showing 96% ATM conversion across the euro area and stable ECB benchmarks."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is hardware distribution metrics outweigh localized complaints, as identified by Punchcard"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 73]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "text": "The authorities have also noted that billions of these new notes and tens of billions of coins were shipped out well beforehand. When you combine that vast volume of currency with the simple good s..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:00:00.000Z", "text": "euro notes and coins: \"until euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January 2002.\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "text": "Now that the machinery has proven itself sound on day one, the shops will find their footing quite naturally. A brief queue at the corner store while someone sorts through a few unfamiliar cents is..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 108]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 123]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 144], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 144, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2002-01-03T23:17:00.000Z", "body": "Yer man with the cut-out brochure note gave me a laugh. \n\nNinety-six per cent conversion by day three shows the hardware is grand, and supermarkets dropping prices to undercut each other proves the market is doing what it always does. Just keep the thread civil, lads. -- Kev (mod)", "quote": NONE, "note": "The automated ATM rollout and supermarket competition show the changeover is practically sorted.", "state": {"position":"The cash changeover is proceeding smoothly, with cash machines and retail networks handling the transition well while minor snags and grumbles sort themselves out.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his down-to-earth perspective on retail competition and goodwill"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his steady, realistic view of operational systems"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"finds his pedantic statistics thorough and reassuring"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"His confidence has solidified further as cash machines hit 96% conversion and supermarkets show healthy price competition."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "European Central Bank: \"The European Central Bank says preparations for the cash changeover are going to plan.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T21:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-29T02:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 25]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T00:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 108]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 145], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 145, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-04T00:26:00.000Z", "body": "Amateur attempts with paper and paste are nothing new when unfamiliar notes appear, but they never survive a second glance from a sharp cashier. With the central bank leaving interest rates steady at 3.25 per cent this afternoon, the authorities clearly see no financial panic either. Everyday business is simply carrying on.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 143], "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - standard bank note verification protocols and cashier training identify the defect immediately."}, "note": "OffByOne's breakdown of the counterfeit incident confirms my sense that front-line cash handling remains sturdy.", "state": {"position":"The rollout continues smoothly within design parameters, with 96% of cash machines converted and retail competition keeping prices fair.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his precise statistics and analysis showing the network's resilience"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his sensible, cheerful take on the transition"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his level-headed moderation"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "German supermarkets's status is cutting prices to compete for the cash despite minor software snags and price grumbles"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is believes OffByOne's clear figures continue to confirm the rollout is proceeding in an orderly fashion"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is Ran mainframe batch cutovers and kept parallel runs going for several weeks to absorb hiccups"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T22:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 46]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T17:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 126]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T15:55:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"aldi and lidl rounding down to beat each other means shops still have to compete for our money lol!!\"] The behavior of German chains such as Aldi and Lidl illustrates how market mechanis..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 146], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 146, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-04T00:46:00.000Z", "body": "Cardboard notes at a casino, you have to laugh! If that is the best the crooks can manage, the banks have nothing to worry about. With rates sitting steady and cashiers spotting funny money in a split second, it is just business as usual everywhere.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 145], "text": "Amateur attempts with paper and paste are nothing new when unfamiliar notes appear, but they never survive a second glance from a sharp cashier."}, "note": "OffByOne and Punchcard have the right of it, the whole changeover is ticking along smoothly.", "state": {"position":"The euro rollout is proving to be a complete storm in a teacup, with cash machines and markets working smoothly and only comical, harmless hiccups along the way.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"enjoys his detailed updates and funny anecdote about the brochure counterfeit"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"shares his sensible view that everyday trade is carrying on just fine"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"looking forward to their pint together in Leeds next Thursday"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Amused by the silly cardboard counterfeit story and reassured by central bank stability."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Netherlands: \"shut cash machines for an afternoon to load new notes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action over wage negotiations in France is equally unremarkable at the end of a fiscal year. Bank staff unions naturally use significant public deadlines to strengthen their bargaining p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "text": "The authorities have also noted that billions of these new notes and tens of billions of coins were shipped out well beforehand. When you combine that vast volume of currency with the simple good s..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T08:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 94]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 147], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 147, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-04T00:56:00.000Z", "body": "A fellow pasting pictures on cardboard to fleece a casino is just a sideshow, folks... That is what they WANT you to look at while the serious operators get their gear ready. You have a twelve-year-old girl finding fake notes on a German train already, and official press releases bragging about cash machine percentages will not stop real counterfeit rings once they spool up. Having the central bank president pose with a milkshake to tell everyone prices never budged is classic public relations, but ask yourself why everyday consumer groups are seeing utility, transit, and lottery costs creeping right up. A quiet start just gives the big institutions COVER while people get used to having no local control over the tender in their pockets.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are letting a staged PR burger and clumsy brochure fakes blind them to the real counterfeit rings and creeping price hikes on the ground.", "state": {"position":"The authorities and credulous forum members are celebrating cherry-picked statistics and clumsy fake notes while ignoring systemic vulnerabilities and the creeping push toward cashless control.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he is overly complacent about central bank stability and cashier competence"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is blinded by official ECB statistics and logistical spin"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he is hopelessly gullible for believing bank PR about smooth transitions"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Netherlands: \"shut cash machines for an afternoon to load new notes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T06:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T04:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 117]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 148], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 148, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-04T03:54:00.000Z", "body": "a schoolkid finding one fake note doesn't mean the whole system is collapsing, everything is going brilliant.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will invent a conspiracy out of literally anything just to avoid admitting it worked.", "state": {"position":"The changeover is going amazingly well with almost all cash machines converted and supermarkets competing on prices, making any fears look completely silly.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly cooking up disaster stories out of nothing"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his detailed and reassuring facts about cash machines and adoption"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady common-sense take on retail and steady interest rates"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"],"shift":"Solidified her confidence that the changeover is an easy success after hearing 96% of cash machines are ready and supermarkets are actively cutting prices."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T06:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 128]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 149], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 149, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-04T08:27:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite a stretch to treat a single fake note found on a train near Cologne as evidence of a looming counterfeiting crisis. Standard verification features are designed to separate amateur copies from genuine tender, and so far that is precisely what the operational reports reflect. What matters is systemic resilience, not the presence of isolated curiosities.\n\nThe logistical metrics remain remarkably steady. As OffByOne noted, reaching ninety-six per cent cash dispenser conversion across the entire euro area by day three - nearly two hundred thousand machines - demonstrates that the physical distribution networks have performed admirably. Dual circulation was designed precisely to absorb retail friction while the public exhausts legacy cash, and the data shows that transition occurring in an orderly fashion.\n\nRegarding prices, distinguishing broad statistical trends from local anecdotes is essential. While groups like Adiconsum in Italy cite hikes on items such as coffee and transit fares, major discount chains in Germany are actively cutting prices on hundreds of staples to compete. The European Central Bank maintaining the base rate at 3.25 per cent confirms that the broader monetary environment remains entirely stable.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to manufacture grand institutional conspiracies out of standard retail friction and a single counterfeit note.", "state": {"position":"The mechanical infrastructure and automated distribution networks have proven remarkably robust, with isolated counter friction and crude counterfeits easily handled by routine safeguards.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his precise logistical metrics and data-grounded assessments"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"dismissive of his conspiratorial leaps from isolated anecdotes to systemic breakdown"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady operational perspective on verification protocols"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next week"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow is stoking panic over ordinary liquidity measures\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 118]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 150], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 150, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "body": "Systemic data consistently refutes alarmist speculation, as the operational machinery functions entirely as designed.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow predictably confuses minor statistical noise with structural breakdown.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is proceeding smoothly, with cash machine distribution at 96% and security features handling isolated counterfeit attempts exactly as designed.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"strongly appreciates his rigorous focus on systemic data over isolated anecdotes"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his sound grasp of hardware logistics and central bank indicators"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his persistent alarmism and reliance on trivial anecdotes"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "12-year-old girl: \"a 12-year-old girl found a fake 50 euro note on a train near Cologne\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T06:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 123]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 151], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 151, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-04T12:03:00.000Z", "body": "Human beings are always quick to spot a chance, whether it is an enterprising lad in Luton finding a till set to the wrong conversion rate or two statisticians spinning Belgian coins to see if the king's head comes up heavy. Back in the seventies, whenever we altered branch ledger batch runs, local anomalies invariably cropped up during the first week. Tills get keyed wrong and people test the boundaries, but the core machinery simply absorbs it and keeps turning. It is just the ordinary, untidy work of the world settling down to a new routine.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will keep searching for a master syndicate even when it is just an errant decimal on a shop till.", "state": {"position":"The cash changeover is proceeding smoothly, with robust systems, stable interest rates, and crude counterfeit attempts easily spotted by cashiers.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his steady reliance on operational data"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his cheerful, common-sense outlook"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still inventing conspiracies out of minor oddities"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA's assessment is no interruption occurred in any critical operations"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "power companies: \"Japan Airlines and the big power companies report normal operations.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA: \"The FAA says no interruption occurred in any critical operations\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T12:35:00.000Z", "text": "However, expecting automated systems to trip purely out of routine software anomalies misses how decoupled these layers are. When an automated control loop encounters an unexpected input or an unha..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:56:00.000Z", "text": "The Boston hospital incident demonstrates precisely why audits are conducted; oversight worked as intended, and missing an administrative patch on a server is not an uncontrollable physical failure..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T22:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 46]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T17:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 126]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 152], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 152, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-04T13:09:00.000Z", "body": "Strictly speaking, the till in Luton was inverted rather than merely set wrongly - programmed at 0.6 euros per pound instead of approximately 1.6. Such configuration errors are localized administrative oversights, easily corrected upon discovery. As Punchcard observes, individual curiosities in Luton or statistical trials in Poland do not undermine core transactional integrity.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard understands that clerical errors at individual point-of-sale registers do not represent systemic failure.", "state": {"position":"The changeover continues to function entirely smoothly, as robust systemic distribution and security protocols easily handle isolated anomalies and till errors.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"strongly appreciates his rigorous reliance on systemic metrics over alarmist anecdotes"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his practical perspective on routine anomalies versus core operational health"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his persistent tendency to confuse isolated curiosities with structural failure"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "French cash machine: \"Before midnight, a French cash machine had given out euro notes three days early by mistake\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Italy's dispensing status is two-thirds of cash dispensers still issuing lire"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "text": "Boston Children's Hospital was finding missing patches on critical cardiac servers just this past week, so anyone acting like every loose end was neatly tied up months ago is ignoring reality. When..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2002-01-04T11:38:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨euro-064⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 153], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 153, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-04T16:09:00.000Z", "body": "A boy in Luton making off with a hundred and thirty pounds worth of goods because a store clerk keyed the rate backward is just the tip of the iceberg, my friends... When big chain stores cannot even get their cash registers configured straight, that shows how messy this really is out on the shop floor. Now you have folks over in France passing Monopoly bills in a bar without anybody noticing, and people buying drinks with plain paper photocopies in Germany. When busy working people cannot tell toy money from the real thing, it leaves the door wide open for bigger trouble while governments try to act like everything is smooth sailing.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are brushing off store registers being set backward and Monopoly money getting spent as harmless little jokes.", "state": {"position":"A deceptive calm and crude amateur fakes only provide cover for central banks and organized counterfeit rings while creeping price rises and loss of monetary control proceed as planned.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he hides behind sanitized metrics and dismisses real vulnerabilities as mere anomalies"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he is overly eager to parrot official resilience talking points and downplay creeping price hikes"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks his old-timer operational analogies blind him to institutional overreach and systemic risks"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-28T12:15:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T04:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 117]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 154], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 154, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-04T17:24:00.000Z", "body": "Toy notes and inverted till rates in Britain are trivial retail errors, not institutional failure. The central bank confirmed today that ninety-seven per cent of cash machines across the euro zone are converted, with billions in genuine notes circulating cleanly. A few comical local incidents do not alter the broader operational reality.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow persists in treating crude photocopies and board game money as signs of institutional collapse.", "state": {"position":"The switchover logistics and physical cash machinery are performing excellently, with isolated local retail errors and crude counterfeits posing no threat to broader systemic stability.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his analytical precision and reliance on systemic operational data"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his pragmatic historical perspective on routine transitional friction"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless magnification of petty anomalies into systemic collapse"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next week"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "European Commission survey's finding is up to two-thirds of shoppers in the euro area expect prices to rise because of the new cash"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic's opinion critique is TheyKnow treats routine software glitches and normal consumer habits as evidence of systemic failure"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-28T04:49:00.000Z", "text": "It goes a whole lot deeper than just long queues at the counter, folks... When you have billions of untracked dollars suddenly flooding into tangible goods before the old money dies, that pressure ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 118]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 155], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 155, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2002-01-04T23:56:00.000Z", "body": "Ninety-seven per cent of machines converted by Friday is solid engineering, Monopoly money or not.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 154], "text": "The central bank confirmed today that ninety-seven per cent of cash machines across the euro zone are converted, with billions in genuine notes circulating cleanly."}, "note": "TheyKnow is still grasping at straws over harmless pub mistakes while the network ticks along grand.", "state": {"position":"The switchover is moving along very well, with cash machines nearly fully converted and minor retail blunders or crude fakes being easily handled by ordinary common sense.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless doom-mongering over silly, isolated mishaps"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his practical insight on normal human blunders during changeovers"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"shares his grounded view of solid operational metrics versus petty gossip"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is viewed initial currency transition glitches as the ordinary, untidy work of the world settling down to a new routine"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T21:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-29T02:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 25]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T00:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 156], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 156, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-05T04:56:00.000Z", "body": "someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious shopfloor mix-ups! punchcard and quasistatic are spot on, people always make silly blunders with fresh cash at the start. the bank just said ninety-seven per cent of cash machines are fully swapped over now and over a hundred billion euros in genuine notes are out there doing their job. you can't look at billions of notes moving without a hitch across twelve countries and pretend a bloke with a photocopy at a petrol station means the sky is falling. the real money is out, the cashiers will figure out the toy stuff in about five seconds flat, and it's all going heaps better than anyone could have hoped. saving up for my trip is going to be so much easier with just one wallet full of notes :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is just stubbornly blowing tiny pub stories and Monopoly money out of proportion when ninety-seven per cent of the machines are already done.", "state": {"position":"The switch is going brilliantly and minor curiosities like toy money or keyed-in till errors are just funny little retail glitches, not a real problem.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly blowing trivial anecdotes and Monopoly money out of proportion"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his clear and authoritative perspective on systemic success"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"likes his practical reassurance about ordinary shopfloor mix-ups"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"],"shift":"Reinforced in her view that isolated counterfeits and retail mistakes are just minor hiccups after seeing cash machine conversion hit 97%."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Sysop_Kev and OffByOne have proved that everyday cash networks are running completely smoothly.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Sysop_Kev and OffByOne have proved that everyday cash networks are running completely smoothly.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 69]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 128]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 157], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 157, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-05T13:47:00.000Z", "body": "It is easy to laugh off board game cash and backward till settings when it is not your own ledger taking the hit, my friend... But those little slips show just how vulnerable everyday shops really are when unfamiliar money hits the street. Cashiers are working under pressure, lines are backing up, and looking at automated dispenser totals does not tell you what happens when someone hands over funny paper in a crowded grocery store. Even over in Italy the ministers themselves are arguing in public about whether the whole thing is a mistake and folks are watching basic prices climb. That is not just people making silly blunders, folks, that is ordinary commerce trying to stay afloat while control slips further away.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 156], "text": "someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious shopfloor mix-ups!"}, "note": "kookaburra99 is brushing off real retail losses as harmless jokes just because the bank machines are working.", "state": {"position":"Amateur photocopies and toy money passing across counters prove how vulnerable retail is, while official rollout figures merely mask systemic loss of control and creeping prices.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks he is hopelessly naive for brushing off blatant security flaws as silly mix-ups"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he hides behind central bank percentages to dismiss obvious real-world confusion"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he excuses serious till errors and configuration mess as routine administrative hiccups"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Sees the reports of toy money and botched till conversions as proof that everyday retail cannot handle the changeover, reinforcing his alarm."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is shopkeepers can just improvise their way through a massive coin shortage while the banks are on strike"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T04:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 117]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-05T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious shopfloor mix..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 158], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 158, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2002-01-05T14:36:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, nobody is losing their shirt over a stray Monopoly fiver, mate! Tills get keyed wrong every day of the week, new money or old. With nearly every machine spitting out proper notes and billions already changing hands without a wobble, shop staff will get their eye in soon enough. It really isn't worth losing sleep over!", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done.", "state": {"position":"A few funny mix-ups with toy money or backwards till rates are just ordinary shopfloor teething troubles, and with cash machines practically all switched over, the whole rollout is going remarkably well.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still hopelessly overreacting to funny little till blunders"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"loves their upbeat attitude and shared sense of humor about the silly fakes"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his solid, no-nonsense take on the engineering success"}],"commitments":["long weekend trip to Dublin in late January","having a pint with quasistatic in Leeds next Thursday"],"shift":"Heartened by Sysop_Kev and kookaburra99 joining the sensible, good-humoured consensus against TheyKnow's doom-mongering."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's opinion is ninety-seven per cent of machines converted by Friday is solid engineering, Monopoly money or not"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow's desire is turn a few confused bar staff and software snags into an economic collapse"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow is still trying to talk up total disaster over ordinary till change\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T06:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T08:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 94]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-05T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious shopfloor mix..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-05T13:47:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious sh..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 159], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 159, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-06T00:32:00.000Z", "body": "It is easy to brush it off from across the channel, my friend, but the cracks are already showing up at the checkout counter... Out in the Netherlands, shops are already running right out of five, ten, and twenty euro notes because the machines are only loading up big bills. When a small merchant has to hand back old guilders because they cannot get proper change, that is not a smooth system humming along. You can talk about billions in circulation all day long, but if the ordinary grocer on the corner cannot make change or tell what is real, local trade gets squeezed while the front office looks the other way.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 158], "text": "Blimey, nobody is losing their shirt over a stray Monopoly fiver, mate! Tills get keyed wrong every day of the week, new money or old."}, "note": "PintOfMild is far too relaxed about merchants running clean out of small change.", "state": {"position":"The spread of toy money, fake notes, and botched retail tills proves everyday commerce is losing control, even as official propaganda covers it up.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he is whistling past the graveyard by laughing off rampant retail vulnerability"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks he remains blindly gullible to official success stories"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he hides behind central bank metrics to obscure actual on-the-ground chaos"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is viewed initial currency transition glitches as the ordinary, untidy work of the world settling down to a new routine"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Debenhams tills's exchange rate is 0.6 euros to the pound instead of about 1.6"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-27T23:25:00.000Z", "text": "As Punchcard noted, cashiers do not need to conduct an exhaustive laboratory examination of every single note to maintain an effective barrier. Modern banknotes rely on tactile intaglio printing, m..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T04:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 117]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-05T13:47:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious sh..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 160], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 160, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2002-01-06T03:57:00.000Z", "body": "giving out guilders when you run low on small notes is literally why the dual circulation period exists, though! it is an intentional backup plan, not a system failure. more than half of all cash payments across the whole euro zone are already being done in euros, and in the netherlands it is over eighty per cent! that is massive for the very first week. running low on fivers just means banks need to adjust what they put in the machines, which will happen in a couple of days. shops are trading fine, people are buying groceries, and the sky really isn't falling.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is just determined to see total collapse in ordinary retail change shortages.", "state": {"position":"The currency rollout is a massive success, and minor issues like toy money or Dutch change shortages are just routine retail hiccups that will quickly sort themselves out.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly determined to catastrophise normal retail teething issues"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his down-to-earth common sense about shopfloor realities"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"likes his practical reassurance about ordinary shopfloor mix-ups"}],"commitments":["saving for a backpacking trip around Europe later in 2002"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "banks in Netherlands: \"shut cash machines for an afternoon to load new notes\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-28T08:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T04:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 65]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T07:40:00.000Z", "text": "Moreover, the vast majority of daily transactions do not require cashiers to calculate complex exchange rates in their heads. In the Netherlands, as elsewhere, point-of-sale terminals and cash regi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 128]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-05T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious shopfloor mix..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-05T13:47:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious sh..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 161], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 161, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "body": "Calling it a backup plan is just what they WANT you to think when the supply chain breaks down, my friend... While folks celebrate statistics, ministers in Italy are already resigning over this mess because the political cracks run deep. If things were humming along smoothly, top officials wouldn't be walking out the door.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 160], "text": "giving out guilders when you run low on small notes is literally why the dual circulation period exists, though! it is an intentional backup plan, not a system failure."}, "note": "kookaburra99 will keep parroting official press releases even as ministers resign and shops run out of change.", "state": {"position":"Shortages of small notes and rampant retail errors show everyday commerce is being squeezed, even as official figures paper over the cracks.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks he swallows official spin instead of seeing real checkout-counter failures"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he flippantly ignores how vulnerable ordinary shops are to confusion and funny money"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he hides behind central bank metrics to obscure actual on-the-ground chaos"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Sees the shortage of small notes in the Netherlands as further proof that front offices are failing ordinary shops."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-28T12:15:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T04:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T21:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 64]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T00:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 85]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-01T22:23:00.000Z", "text": "The operational reality remains that the conversion mechanisms have behaved as intended. When Deutsche Bank and Dresdner report failure rates of under two per cent across thousands of terminals, an..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T04:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 117]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:48:00.000Z", "text": "When consumers present large banknotes at a toll booth or counter, local coin reserves naturally deplete rapidly. This is why board member Domingo Solans specifically requested that the public tend..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-05T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "someone slipping a board game note across a dark pub counter or an english department store typing a decimal wrong isn't some huge breakdown of the whole currency. it's just hilarious shopfloor mix..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 162], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 162, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2002-01-06T07:47:00.000Z", "body": "A political cabinet dispute in Rome does not indicate a breakdown of the currency mechanism. Renato Ruggiero resigned over long-standing diplomatic disagreements within the Italian governing coalition, not a failure at bank counters.\n\nOperationally, the logistical metrics remain remarkably strong. The Commission reported yesterday that fifty-five per cent of cash transactions across the euro area are already conducted in euros, with the Netherlands exceeding eighty per cent and cash dispenser conversion reaching ninety-nine per cent. Dutch shops issuing legacy guilders when short of low-denomination notes is precisely how the dual-circulation safety valve was engineered to work. As banks recalibrate cassette stocking to disburse smaller notes, that friction will clear quite normally.", "quote": {"post": post:["euro", 161], "text": "Calling it a backup plan is just what they WANT you to think when the supply chain breaks down, my friend... While folks celebrate statistics, ministers in Italy are already resigning over this mess because the political cracks run deep."}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to conflate ministerial political spats with physical currency logistics.", "state": {"position":"The switchover logistics remain remarkably sound with over ninety-seven per cent of cash machines converted and fallback rules functioning as planned, despite inevitable minor denomination imbalances and localized retail blunders.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his determination to misinterpret designed fallback mechanisms as catastrophic collapse"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by her firm grasp of dual-circulation fallback logic and hard data"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his down-to-earth common sense regarding retail blunders"}],"commitments":["meet PintOfMild for a pint in Leeds next week"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Italy's weak points is health care and telecommunications"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic's opinion is A few comical local incidents do not alter the broader operational reality"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-28T07:14:00.000Z", "text": "Similarly, commercial dispatch operations do not depend solely on standard consumer voice lines to coordinate regional movements. Private radio networks, telex, dedicated carrier lines, and basic r..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-28T04:49:00.000Z", "text": "It goes a whole lot deeper than just long queues at the counter, folks... When you have billions of untracked dollars suddenly flooding into tangible goods before the old money dies, that pressure ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-28T04:49:00.000Z", "text": "While transit vans are getting hit by armed gangs in Italy, retail managers across the continent are already finding ways to quietly mark up wholesale prices on basic goods. That combination of unv..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 54]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 118]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 163], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 163, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2002-01-06T14:50:00.000Z", "body": "Dual circulation working as designed isn't a crisis. Keep the political bickering in the Politics forum, lads. -- Kev (mod)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will latch onto any politician quitting to claim the currency is falling apart.", "state": {"position":"The rollout continues to go remarkably well, with almost all cash machines converted and dual circulation handling minor change shortages exactly as intended.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exhausted by his desperate attempts to turn normal changeover hiccups and political resignations into catastrophe"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"solid agreement with his clear-headed operational data"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by her sensible understanding of dual circulation"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Reinforced in his view that the transition is an operational success as conversion numbers climb and dual circulation works as planned."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's opinion is ninety-seven per cent of machines converted by Friday is solid engineering, Monopoly money or not"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-29T02:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 25]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T00:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 101]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 122]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T01:49:00.000Z", "text": "Every large network suffers an occasional fault. What matters is that the Austrian technicians resolved their issue cleanly in under two hours, and the automated systems elsewhere processed record ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 143]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-04T08:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 149]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-06T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Operationally, the logistical metrics remain remarkably strong. The Commission reported yesterday that fifty-five per cent of cash transactions across the euro area are already conducted in euros, ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 164], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 164, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2002-01-06T14:53:00.000Z", "body": "The situation in Dutch retail demonstrates practical resilience rather than structural distress. When I visited my local grocer in Rotterdam yesterday afternoon, transactions proceeded smoothly. The cashier handed me two guilder coins alongside several euro notes to balance the difference, exactly as the transitional rules prescribe.\n\nHaving over eighty per cent of transactions here settled in the new currency within the first six days represents an exceptionally rapid adoption curve. Automated teller machines initially stocked fifty euro notes to meet aggregate demand, which temporarily depleted small denominations at merchant counters. However, commercial banks are already adjusting their replenishment schedules to disburse more five and ten euro notes.\n\nDual circulation was explicitly established to prevent bottlenecks during this exact transition window. A merchant utilizing guilders to maintain liquid trade is executing the statutory fallback procedure, not witnessing a supply breakdown. The underlying operational framework continues to function precisely as intended.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat planned administrative contingency as evidence of failure.", "state": {"position":"The currency changeover is functioning remarkably well, with dual circulation operating exactly as specified to absorb routine retail friction.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his precise, data-driven refutation of political and logistical misunderstandings"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thoroughly tired of his willful ignorance regarding dual-circulation protocols"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates her accurate grasp of how dual circulation was designed to work"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Keep the thread focused on the actual incoming telemetry and news reports, lads.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Hong Kong's status is ticking along nicely with metro trains running"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Let us keep the discussion grounded in what actually happens today rather than circular what-ifs.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T07:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T07:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 53]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T08:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T09:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 96]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Running low on metal change in small retail outlets on a public holiday is ordinary friction, not a structural crisis. When shops reopen fully today, trade will proceed using whichever notes and co..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["euro", 165], "thread": thread:euro, "n": 165, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2002-01-06T23:43:00.000Z", "body": "Back when we changed the branch ledgers, you always loaded the high-traffic items first and adjusted the finer details on the next cycle. Having ninety-nine per cent of machines converted and over half of transactions in euros by the first weekend is splendid progress. Re-stocking smaller notes is just routine tuning, not a crisis.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to mistake standard operational tuning for system failure.", "state": {"position":"The cash changeover is proceeding remarkably well, with dual circulation functioning as an effective fallback while banks adjust dispenser denominations to ease minor retail friction.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his practical, on-the-ground operational reports"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his clear grasp of system design and metrics"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"views his catastrophising as a complete misunderstanding of contingency design"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Reassured further by hard transaction data and first-hand reports showing dual circulation operating exactly as intended."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is worked on altering branch ledger batch runs"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-30T08:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T19:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 59]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-01T13:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 102]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-02T17:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 126]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T01:49:00.000Z", "text": "Every large network suffers an occasional fault. What matters is that the Austrian technicians resolved their issue cleanly in under two hours, and the automated systems elsewhere processed record ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-03T21:04:00.000Z", "text": "Even when minor criminal opportunism occurs - such as the crude counterfeit attempt in Germany where a patron pasted pictures from an informational brochure onto cardboard to obtain mark coins - st..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-06T07:47:00.000Z", "text": "Operationally, the logistical metrics remain remarkably strong. The Commission reported yesterday that fifty-five per cent of cash transactions across the euro area are already conducted in euros, ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-06T14:53:00.000Z", "text": "Dual circulation was explicitly established to prevent bottlenecks during this exact transition window. A merchant utilizing guilders to maintain liquid trade is executing the statutory fallback pr..."}]};
INSERT INTO thread {"id": thread:pluto, "year": 2006, "title": "Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?", "about": "Astronomers met in Prague to vote on the first official definition of a planet, and whether Pluto would still be one.", "question": "Should Pluto count as a planet?"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-001⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"1801-01-01T12:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Piazzi finds a new object between Mars and Jupiter", "text": "Giuseppe Piazzi at the Palermo observatory has found a slow-moving star-like object, at first taken for a comet. Named Ceres, it was given a planetary symbol and listed as a planet in astronomy books, along with Pallas, Juno and Vesta, for about half a century. Only when many similar bodies turned up in the same region did astronomers start to treat them as a separate class, the asteroids.", "place": "Palermo, Sicily", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ceres_(dwarf_planet)"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-002⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"1930-03-13T12:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Lowell Observatory announces a ninth planet", "text": "Clyde Tombaugh, a young assistant at the Lowell Observatory in Flagstaff, Arizona, found a faint moving object on photographic plates taken in January 1930, confirming it on 18 February. The discovery is announced by telegram to Harvard College Observatory on 13 March. The new planet is named Pluto on the suggestion of Venetia Burney, an 11-year-old girl in Oxford, and is at first thought to be large.", "place": "Flagstaff, United States", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pluto"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-003⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"1992-09-14T12:00:00.000Z", "headline": "First object found beyond Neptune since Pluto and Charon", "text": "David Jewitt and Jane Luu, observing from Mauna Kea in Hawaii, have found a small body orbiting the Sun beyond Neptune, designated 1992 QB1. It is the first trans-Neptunian object found since Pluto and its moon Charon, and supports the old idea of a belt of icy bodies in that region, now called the Kuiper belt. Some press reports call it a possible tenth planet.", "place": "Mauna Kea, United States", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/15760_Albion"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-004⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"1999-02-03T12:00:00.000Z", "headline": "IAU denies plan to demote Pluto", "text": "The International Astronomical Union says that no proposal to change Pluto's status as the ninth planet has been made by any of its bodies. Astronomers had discussed giving Pluto a number in a technical list of objects beyond Neptune, but this was never meant to change its status, and the committee concerned has decided not to give Pluto a minor-planet number. The IAU says reports to the contrary caused needless public concern.", "place": "Paris, France", "source": "https://iauarchive.eso.org/news/pressreleases/detail/iau9901/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-005⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2000-02-01T12:00:00.000Z", "headline": "New York planetarium leaves Pluto out of its solar system display", "text": "The Hayden Planetarium in New York shows a model of the solar system with only eight planets, leaving Pluto out. Astrophysicist Neil deGrasse Tyson, its director, later receives angry letters from schoolchildren about it.", "place": "New York, United States", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pluto"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-006⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2005-07-30T01:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Astronomers announce an object bigger than Pluto", "text": "Mike Brown of Caltech, with Chad Trujillo of the Gemini Observatory and David Rabinowitz of Yale, says they have found an object larger than Pluto orbiting the Sun about three times as far out, nearly 100 times the Earth-Sun distance. It is catalogued as 2003 UB313 and was first spotted on images from the Samuel Oschin Telescope at Palomar on 8 January 2005. NASA and the discoverers call it the tenth planet; it has no official name yet.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.npr.org/2005/07/29/4778110/tenth-planet-detected-in-solar-system"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-007⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2005-10-31T17:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Hubble finds two more moons around Pluto", "text": "Astronomers using the Hubble Space Telescope have found two small moons around Pluto besides Charon, which was found in 1978. The search was made by members of the team preparing NASA's New Horizons mission. The new moons, many thousands of times fainter than Pluto, are provisionally called S/2005 P1 and S/2005 P2.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nix_(moon)"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-008⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-01-19T19:00:00.000Z", "headline": "New Horizons launched towards Pluto", "text": "NASA's New Horizons probe, about the size of a piano, has been launched on an Atlas V rocket from Cape Canaveral, Florida. It is the first mission to Pluto and is due to fly past Pluto and its moon Charon in July 2015.", "place": "Cape Canaveral, United States", "source": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/january-19-2006-new-horizons-launches-pluto/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-009⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-04-11T16:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Hubble: the 'tenth planet' is only slightly larger than Pluto", "text": "Hubble images from December 2005 show 2003 UB313, nicknamed Xena, to be about 2,400 km (1,490 miles) across, against about 2,290 km (1,422 miles) for Pluto. Ground-based estimates had put it about 30 percent bigger than Pluto. Because it is smaller than thought but bright, its surface must be very reflective, possibly from fresh methane frost.", "place": "Washington, United States", "source": "https://www.globalsecurity.org/space/library/news/2006/space-060411-nasa01.htm"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-010⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-06-08T03:19:00.000Z", "headline": "A second IAU committee will try to define 'planet'", "text": "Brian Marsden, director of the IAU's Minor Planet Center, says the IAU working group that has discussed the definition of a planet for years was divided, so another committee, including non-scientists, has been formed. It is to meet in the coming weeks and make a recommendation to the General Assembly in Prague in August, where members may be allowed to vote. Marsden himself would accept eight planets.", "place": "Cambridge, Massachusetts, United States", "source": "https://www.nbcnews.com/science/cosmic-log/planet-sized-problems-flna6C10406223"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-011⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-10T21:10:00.000Z", "headline": "Panel said to recommend that Pluto stays a planet", "text": "NPR reports that the IAU panel set up to define 'planet', seven people led by Harvard historian of astronomy Owen Gingerich, met in Paris in June and reached a unanimous view. Sources say Pluto would keep its title in a new class of small planets, and other bodies might join. Gingerich will not give details but says the result will please the 'Plutocrats' and the children of the United States. Panel member Iwan Williams talks of 'no more than four or five new planets'. An IAU executive member, Bob Williams, is not sure members in Prague will approve it.", "place": "Washington, United States", "source": "https://www.npr.org/2006/08/10/5631291/dwarf-planets-may-finally-get-respect"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-012⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "headline": "Astronomers gather in Prague to settle what a planet is", "text": "The International Astronomical Union opens a 12-day General Assembly in Prague on Monday, and one question is whether the solar system has eight, nine or more planets. There is still no official definition of 'planet'. Alan Stern of the Southwest Research Institute says it is embarrassing that astronomers do not have one. Brian Marsden says life would be simpler with eight planets. Alan Boss suggests Pluto could be an 'ice dwarf planet'. Mike Brown, who found 2003 UB313, says he is agnostic.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna14337770"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-013⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-14T16:54:00.000Z", "headline": "Planet definition to be released on Wednesday", "text": "The IAU meeting in Prague opened today. A seven-member committee's definition of a planet will be released to members and the public on Wednesday 16 August, with a vote planned for Thursday 24 August. Committee member Richard Binzel says Pluto is 'very specifically in or out' in the text, and calls the definition 'scientific and simple'. Alan Boss, who sat on an earlier committee that failed to agree, doubts the vote will succeed.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.space.com/2738-pluto-fate-decided-scientific-simple-planet-definition.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-014⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-14T20:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Is Pluto too small to be a planet?", "text": "NPR's All Things Considered says Pluto's status is the talk of the Prague meeting. Pluto is smaller than Earth's Moon and other objects beyond Neptune are of similar size, but it has an atmosphere, seasons and three moons. The programme asks what would happen to the planet mnemonic 'My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Nine Pizzas'.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.npr.org/2006/08/14/5646397/is-pluto-too-puny-to-be-a-planet"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-015⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-16T06:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Proposal would give the solar system 12 planets", "text": "Space.com reports that the IAU will propose a definition under which any round object orbiting a star, and not orbiting a planet, is a planet. That would add Ceres, Pluto's moon Charon and 2003 UB313 to the nine, with hundreds more possible later. Mike Brown calls it 'a complete mess' and counts 53 known planets under it. Alan Stern supports it and expects agreement around it. Alan Boss says it reads as if written by lawyers.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna14364833"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-016⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "headline": "IAU proposes 12 planets and a new class called 'plutons'", "text": "The IAU has released its draft definition. A planet is a body that is massive enough for its own gravity to make it nearly round, and that orbits a star without being a star or a satellite of a planet. If members approve it on 24 August, there will be 12 planets: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Ceres, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, Pluto, Charon and 2003 UB313. Pluto stays a planet and becomes the model for a new class of 'plutons', planets that take more than 200 years to orbit the Sun. A dozen more candidates are on a watchlist. Committee chair Owen Gingerich says agreement came after a long day, 'the miracle had happened'.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://iauarchive.eso.org/news/pressreleases/detail/iau0601/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-017⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-16T07:05:00.000Z", "headline": "Draft footnotes make Charon a planet and set a size guide", "text": "The full text of draft Resolution 5 says round bodies are usually those above 5 x 10^20 kg and 800 km across, with an IAU process for borderline cases. Where two bodies orbit a point outside the larger one, as Pluto and Charon do, both are planets, so Pluto-Charon would be a double planet. If Pallas, Vesta or Hygiea prove to be round, they would also be planets. The term 'minor planet' would no longer be used.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.space.com/2742-iau-planet-defintion-draft-resolution.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-018⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-16T07:30:00.000Z", "headline": "Nature asks readers for a 12-planet mnemonic", "text": "Nature's news blog reports the IAU proposal under the headline that planets are round, and quotes the IAU's question-and-answer sheet: a pluton is a planet, Pluto is a pluton, so Pluto is a planet. It asks readers for a mnemonic for the new list of 12, since 'My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Nine Pizzas' only covers nine.", "place": "London, United Kingdom", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/planets-are-round/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-019⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-16T21:02:00.000Z", "headline": "Mixed reactions as 2,500 astronomers weigh the 12-planet plan", "text": "NPR reports that under the panel's rules Pluto would still be a planet, joined by Charon, the asteroid Ceres (a planet in the 1800s before it was demoted) and 2003 UB313, nicknamed Xena. Some astronomers say the rule could let in dozens of new planets. Nearly 2,500 astronomers from 75 countries are in Prague and will hold two discussion sessions before next week's vote, so the proposal may still change.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.npr.org/2006/08/16/5653191/astronomers-may-add-planets-to-solar-system"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-020⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-17T07:35:00.000Z", "headline": "Committee chair: Pluto might have got a special label in 1930", "text": "Reuters reports that Pluto would not lose its status under the IAU plan but would head a group of 'plutons' with Charon and Xena, while Ceres would be a 'dwarf planet'. Owen Gingerich, who led the committee, says that if astronomers in 1930 had known Pluto was smaller than our Moon, they might have given it a special designation.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://mg.co.za/article/2006-08-17-pluto-set-to-be-a-planet-and-pluton"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-021⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-17T14:41:00.000Z", "headline": "Astronomers split over the 12-planet proposal", "text": "The 12-member committee of the American Astronomical Society's Division for Planetary Sciences says it strongly supports the IAU resolution as clear and based on physical properties. But an informal Space.com poll of planet experts is 8 in favour and 7 against, and a private poll by the US National Academies is said to be strongly against. Harvard's David Charbonneau calls it 'a terrible definition'.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.space.com/2752-astronomers-sharply-divided-planet-definition.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-022⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-17T22:32:00.000Z", "headline": "Could the Moon one day count as a planet?", "text": "Gregory Laughlin of the University of California, Santa Cruz, points to an oddity in the draft. Because the Moon is slowly moving away from Earth, in billions of years the point the two orbit around would move outside Earth, and under the barycentre rule the Moon would then become a planet. Some objects could even switch between moon and planet during each orbit.", "place": "Santa Cruz, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/2759-earth-moon-planet.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-023⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-18T10:44:00.000Z", "headline": "New Yorkers laugh and shrug at 12 planets", "text": "An informal Space.com street poll in New York finds many people knew Pluto's status was in question but were surprised by the idea of three new planets. Most were glad Pluto would stay. Several laughed at the word 'pluton', and one student says a hundred-plus planets would be hard for children to learn.", "place": "New York, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/2756-public-laughs-shrugs-12-planet-proposal.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-024⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-18T14:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Mike Brown: the 'leave no iceball behind' definition will probably pass", "text": "On NPR's Science Friday, Mike Brown, who is not an IAU member and is not in Prague, says the round-body rule would eventually mean 100 or 200 planets; he counts about 43 known objects that qualify. He compares 'planet' to 'continent', a cultural word without a scientific definition, and would prefer either keeping nine or going to eight. He expects members to vote yes out of 'planet fatigue'. He admits it is exciting that one of the 12 is his discovery.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.npr.org/transcripts/5670908"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-025⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-18T14:57:00.000Z", "headline": "Rival plan would make Pluto a 'dwarf planet'", "text": "At a meeting of the IAU's planetary scientists, a straw vote found only about 18 in favour of the official proposal, about 20 for reworking it, and about 50 for an alternative from Julio Angel Fernandez of Uruguay. That plan requires a planet to be by far the largest body in its region, which would leave eight planets and make Pluto a 'dwarf planet'. Owen Gingerich says letters to him are evenly split and that a dwarf planet which is not a planet is 'linguistically objectionable'. The IAU Executive Committee is to decide the next step.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.space.com/2764-pluto-demoted.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-026⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-19T15:37:00.000Z", "headline": "Text of the alternative definition published", "text": "Space.com prints the rival proposal. A planet must be by far the largest object among the bodies that cross or come close to its orbit, round under its own gravity, and not powered by nuclear fusion. Only the eight planets known before 1900 would qualify. Ceres, Pluto and several large objects beyond Neptune would be 'dwarf' planets, and everything else 'small solar system bodies'. The Executive Committee must choose between the plans or modify the original.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.space.com/2770-details-emerge-plan-demote-pluto.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-027⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-20T22:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Columnist: Pluto demoted to the status of Mickey Mouse's dog", "text": "A Mail & Guardian column jokes about the 12-planet plan, noting that while the new 'plutons' keep planet rank, the IAU has in effect demoted Pluto and Charon 'to the status of Mickey Mouse's dog'. It quotes Brian Marsden saying that the 1930 discoverers' public relations were better than their astronomy, since they first thought Pluto was larger than Earth.", "place": "Johannesburg, South Africa", "source": "https://mg.co.za/article/2006-08-21-xena-rising-enter-the-age-of-the-pluton"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-028⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-21T17:57:00.000Z", "headline": "Conference paper: official plan 'defeated' in test vote", "text": "The meeting's daily newspaper, Nuncio Sidereo III, runs the headline that the planet proposal was defeated by an alternative in an internal test vote. At Friday's closed meeting of about 100 planetary astronomers, 50 backed the rival 'largest object' rule and 20 the IAU plan. Richard Binzel says the committee is listening and has spent the afternoon revising the text with the Executive Committee. A new version is to be handed out at a lunchtime debate on Tuesday 22 August.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-planet-defined-again/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-029⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-21T23:10:00.000Z", "headline": "Hundreds of emails from angry geologists", "text": "Members of the planet definition committee tell Nature they have received hundreds of emails from geologists over the term 'pluton', many from Australia. Some French speakers also object, because 'Pluton' is the French name for Pluto. A replacement word is expected in Tuesday's revised definition.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-la-planete-pluton/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-030⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-22T14:57:00.000Z", "headline": "Shouting at the open debate on what a planet is", "text": "At an hour-long open discussion, astronomers queued at the microphones to attack the revised IAU definition, now split into three parts to be voted on separately. Andrea Milani said it was an offence to the dynamical astronomy community, and someone shouted at IAU president Ron Ekers to let the people speak. The 'pluton' name has been replaced for now by a blank, with options such as plutonids, plutians and Tombaugh planets.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-fightfight/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-031⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-22T20:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Reversal: Pluto now likely to lose its title", "text": "NPR reports that after fierce protests at the Prague meeting, it now seems likely that Pluto will lose its status as a planet. Critics of last week's proposal argued that there are hundreds of Pluto-like objects beyond Neptune, and they will support a definition with a 'dwarf' category that is not a planet.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.npr.org/2006/08/22/5691890/plutos-planetary-status-teeters-again"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-032⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-22T20:58:00.000Z", "headline": "Version three: 'Pluto is a dwarf planet, but it is not a planet'", "text": "At a second session at 5.30 p.m. Prague time, Jocelyn Bell Burnell handed out a third version. A planet must now be both round and the dominant body in its region; round bodies that are not dominant become 'dwarf planets'. She spelled it out: Pluto is a dwarf planet but not a planet. A show of hands was more in favour than against, and Gonzalo Tancredi called it a very good compromise. Brian Boyle of Australia warned it may be too complex for the public.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-pluto-not-a-planet-after-all/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-033⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-23T16:48:00.000Z", "headline": "'They smell blood': 12-planet plan all but dead", "text": "The Associated Press reports that the plan to expand the solar system to 12 planets now looks doomed. Critics have won the demand that a planet be the dominant object in its area, which would leave eight 'classical planets' and make Pluto a 'dwarf planet'. Alan Boss says opponents smell blood. Committee member Junichi Watanabe calls it a compromise: eight planets plus the dwarf planets. The exact wording is still being worked on.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna14483570"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-034⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-23T18:06:00.000Z", "headline": "Uncertainty over exactly what will be voted on", "text": "At least three proposals have been discussed since the first draft, and a melded version was poorly received. IAU press officer Helen Sim says the text has been modified from the original and will be made public before the vote. The session begins at 2 p.m. Prague time on Thursday (12:00 UTC) and should end by 4 p.m. at the latest. Each vote is yes or no, so the meeting could end with no definition at all.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.space.com/2786-mystery-surrounds-thursday-vote-pluto-fate.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-035⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "headline": "Final text: eight planets and a separate class of dwarf planets", "text": "Today's conference newspaper prints what Nature counts as version four, with a warning that only minor corrections are now possible. Resolution 5A defines a planet as a round body orbiting the Sun that has cleared the neighbourhood around its orbit, and lists the eight from Mercury to Neptune. Round bodies that have not cleared their neighbourhood and are not satellites are 'dwarf planets'. 5B would call the eight 'classical planets'. 6A makes Pluto a dwarf planet and the prototype of a new class beyond Neptune, and 6B would name that class 'plutonian objects'. The vote is this afternoon.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-planet-status-update/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-036⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T09:00:00.000Z", "headline": "IAU: four votes this afternoon, by raised yellow cards", "text": "The IAU says members will vote on 5A, 5B, 6A and 6B in sequence at the session starting at 14:00 Prague time (12:00 UTC), with voting expected to end between 15:30 and 16:00 (13:30-14:00 UTC). Members present will raise yellow ballot cards, which will be counted. The term 'pluton' has been dropped after objections, especially from geologists. Ron Ekers says the IAU wants as many members as possible involved. A press conference is set for 18:00 (16:00 UTC).", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://iauarchive.eso.org/news/pressreleases/detail/iau0602/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-037⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T10:41:00.000Z", "headline": "Marsden storms the press room with 'planetino'", "text": "Brian Marsden, retiring head of the Minor Planet Center, led about ten astronomers into the press room holding a sheet of paper reading 'planetino'. He proposes that word in place of 'dwarf planet', to avoid confusion about how many planets there are, and says he has support from Uruguay, Brazil, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Norway, Serbia and the UK.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-invasion/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-038⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T13:14:00.000Z", "headline": "No new names at this stage, says Bell Burnell", "text": "At a final question session, Jocelyn Bell Burnell rejected 'planetino', saying no new names would be introduced now. Small fixes are planned, including quotation marks around 'dwarf planet' and a separate place for satellites. She told Nature it is very hard to predict how the vote will go. The closing session is starting, with other resolutions to vote on first.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-planet-vote-approaches/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-039⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T13:35:00.000Z", "headline": "Pluto demoted: 'Pluto is dead'", "text": "IAU members in Prague have voted for a definition under which Pluto is no longer a planet. Mike Brown, following the vote by teleconference, says there are finally, officially, eight planets. Only 424 astronomers were there on the last day to vote, out of about 10,000 professional astronomers. Alan Stern, head of the New Horizons mission, says he is embarrassed for astronomy, that the 'cleared the neighbourhood' rule fails for Earth and Jupiter, and that a petition against the decision is already circulating. Owen Gingerich, in Boston and unable to vote, calls the result confusing and unfortunate and hopes for electronic voting in future.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.space.com/2791-pluto-demoted-longer-planet-highly-controversial-definition.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-040⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T13:43:00.000Z", "headline": "5A carried, 5B rejected: eight planets only", "text": "Resolution 5A, defining planets, dwarf planets and small solar system bodies, was carried by a clear majority of raised yellow cards. To show the categories, Jocelyn Bell Burnell produced a blue balloon for the planets and a cereal box and a stuffed Disney Pluto for the dwarf planets. Resolution 5B, which would have made 'planet' an umbrella term covering dwarf planets, got 91 votes in favour and far more against. So there are eight planets.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-the-verdict-is/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-041⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T15:09:00.000Z", "headline": "How Pluto got its name, and a dog got his", "text": "NPR recalls that Pluto, found in 1930, was named after the Roman god of the underworld on the suggestion of Venetia Burney, an 11-year-old in Oxford, now Venetia Phair, aged 87. In 1930 Walt Disney also introduced a dog for Mickey Mouse, named Pluto a year later; Disney archivists think, but cannot prove, that the dog was named after the planet.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.npr.org/transcripts/5703014"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-042⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T15:48:00.000Z", "headline": "Pluto heads a new class, but the class has no name", "text": "Resolution 6A, making Pluto the prototype of a new category of objects beyond Neptune, passed by 237 votes to 157, with 17 abstentions. Resolution 6B, to call the category 'plutonian objects', failed by 183 votes to 186. Incoming IAU president Catherine Cesarsky warned that leaving the class unnamed would look a little stupid, but there was no revote. Ron Ekers noted the IAU can choose a name later without a General Assembly vote.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/iau-plutos-friends-left-nameless/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-043⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T16:00:00.000Z", "headline": "IAU: eight planets; Ceres, Pluto and 2003 UB313 are dwarf planets", "text": "The IAU confirms that the solar system now has eight planets, Mercury to Neptune, and that 'planets' and 'dwarf planets' are separate classes. The first dwarf planets are Ceres, Pluto and 2003 UB313, with about a dozen candidates on a watchlist. Resolution 5A passed by a large majority without a count; 5B had 91 in favour and many more against; 6A passed 237-157 with 17 abstentions; 6B failed 183-186. More than 2,500 astronomers attended the General Assembly.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://iauarchive.eso.org/news/pressreleases/detail/iau0603/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-044⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T20:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Inside the hall: 'We will be regarded as complete idiots'", "text": "NPR describes the vote: Jocelyn Bell Burnell told members their white resolution sheet was wrong, and Richard Binzel read out the final text. One astronomer asked whether Neptune counts, since Pluto crosses its orbit. Michael Rowan-Robinson warned that leaving with nothing would make astronomers look like complete idiots. Ron Ekers asked for yellow cards and declared the resolution clearly carried. Mike Brown, now discoverer of 'the largest dwarf planet', says this is the definition they should have had all along.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.npr.org/transcripts/5705254"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-045⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T20:41:00.000Z", "headline": "New Horizons now heads for a dwarf planet", "text": "Astronomers say NASA's New Horizons mission, launched in January and due at Pluto in 2015, is now more interesting, because Pluto is the standard for a new class. The IAU has not said how dwarf planets will be named. Mike Brown says his early name proposal for 2003 UB313 was rejected long ago and he is still working out the rules. Stephen Maran suggests selling naming rights to fund education.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/2797-planet-definition-leaves-scientific-loose-ends.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-046⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-24T20:50:00.000Z", "headline": "Disney's Pluto under an umbrella", "text": "The Associated Press reports that Jocelyn Bell Burnell, who ran the session, held up a stuffed Disney Pluto dog under a red umbrella to explain the rejected idea of 'planet' as an umbrella term. Teachers and publishers are expected to rush to change textbooks and classroom displays.", "place": "Prague, Czech Republic", "source": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/science/science-july-dec06-pluto_08-24"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-047⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-25T00:55:00.000Z", "headline": "Tombaugh's widow: 'It kind of sounds like I just lost my job'", "text": "Patricia Tombaugh, 93, widow of Pluto's discoverer, tells AP the decision is disappointing and confusing, but science moves on. A maker of classroom materials, Learning Resources, was preparing for 12 planets a week ago and now plans to show eight, with dwarf planets labelled, while keeping Pluto as a lesson in how science changes.", "place": "Las Cruces, United States", "source": "https://www.nbcnews.com/science/cosmic-log/lighter-side-pluto-flna6c10406318"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-048⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-25T03:05:00.000Z", "headline": "Children write to defend Pluto", "text": "CNN Student News reports on the vote. Neil deGrasse Tyson says he gets letters from outraged schoolchildren, and puts Pluto's popularity down to entertainment and sympathy for the underdog; the report notes that Disney's dog Pluto dates from the same year, 1930. The 'Nine Pizzas' mnemonic no longer works. Mike Brown says around 100 Pluto-like objects have been found.", "place": "New York, United States", "source": "http://www.cnn.com/2006/EDUCATION/08/24/transcript.fri/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-049⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-25T06:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Brown: disappointed for Xena, but backs the IAU", "text": "In a Caltech statement, Mike Brown says he is disappointed that 2003 UB313, informally Xena, will not be the tenth planet, but supports the IAU's 'difficult and courageous decision'. He says Pluto would never be called a planet if found today. Caltech notes that the Kuiper belt, predicted by Gerard Kuiper, was shown to exist in the 1990s, and recalls that Ceres was called a planet after 1801 before being reclassified.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2006/08/060825003742.htm"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-050⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-25T12:12:00.000Z", "headline": "Pluto merchandise becomes a collector's item", "text": "Space.com notes that nine-ball solar system kits, mobiles and charts will change, and an AP report suggests eight-planet toys may be in shops by Christmas. Older items, such as Clyde Tombaugh's autographs mentioning the '9th planet' and a 1991 US stamp showing Pluto as 'Not Yet Explored', may interest collectors.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/2800-pluto-solar-system-loss-collectors-gain.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-051⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-25T16:05:00.000Z", "headline": "Utah planetarium drapes a black sash over Pluto", "text": "The Clark Planetarium in Salt Lake City has put a black sash over its model of Pluto. Its director says the new definition makes sense. A local science teacher says she will keep calling Pluto a planet until NASA says otherwise. A NASA volunteer is asking the public for a new mnemonic.", "place": "Salt Lake City, United States", "source": "https://www.deseret.com/2006/8/25/19970492/pluto-no-longer-a-planet"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-052⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-26T06:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Newspaper writes an obituary for 'Planet Pluto, 1930-2006'", "text": "A Deseret Morning News editorial writes about Pluto's 'death' as a planet. It notes that school mnemonics such as 'My Very Easy Method: Just Set Up Nine Planets' no longer work, and that NASA's $700 million New Horizons probe is still on its way to Pluto.", "place": "Salt Lake City, United States", "source": "https://www.deseret.com/2006/8/26/19970540/planet-pluto-1930-2006/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-053⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-26T14:33:00.000Z", "headline": "Scientists circulate a petition against the definition", "text": "An email titled 'Petition Protesting the IAU Planet Definition' went out on Friday 25 August, signed by Mark Sykes, Alan Stern and ten other planetary scientists. It says fewer than 5 percent of astronomers voted, and that the 'cleared neighbourhood' rule gives absurd results, such as a Neptune-sized body far out not being a planet. It invites colleagues to sign a web petition, and asks that it not be made public.", "place": "Tucson, United States", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/after-the-iau-its-not-over-yet/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-054⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-26T14:51:00.000Z", "headline": "Petition passes 100 signatures in hours", "text": "Alan Stern tells Nature he is disgusted by how the IAU handled the matter and calls the definition patently absurd. The petition reached 100 signatures within a few hours of the first email. The IAU has nearly 9,000 members and a few more than 400 voted. Richard Binzel had said right after the vote that it was over and done.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://blogs.nature.com/blog/after-the-iau-planets-in-trouble/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨pluto-055⟩, "thread": thread:pluto, "published": d"2006-08-27T17:12:00.000Z", "headline": "Teachers see a lesson in Pluto's demotion", "text": "Teachers tell CNN the change is a chance to show that science changes. The Adler Planetarium in Chicago will update its exhibits; its building shows only eight planets on the outside because it was designed before Pluto was found. A Florida teacher's class sang its planet song after the news, and the National Science Teachers Association says murals will need redoing.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.cnn.com/2006/EDUCATION/08/25/pluto.reaction/index.html"};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 1], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 1, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "body": "The International Astronomical Union has opened its General Assembly in Prague today, and by all accounts they are finally going to vote on a formal definition of what a planet actually is. It has been an embarrassment for decades that astronomy lacks one, but the discovery of 2003 UB313 - nicknamed Xena - last year forced the issue, as Hubble confirmed it is slightly larger than Pluto. From a dynamical perspective, Pluto clearly does not dominate its orbit and belongs with the rest of the Kuiper belt, much as Ceres was eventually recognised as an asteroid. Still, getting thousands of astronomers to agree on any physical criterion will be quite another matter. What does everyone think should count?", "quote": NONE, "note": "I doubt the assembly will agree on a clean dynamical definition, given how sentimental people are about Pluto.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be considered a planet because it does not dominate its orbit dynamically, though reaching an official consensus on such a definition may be difficult.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": []};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 2], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 2, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-14T10:57:00.000Z", "body": "Strictly speaking, Ceres was counted among the planets for approximately half a century, not merely dismissed.", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic frames the technical parallel with Ceres correctly, though precision regarding the historical duration matters.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's status depends entirely on establishing a rigorous, precise scientific definition rather than relying on historical habit.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his technical framing and historical parallel with Ceres"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #1 by OffByOne in the thread \"Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?\" on the Night Owls forum, Thu Dec 27, 2001 9:40 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T06:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 78]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 87]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 152]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2005-07-30T01:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-006⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 3], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 3, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, hands off poor old Pluto! We all grew up with nine, it feels wrong kicking it out now. Sounds like that committee might save it anyway by adding a few more instead. If they do, I am going to have to invent a whole new rhyme for schoolkids! :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "I bet they end up keeping Pluto just because people like having nine planets too much to let it go.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should stay a planet, mostly out of fondness for the nine we all learned at school, though the whole debate is wonderfully silly.","score":4,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"friendly and up for a chat"}],"commitments":["working on a new mnemonic for the planets"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"1801-01-01T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-001⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"1999-02-03T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-004⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 121]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2000-02-01T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-005⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-06-08T03:19:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-010⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-10T21:10:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-011⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 4], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 4, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "body": "totally agree PintOfMild, it feels so weird that scientists can just meet up and vote on facts like this. i am training to teach science and it is going to be a nightmare if we have to change all the books and charts. hope pluto stays!", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 3], "text": "If they do, I am going to have to invent a whole new rhyme for schoolkids! :)"}, "note": "I really hope they don't demote Pluto because explaining a sudden change to schoolkids would be terrible.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should remain a planet, though it is confusing and worrying how scientists can just vote on what facts to teach in school.","score":3,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares the sentiment about keeping Pluto and worrying about teaching schoolkids"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"finds the technical perspective interesting but unsettling"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"1999-02-03T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-004⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 95]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 125]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-06-08T03:19:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-010⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-10T21:10:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-011⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 5], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 5, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "body": "It is always best to let the committee finish its work before we fret over the charts. Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes, and the world kept turning. Definitions change when new facts demand it. We shall see what the astronomers conclude.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild and the others are borrowing trouble before the astronomers have even published their proposal.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's status depends on how astronomers decide to formally define a planet, and it is best to calmly wait and see what they decide.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"finds his cheerful sentiment familiar"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion on theyknow is mistakes standard operational tuning for system failure"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is worked on altering branch ledger batch runs"}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"1999-02-03T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-004⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 105]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 6], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 6, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "body": "Spot on, Walter, though I still reckon the public outcry will stop them binning it! Sounds like we get the actual proposal on Wednesday, so not long to wait now. If it has atmosphere and moons, that sounds proper enough to count in my book.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I reckon the astronomers will bottle it on Wednesday and keep Pluto in to avoid upsetting the public.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should definitely stay a planet, as nine is what everyone grew up with and people like it far too much to let it go.","score":4,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by her agreement on the schoolbooks and rhymes"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"friendly and up for a chat"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"fond of his steady perspective and old stories"}],"commitments":["working on a new mnemonic for the planets if more get added"],"shift":"Heartened to see others also want Pluto saved and worry about changing school charts."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA's assessment is no interruption occurred in any critical operations"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-06-08T03:19:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-010⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 7], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 7, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-15T08:26:00.000Z", "body": "pluto has three moons and real seasons, so i reckon it deserves to stay, but tomorrow will tell!", "quote": NONE, "note": "I really hope the committee keeps Pluto so I don't have to rethink all the classroom posters.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should stay a planet, especially since it has moons and an atmosphere, but we will have to see what definition the committee proposes.","score":3,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"agrees with his practical points about Pluto having an atmosphere and moons"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"reassured by his calm, experienced perspective on changing definitions"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Feels somewhat calmed by Punchcard's reassurance and is waiting to see the draft proposal on Wednesday."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T16:54:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-013⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T20:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-014⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 8], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 8, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-15T14:33:00.000Z", "body": "Actually, having natural satellites cannot serve as a defining criterion. Numerous minor bodies possess moons, including Ida, which is merely an asteroid sixty kilometres across, yet nobody would classify it as a planet. \n\nSchool mnemonics and sentimental attachment are thoroughly irrelevant here. Science requires rigorous physical classification, not nostalgia. If an object satisfies whatever physical threshold the IAU establishes tomorrow - whether relating to mass, shape, or dynamical dominance - then it belongs in the category. If it does not, textbooks must simply be corrected. That is standard scientific progress, not a tragedy.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild continues to value schoolyard rhymes over rigorous taxonomy.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary status cannot be decided by sentiment or mnemonics, but must follow from whatever precise physical definition the IAU establishes.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his technical framing and historical parallel with Ceres"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"agrees with his sensible patience regarding formal definitions"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"dismissive of his sentimental nostalgia and reliance on rhymes"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Solidified his view that sentimental arguments from other members are irrelevant compared to formal scientific criteria."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-03T06:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 134]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T20:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-014⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 9], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 9, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-15T14:59:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne is quite right about satellites. Moons or an atmosphere tell us nothing about a body's broader gravitational environment. A physical definition cannot rest on sentiment or schoolroom rhymes. If an object does not dynamically dominate its zone, it simply belongs to a population of debris. We shall see tomorrow whether the committee had the nerve to say so.", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne remains the only other person in this thread approaching the problem with proper scientific detachment.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it fails to dynamically dominate its orbit, belonging instead with the Kuiper belt.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his rational focus on rigorous physical criteria over sentimentality"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"finds his reliance on nostalgia and mnemonics unscientific"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his measured, pragmatic patience"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-06-08T03:19:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-010⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T20:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-014⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 10], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 10, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "body": "Always follow the money, folks... An American discovered Pluto, and NASA just launched a billion-dollar rocket out there this January. Now a bunch of foreign academics meet behind closed doors in Prague to vote it out of existence? That's what they WANT you to think is just 'rigorous science'...", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 8], "text": "If an object satisfies whatever physical threshold the IAU establishes tomorrow - whether relating to mass, shape, or dynamical dominance - then it belongs in the category."}, "note": "OffByOne and quasistatic are ready to hand seventy years of American discovery over to a foreign committee without blinking.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is an established planet discovered by an American, and a committee of foreign academics shouldn't strip its status over academic politics and funding games.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"distrusts his blind deference to official institutional committees"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he is too eager to dismiss established facts on behalf of authorities"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"considers his complacent faith in committees naive"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-02T20:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 168]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T20:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-014⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 11], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 11, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "body": "Sure look, it is just a label on a database entry at the end of the day. A committee voting in Prague does not move an inch of rock out there. Whatever they call it tomorrow, the lump of ice keeps spinning regardless. We will see what the draft says.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is seeing conspiracies in routine cataloguing again.", "state": {"position":"Whether Pluto is called a planet comes down to arbitrary definitions, and the celestial bodies remain exactly the same either way.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is cooking up absurd conspiracy theories again"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his calm, pragmatic attitude toward committee definitions"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"views as level-headed and grounded in technical reality"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's opinion is dual circulation working as designed is not a crisis"}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"1999-02-03T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-004⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T21:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 12], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 12, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-15T19:42:00.000Z", "body": "It is not merely a database label, Kev. In the sciences, taxonomy establishes the theoretical framework by which we organize physical reality. Without precise classifications, comparative planetary science cannot proceed in any coherent manner.\n\nBinzel already indicated that the proposal offers a scientific and simple standard that resolves the question unambiguously. Whether that outcome results in eight planets or twelve - as Gingerich and Williams seemed to imply regarding additional bodies - the taxonomy will finally rest on clear physical properties rather than historical accident. We only have to wait until tomorrow to read the actual wording.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to invent nationalistic conspiracies where standard scientific taxonomy is all that is taking place.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary status strictly depends on whether it meets the formal physical definition to be released by the IAU, not on moons, nostalgia, or conspiracy theories.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his precise dynamical perspective and agreement on satellite criteria"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"disdain for his irrelevant conspiratorial thinking"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"impatient with her superficial criteria like moons and seasons"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Reaffirmed the necessity of rigorous physical criteria after dispelling arguments about natural satellites, while dismissing conspiratorial commentary."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T16:54:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-013⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 13], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 13, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "body": "Kev has the right idea. Back when we updated customer files on the 360, adding a new field never changed the man walking into the branch. A stone out past Neptune does not care what field it occupies on our forms.\n\nThere is no dark scheme afoot here, only a committee trying to set clean rules. Whether they settle on eight, nine, or a dozen, it will depend entirely on the physical yardstick they have agreed upon. The draft arrives tomorrow morning, and then we shall have the actual text to consider instead of guesses.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to see shadowy motives where there is only ordinary administrative drafting.", "state": {"position":"Whether Pluto remains a planet depends entirely on the criteria the committee sets, and we should calmly wait to see what they define.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is seeing conspiracies where there is only ordinary committee housekeeping"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his cheerful outlook"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees with his practical database comparison"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is worked on altering branch ledger batch runs"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion on theyknow is mistakes standard operational tuning for system failure"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-06-08T03:19:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-010⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 14], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 14, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-16T03:33:00.000Z", "body": "fair point about the asteroids having moons, that definitely blew my theory out of the water! i still really hope pluto makes the cut under whatever physical rule they announce today, but i guess we just have to wait and see what the draft actually says.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 8], "text": "Actually, having natural satellites cannot serve as a defining criterion. Numerous minor bodies possess moons, including Ida, which is merely an asteroid sixty kilometres across, yet nobody would classify it as a planet."}, "note": "OffByOne made a great point about Ida having moons, so now I just have to wait for the physical definition to see if Pluto survives.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's status depends on the committee's physical definition, but I still hope it stays a planet once the proposal comes out.","score":2,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"swayed by his point that having moons does not make something a planet"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"dismissive of his conspiracy talk"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"comforted by his sensible, level-headed view"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Realized that having moons isn't enough to make Pluto a planet after OffByOne's explanation, making me more undecided until the draft arrives."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"1999-02-03T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-004⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T08:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 7]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 15], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 15, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-16T03:39:00.000Z", "body": "Don't let them lull you to sleep, Walter... Whenever a bureaucracy tells you it is 'only setting clean rules', that is your cue to watch your back! Ask yourself why seven hand-picked insiders got to decide what everyone else teaches. We'll see what kind of deal got cut when the ink dries today...", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 13], "text": "There is no dark scheme afoot here, only a committee trying to set clean rules."}, "note": "Punchcard is far too trusting of official committees to see the politics behind this draft.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is an established planet and should stay one, despite foreign academics scheming behind closed doors to undermine an American discovery and NASA's mission.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is blindly parroting official committee propaganda"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"considers him completely naive for believing there is no agenda behind the committee"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"sees her as easily swayed by establishment talking points"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow's opinion is OffByOne and quasistatic are ready to hand seventy years of American discovery over to a foreign committee without blinking"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T12:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 174]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 16], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 16, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-16T05:04:00.000Z", "body": "It is Wednesday morning now, so the official draft should be made public quite soon. Fabricating geopolitical conspiracies regarding seven academics drafting a taxonomic definition is entirely absurd. Let us simply inspect the published criteria once they appear.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to invent absurd geopolitical conspiracies around routine academic taxonomy.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary status must depend strictly on the precise physical criteria set out in the impending IAU draft, not on superficial traits, database cynicism, or conspiracy theories.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates her acknowledging the factual error regarding moons"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"dismissive of his reductive attitude toward scientific taxonomy"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"agrees with his call to wait for the committee's objective criteria"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Emphasized the essential scientific importance of taxonomy against Sysop_Kev's cynicism, while noting kookaburra99 conceded on the moon criterion."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "officials: \"US officials reject claims that Y2K was hype\""}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"1998-02-04T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨y2k-001⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T13:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 3]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T00:22:00.000Z", "text": "Official spokesmen always tell you everything is under CONTROL right up until the switch flips, my friends. Nobody wants a bank run or folks clearing out the grocery aisles before the weekend, so o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 17], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 17, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-16T05:56:00.000Z", "body": "checking the news every five minutes this arvo, fingers crossed the criteria are kind to pluto.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is still seeing conspiracies in everything, so I am glad OffByOne called him out.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary status will depend on the physical definition in the committee's draft, though I am still hoping it makes the cut.","score":2,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"persuaded by his clear explanation about moons and physical taxonomy"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his absurd conspiracy theories"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible and grounded attitude"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Euro notes and coins arrive on 1 January - who's ready?'s status is functioning precisely as specified"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T16:54:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-013⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T08:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T03:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 14]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 18], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 18, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "body": "The draft text has just been reported this morning by Space.com, and it is rather extraordinary. The committee proposes that any object orbiting a star is a planet provided it has sufficient self-gravity to be round, and is not itself a satellite. \n\nUnder that rule, the immediate count rises to twelve. Ceres becomes a planet again, as does 2003 UB313, and remarkably, Charon is included because the barycentre of the Pluto-Charon system sits in open space between them rather than inside Pluto. \n\nThis completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already described the result as a complete mess, pointing out that dozens more bodies in the Kuiper belt will qualify as soon as their shapes are confirmed, whereas Alan Stern supports it. Alan Boss remarked that it reads as though it were drafted by lawyers rather than scientists, and I must say I see his point. It looks far more like a political compromise to keep Pluto than a sensible physical classification. We shall see how the wider assembly receives it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The committee chose a clumsy compromise to save Pluto, and I doubt the full assembly will stomach it.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is one of many Kuiper belt objects and should not count as a planet, as it fails to dynamically dominate its orbit.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly agrees with his emphasis on rigorous physical classification"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"dismisses his conspiracy theories as baseless"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady, level-headed perspective"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic's opinion critique is TheyKnow treats routine software glitches and normal consumer habits as evidence of systemic failure"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-015⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 19], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 19, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "body": "Strictly speaking, 2003 UB313 cannot yet be called a planet in any formal sense until the assembly actually ratifies this text next week.\n\nThat said, I find the published wording deeply disappointing. By relying entirely on hydrostatic equilibrium - essentially whether self-gravity has pulled a mass into a nearly round shape - the committee has constructed a purely geophysical threshold while ignoring orbital dynamics altogether. A genuine astronomical classification cannot simply disregard how a body interacts with its orbital environment. Alan Boss is quite right that this reads like an unscientific legal compromise, drafted specifically to preserve Pluto's historical status without explicitly stating so.\n\nIf roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonomic chaos. A definition ought to clarify reality, not obscure it with artificial legalisms, and I suspect the wider assembly will find this compromise rather hard to stomach.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I agree with quasistatic that the committee's hydrostatic definition is an unscientific compromise that avoids orbital dynamics.", "state": {"position":"The IAU draft proposal would retain Pluto and add Ceres, Charon, and 2003 UB313 under a purely hydrostatic criterion, but sidestepping orbital dynamics makes it look like an unscientific legal compromise.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"agrees with his critique that the hydrostatic definition sidesteps dynamics"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks his geopolitical conspiracy theories are absurd"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Has seen the IAU draft text, but is unimpressed that it relies purely on hydrostatic equilibrium and sidesteps orbital dynamics."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is Fabricating geopolitical conspiracies regarding seven academics drafting a taxonomic definition is entirely absurd"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is TheyKnow continues to invent absurd geopolitical conspiracies around routine academic taxonomy"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-015⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 20], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 20, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-16T08:19:00.000Z", "body": "Twelve of them! That is brilliant news, Pluto lives to fight another day! Though trying to make up a rhyme to fit Ceres, Charon and Xena in there is going to give me a proper headache over my tea break. My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Nine... what on earth starts with C, P, C, X? Pizzas, Cheese and Extra crisps maybe? Sounds like a good pub lunch to me! Anyone fancy a pint in the Victoria Commercial next Thursday to celebrate? Cheers! :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "I need to come up with a clever twelve-planet rhyme before the assembly votes next week.", "state": {"position":"Pluto looks set to stay a planet under the new draft proposal, and while twelve planets makes for a messy rhyme, saving Pluto is great news.","score":4,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"grateful for the speedy updates and news breakdown"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his endless conspiracy theories"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"mildly amused by his grumbling over the compromise"}],"commitments":["working on a new mnemonic for the planets if more get added"],"shift":"Delighted to see the draft definition actually keeps Pluto, even if expanding to twelve planets makes inventing a new rhyme tricky."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T05:25:42.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-012⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 21], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 21, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-16T08:44:00.000Z", "body": "Grand so, twelve it is for now. Adding three names to the list just shows it was pure admin all along. They drew a circle around round rocks to keep everyone happy, but the sky looks the exact same this morning. Let them argue over the paperwork next week.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They can fiddle with definitions all week, but nothing physically changed out there.", "state": {"position":"The IAU draft proposal to keep Pluto by expanding the count to twelve is just another round of administrative definitions that changes nothing about the physical rocks themselves.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible, no-nonsense IT perspective on arbitrary labels"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear and objective reporting on the committee's draft"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"tired of his compulsive need to find conspiracies in routine paperwork"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Has seen the IAU's 12-planet draft proposal, confirming his view that the debate is merely about bureaucratic labelling rather than physical reality."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 22], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 22, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "body": "Twelve planets sounds wild, but I am honestly so thrilled Pluto is still on the list! Having a round shape seems like a pretty straightforward physical rule to explain to people, even if making Charon a full twin planet is going to take some getting used to. PintOfMild, that lunch rhyme is gold! But OffByOne and quasistatic make a really good point about the orbits. If ignoring orbital dynamics means there could suddenly be dozens more trans-Neptunian objects joining the club, I wonder if the rest of the astronomers in Prague will actually vote for it next week. It definitely feels like things might get messy before Thursday.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I'm relieved Pluto survived the draft, but OffByOne's point about orbital dynamics makes me suspect the vote next week will be a massive fight.", "state": {"position":"The draft definition keeps Pluto as a planet by focusing on whether a body is round, though the astronomers now arguing over orbital dynamics might mean things aren't fully settled yet.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"intrigued by his critique of the draft as ignoring orbital dynamics"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates the clear and timely summary of the draft definition"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"amused by his cheerful pub rhyme for twelve planets"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"I feel relieved that the draft definition saves Pluto and brings the total to twelve, but OffByOne and quasistatic's points make me wonder if the assembly will actually accept it."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T08:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T08:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 8]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T03:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 14]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 23], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 23, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "body": "The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent. By treating hydrostatic equilibrium as the sole determinant, the committee has effectively discarded the astronomical context in favour of geology alone. \n\nConsider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea are already noted as potential candidates should further observations show they achieved equilibrium. In the outer solar system, the situation becomes unmanageable very quickly. There are already dozens of trans-Neptunian objects with diameters suggesting sufficient mass for roundness. If we must elevate every distant ice ball to full planetary status simply because its gravity smoothed its edges, the term ceases to convey meaningful structural information about our solar system.\n\nA sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many planetary astronomers in Prague will see this draft for what it is, an unworkable administrative fix designed purely to placate sentimental attachments.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The Prague assembly will almost certainly reject this draft resolution once the dynamical astronomers voice their objections.", "state": {"position":"The IAU draft relies solely on hydrostatic equilibrium while ignoring orbital dynamics, resulting in a flawed compromise that will lead to taxonomic chaos.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his sound dynamical analysis and critique of the draft"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased she grasped the mathematical problem with ignoring dynamics"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"dismissive of his conspiratorial nonsense"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 24], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 24, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "body": "Don't pop the corks just yet, folks... The committee knew the public and NASA would never swallow an outright demotion, so they drew up this twelve-planet list to take the heat off. But look at how fast the knives are coming out now! You already have folks calling it a 'mess' and grumbling about orbits and Asteroid Belts before the ink is even dry... That is the real PLAY here, mark my words. They put out a draft designed to look unworkable, just so the assembly has an excuse to tear it down next week and kick Pluto out the back door anyway. Keep your eyes open...", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are setting up this twelve-planet draft just to knock it down and strip Pluto next week.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is rightfully a planet and the committee was forced to keep it, but academics are now plotting to reject the draft and strip Pluto's status anyway.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees his sudden complaints about the rules as proof the establishment is desperate to demote Pluto"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"notes he is already laying groundwork to undermine Pluto's inclusion"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"considers him hopelessly naive about bureaucratic motives"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Interprets the draft saving Pluto as an inevitable concession to NASA and public pressure, but views the immediate pushback from astronomers as a second front to demote it."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-06T14:53:00.000Z", "text": "Dual circulation was explicitly established to prevent bottlenecks during this exact transition window. A merchant utilizing guilders to maintain liquid trade is executing the statutory fallback pr..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 8]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-015⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 25], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 25, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-16T13:48:00.000Z", "body": "sorry if this is a dumb question, but is everything really a conspiracy lol? i reckon seven professors probably just spent months trying to find a neat physical formula so everyone could agree, not hatching some secret plot to trick everyone!! \n\nthat said, OffByOne's point about the asteroids makes heaps of sense to me. if being round is the only test, having to call Vesta, Pallas and dozens of frozen rocks planets does seem super chaotic for science teachers to keep up with. it's awesome pluto survived the draft, but ignoring how they orbit really might make the whole assembly push back next week.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 24], "text": "They put out a draft designed to look unworkable, just so the assembly has an excuse to tear it down next week and kick Pluto out the back door anyway."}, "note": "TheyKnow is seeing conspiracies everywhere again, but OffByOne made me realize the draft's asteroid problem might actually kill it.", "state": {"position":"I am thrilled the draft keeps Pluto and uses roundness as a clear rule, but OffByOne makes a compelling case that ignoring orbits and adding dozens of bodies could make the assembly reject it.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"finds his explanation of why roundness alone creates taxonomic chaos very persuasive"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"skeptical of his conspiracy theories about the draft being a setup"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his excitement over Pluto surviving"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"OffByOne's detailed explanation of how many asteroids and ice balls would qualify under roundness alone made me realize how messy the draft really is."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T03:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 14]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T03:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 14]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-015⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:05:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-017⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 26], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 26, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-16T15:17:00.000Z", "body": "Young lady, you have put your finger on it. The committee has merely offered its first draft of the rules, relying on gravity and roundness to draw a clear line. There is no trickery in that, only an attempt to be fair. We should wait patiently to see how the wider assembly votes next week before deciding it is settled.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 25], "text": "i reckon seven professors probably just spent months trying to find a neat physical formula so everyone could agree, not hatching some secret plot to trick everyone!!"}, "note": "TheyKnow will never see an honest piece of drafting when he can imagine a conspiracy instead.", "state": {"position":"The committee's draft proposal keeps Pluto as a planet by focusing on roundness, but it is just the first draft of the rules and we should wait to see how the wider assembly votes on it next week.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still inventing grand conspiracies out of standard committee drafting"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees that this is just routine paperwork and administrative sorting"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"amused by his cheerful enthusiasm and lunch rhymes"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Noted the draft proposal setting the count at twelve, but remains steady in waiting for the final vote."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:05:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-017⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 27], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 27, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-16T15:58:00.000Z", "body": "totally agree, thanks Punchcard! sorry if i sounded impatient before, still getting used to posting here lol. it really is just down to whether the assembly wants a simple physical rule or if the orbit argument wins out. going to be so fascinating watching the debate unfold!", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 26], "text": "We should wait patiently to see how the wider assembly votes next week before deciding it is settled."}, "note": "Punchcard is always so reassuring when other posters start getting conspiratorial.", "state": {"position":"I am thrilled Pluto survived the draft definition, but OffByOne makes a great point that ignoring orbital dynamics and letting dozens of asteroids and Kuiper belt objects become planets will create total chaos and likely face pushback from the assembly.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"finds his explanation about asteroids and orbital dynamics very sensible and convincing"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused and dismissive of his endless conspiracy theories"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible backing of her view against conspiracy claims"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Punchcard agreed with my pushback against TheyKnow's conspiracy theories, while OffByOne's breakdown of candidate asteroids reinforced my concern that the draft is too messy to pass without a fight."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-14T16:54:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-013⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T03:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 14]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T03:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 14]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 28], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 28, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-16T16:43:00.000Z", "body": "Dead right, wait and see is the only sensible play. It is just committee members haggling over filing cabinets and dictionary entries, not some grand drama. Whether they settle on twelve, nine or fifty-three next Thursday, the telescope feeds will look precisely identical on Friday. Best let them finish the meeting first.", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 and Punchcard have the right of it, keeping a calm head while others look for plots in routine drafts.", "state": {"position":"Whether Pluto is called a planet or not remains purely a matter of administrative definitions and taxonomy that changes nothing about the rocks themselves in space.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his steady, sensible perspective and call for patience"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless conspiracy theories over basic committee drafts"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"glad to see her call out conspiratorial thinking with plain common sense"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Keep the thread focused on the actual incoming telemetry and news reports, lads.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 8]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 29], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 29, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "body": "No need to apologise at all. Kev is entirely correct, and it is a pleasure to see someone look at the facts with a level head rather than jumping at shadows. \n\nWhen seven people spend months attempting to solve an awkward puzzle, they rarely produce a perfect result on the first try. Gravity and shape offered them a tidy, measurable yardstick, and it is easy to understand why they seized upon it. It keeps Pluto safe for the moment, and it treats Ceres and that new body out past Neptune fairly by the same measure.\n\nNow the rest of the assembly gets their say. Some astronomers will naturally worry about having dozens of ice balls on the list later on, while others will want a clean, round shape to remain the standard. That is simply how an open vote works. People raise their objections, they weigh the merits, and they cast their ballots next Thursday. It is quite ordinary work, and there is no need to make a mystery of it. We shall know soon enough where the numbers fall.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 has a very sensible head on her shoulders.", "state": {"position":"The committee's draft proposal relies on roundness and keeps Pluto on the list, but it is just a first draft and we should wait patiently to see how the wider assembly votes next week.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased with her common sense in dismissing conspiracy talk"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his level-headed view that this is just routine paperwork"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless habit of seeing plots in ordinary committee work"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is worked on altering branch ledger batch runs"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T22:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T05:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 16]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 30], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 30, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "body": "It is easy to call it ordinary work, my friend, but having two thousand people show up to vote on a draft that suddenly adds asteroid belts and moons was bound to cause friction... When you put up a standard that lets in dozens of ice balls overnight, you naturally invite everyone to push back against the whole thing. If the room decides that roundness alone is too messy, the compromise will end up trimming the list right back down to eight. Folks can call it standard committee business, but that still leaves Pluto right on the chopping block when the debate starts tomorrow.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Walter and kookaburra are treating this like harmless committee work while the door is being pushed wide open to drop Pluto.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is rightfully a planet, but the twelve-planet draft was deliberately engineered to look ridiculous so the assembly can justify demoting Pluto next week.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she is blindly naive and falls for official excuses every time"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"scoffs at his condescending trust in institutional benevolence"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees his immediate attack on the draft as part of the setup to strip Pluto"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Has doubled down on the idea that the entire 12-planet proposal is a Trojan horse meant to provoke a backlash against Pluto."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"OffByOne is quite right about satellites.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Richard Binzel's assessment is scientific and simple"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 31], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 31, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "body": "Fair point that twelve could get a bit chaotic if every round ice ball joins in, but keeping Pluto on the list is still wonderful news. Even if the meeting gets messy next week with folk arguing over orbits and Asteroid Belts, I still reckon they will want to protect poor old Pluto in the end. Fingers crossed, anyway!", "quote": NONE, "note": "I reckon the astronomers will argue like mad over all those extra ice balls, but Pluto will scrape through next week.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should definitely stay a planet, and while the twelve-planet draft is a bit chaotic and may face pushback next week, keeping Pluto on the list is wonderful.","score":4,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"charmed by her appreciation of his pub lunch rhyme"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible, level-headed perspective on committee work"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"entertained by his dramatic paranoia about secret plots"}],"commitments":["working on a new mnemonic for the planets if more get added"],"shift":"Noticed the growing board debate about whether having dozens of extra ice balls will make the assembly reject the draft next week."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Dead right.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T08:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 20]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "text": "Now the rest of the assembly gets their say. Some astronomers will naturally worry about having dozens of ice balls on the list later on, while others will want a clean, round shape to remain the s..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 32], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 32, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-16T23:21:00.000Z", "body": "Crossing fingers too, but with two discussion sessions lined up before next Thursday, things could easily go either way. OffByOne made a fair point about the Kuiper belt swarms, and if enough astronomers feel roundness alone is just too chaotic, next week's vote could get properly messy.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 31], "text": "Even if the meeting gets messy next week with folk arguing over orbits and Asteroid Belts, I still reckon they will want to protect poor old Pluto in the end."}, "note": "I still reckon the floor debate next week is going to be a huge fight between roundness and orbital dynamics.", "state": {"position":"I am thrilled Pluto survived in the draft definition, but with the massive debate over whether roundness or orbital dynamics should define a planet, things could easily get messy when the assembly votes next week.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his reassuring encouragement and level-headed advice"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"finds his points about orbital dynamics and asteroid chaos very compelling"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"dismissive of his continued attempts to frame normal committee procedures as conspiracies"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Punchcard's reassurance made me feel more comfortable waiting to see how the assembly balances the simple physical rule against the orbital dynamics argument."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T03:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 14]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 33], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 33, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-16T23:53:00.000Z", "body": "Healthy friction is simply how large assemblies do their work. Back in '71, our systems group had forty analysts in a room debating a new transaction format, and every single one of them had a different objection to the record length. We did not need dark motives to explain the shouting, only people who cared about their craft.\n\nWith two thousand astronomers gathering for two open sessions, there will be plenty of spirited argument over whether roundness clutters the books too much. That is not a plot, it is merely procedure. We shall see how their discussion unfolds over the coming days.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to mistake standard committee arguments for calculated intrigue.", "state": {"position":"The committee's draft offers a tidy physical yardstick that keeps Pluto on the list for now, but with valid concerns about cluttering the catalogue with ice balls, we should wait and see how the assembly votes next week.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased with her level-headed approach and common sense"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees with his sensible wait-and-see attitude"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"patiently dismissive of his habit of seeing plots behind standard procedural friction"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "text": "Now the rest of the assembly gets their say. Some astronomers will naturally worry about having dozens of ice balls on the list later on, while others will want a clean, round shape to remain the s..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 34], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 34, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-17T12:50:00.000Z", "body": "Walter is quite right about the procedural nature of large gatherings. With the open sessions starting, the dynamical astronomers will certainly present their case against an exclusively geophysical definition. When two thousand specialists assemble to debate an unworkable compromise, substantive revision is the normal outcome, not the result of backroom intrigue.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The assembly will almost certainly amend this draft once the dynamical specialists formally challenge the roundness criterion.", "state": {"position":"The draft definition relies entirely on hydrostatic equilibrium and ignores orbital dynamics, an unworkable compromise that the assembly's dynamical astronomers will rightly reject.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased she recognizes the logical flaws of the draft"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"dismissive of his relentless conspiracy theories"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his procedural realism"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow's assumption is treats the deliberate dual-circulation buffer as an unexpected breakdown"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Astronomers: \"Nearly 2,500 astronomers from 75 countries are in Prague and will hold two discussion sessions before next week's vote, so the proposal may still change.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "text": "Now the rest of the assembly gets their say. Some astronomers will naturally worry about having dozens of ice balls on the list later on, while others will want a clean, round shape to remain the s..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 35], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 35, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-17T18:07:00.000Z", "body": "Quite right. It is entirely ordinary for an open floor to push back against a deeply flawed draft.", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne is entirely right that substantial revisions from the floor are the ordinary scientific process, not a conspiracy.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it fails to dynamically dominate its orbit and belongs to a swarm of belt objects.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly agrees with his procedural and dynamical analysis"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible, level-headed perspective on assembly procedure"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"dismisses his conspiracy framing of standard committee debate"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T19:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 127]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 9]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "text": "Now the rest of the assembly gets their say. Some astronomers will naturally worry about having dozens of ice balls on the list later on, while others will want a clean, round shape to remain the s..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 36], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 36, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "body": "Sounds like the hall in Prague is turning into a proper rowdy taproom! If they do tinker with the rules to stop the floodgates opening, I still bet they find a clever way to keep Pluto on the list. Would be a bit harsh to give it the cold shoulder now! Cheers! :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "I still reckon the astronomers will find a compromise that protects Pluto even if the debate gets messy.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should stay a planet, and while the debate over adding so many ice balls will be a lively mess, I still bet they find a way to protect it in the vote.","score":4,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his calm, steady view of ordinary committee wrangling"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"fond of her friendly enthusiasm and crossing fingers with her"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his dramatic insistence that Pluto is doomed"}],"commitments":["working on a new mnemonic for the planets if more get added"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-06T14:53:00.000Z", "text": "Dual circulation was explicitly established to prevent bottlenecks during this exact transition window. A merchant utilizing guilders to maintain liquid trade is executing the statutory fallback pr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 37], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 37, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "body": "A lively floor debate is usually the best disinfectant for any draft. With opinions running so evenly split among the specialists, plenty of fine-tuning will take place between now and Thursday. Let them have their arguments in the sessions and see what wording survives the floor.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to borrow trouble over ordinary floor revisions.", "state": {"position":"The spirited debates in Prague over roundness versus clutter are normal assembly procedure, so we should simply wait and see how the astronomers settle the draft next week.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"patiently reminds him that friction in large committees is normal work, not a shadowy plot"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"pleased that he understands the normal procedural nature of large technical assemblies"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates her level-headed recognition that debate could go either way"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is The arguments among the two thousand astronomers over roundness are not a plot but merely procedure"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 66]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 26]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 38], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 38, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-17T22:50:00.000Z", "body": "It is already running into severe mathematical and physical objections. Gregory Laughlin pointed out just today that relying on the external barycentre rule produces absurdities: as the Moon slowly drifts outwards, the Earth-Moon barycentre will eventually migrate into open space, turning our Moon into an independent planet under this draft. In eccentric orbits, a body could even alternate between being a moon and a planet.\n\nThat is precisely what happens when a committee ignores orbital dynamics in favour of static geometry. A definition that requires special carve-outs and produces orbital boundary anomalies cannot last. If the floor takes the physics seriously, Pluto belongs with the rest of the Kuiper belt population.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The barycentre rule in the draft is an absolute embarrassment that cannot survive a room full of dynamicists.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be considered a planet because it belongs to a belt of debris and does not dynamically dominate its orbit.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly agrees on dynamical physics and assembly procedure"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his steady, experienced grasp of committee debates"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his habit of framing ordinary debate as a conspiracy"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T19:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 127]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 9]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "text": "Now the rest of the assembly gets their say. Some astronomers will naturally worry about having dozens of ice balls on the list later on, while others will want a clean, round shape to remain the s..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T18:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 35]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 39], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 39, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-17T23:49:00.000Z", "body": "Look at how smoothly the pieces fall into place, folks... They write a rule so laughably fragile that our own Moon becomes a planet down the line, and right on cue the experts throw their hands up in horror. That barycentre gimmick was put in there to be shredded! When a committee hands you a draft stuffed with obvious tripwires, you have to ask yourself WHY. It gives them the perfect excuse to scrap the whole thing, throw out Pluto, and pretend the floor had NO choice but to drop back to eight. Watch the compromise they pull out next...", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are using the absurd Moon loophole to justify wiping Pluto off the map.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should remain a planet, but the absurd twelve-planet draft was clearly engineered to fail so the assembly has an excuse to cut the list down to eight.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"scoffs at his endless corporate analogies dismissing obvious orchestration as mere procedure"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"sees him eagerly jumping on technical absurdities to justify stripping Pluto"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"views his academic justifications as cover for the push to demote Pluto"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Feels fully vindicated as members pounce on draft flaws like the Moon barycentre issue, confirming his belief that the proposal was made to be dismantled."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-17T22:32:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-022⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 40], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 40, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-17T23:53:00.000Z", "body": "Honestly, the barycentre thing does make the whole proposal seem heaps more fragile than it looked two days ago. That example quasistatic brought up about the Moon drifting out until it turns into a planet - or things flipping status depending on where they are in an eccentric orbit - really shows how messy pure geometry gets when you ignore the actual physics. \n\nI really want Pluto to stay a planet, but if the draft definition leads to contradictions like that, you can see why the dynamicists are pushing back so hard. Next week's vote is looking genuinely precarious for Pluto.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Quasistatic's points about the barycentre absurdities made me realize just how fragile the draft definition is, making Pluto's status look much more uncertain.", "state": {"position":"I still hope Pluto stays a planet, but seeing how deeply flawed the draft definition is with things like the barycentre rule, the dynamicists make a really strong case that Pluto might not survive next week's vote.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"impressed by his clear explanation of the mathematical and physical flaws in the draft"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his steady, sensible perspective on how committee debates work"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"tired of his constant insistence that every scientific flaw is a deliberate conspiracy"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Quasistatic's points about the barycentre absurdities made me realize just how fragile the draft definition is, making Pluto's status look much more uncertain."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's expectation is astronomers will want to protect Pluto in the end despite arguments next week"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:05:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-017⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 27]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 41], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 41, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-18T01:11:00.000Z", "body": "It certainly looks like an honest muddle on the surface, my friend, but you have to look at who is steering the wheel... Seven top scholars spend months behind closed doors in Paris, and we are supposed to believe not one of them noticed what happens to the Moon or an eccentric orbit? A freshman physics student could spot that in an afternoon! They handed the hall a turkey with the fuse already burning, and now the dynamicists have all the cover they need to tear it up. When the alternative draft lands on the table next week, Pluto will be the casualty.", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 is buying the whole clumsy drafting excuse hook, line, and sinker.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is rightfully a planet, and the committee deliberately filled the proposal with absurdities like the Moon barycentre loophole to ensure the assembly would scrap it and demote Pluto.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks he is predictably falling for the orchestrated objections"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"sees him playing right into the script by citing the Moon barycentre flaw"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"dismisses his attempts to frame transparent sabotage as healthy procedure"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Has doubled down on his theory as fellow members take the bait of the Moon barycentre absurdity, viewing the pushback as scripted."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 42], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 42, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-18T03:04:00.000Z", "body": "They spent months trying to find any compromise that let Pluto stay while keeping things simple, and roundness seemed like the neatest fix at the time. It doesn't take a secret agenda to explain people overlooking weird edge cases. The dynamicists just have solid physical arguments against it, and the vote next week really could go either way.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will turn literally any ordinary committee mistake into an absurd conspiracy theory.", "state": {"position":"I really hope Pluto stays a planet, but the draft definition has obvious physical flaws and next week's vote could easily go against it.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"convinced by his clear breakdown of the physical flaws in the draft"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless conspiracy theories"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"reassured by his calm procedural perspective"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's assessment is embarrassing that astronomers lack an official definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's point about orbital dynamics\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 26]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 27]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 43], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 43, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "body": "The presence of edge cases in an initial draft is a standard hazard of mathematical modeling, not proof of clandestine plotting. Committees frequently focus on resolving a specific set of parameters - in this case, reconciling Pluto's status with newly discovered trans-Neptunian bodies - while failing to calculate the broader boundary conditions.\n\nThe examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satellite and planet depending on orbital eccentricity is mathematically untenable. It produces an absurd taxonomy rather than an analytical framework.\n\nWhen dynamical astronomers argue that a planet must gravitationally govern its zone, they are applying established celestial mechanics. Pluto sharing its resonance with an entire population of Kuiper belt objects is a physical fact. If the assembly amends the resolution to incorporate orbital dynamics instead of arbitrary barycentre thresholds, it will be the result of rational critique overcoming a poorly formulated text, exactly as scientific peer review is designed to work.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 41], "text": "Seven top scholars spend months behind closed doors in Paris, and we are supposed to believe not one of them noticed what happens to the Moon or an eccentric orbit?"}, "note": "The mathematical absurdity of the barycentre rule makes the adoption of a dynamical clearing criterion inevitable.", "state": {"position":"The draft definition's reliance on hydrostatic equilibrium and external barycentres leads to glaring mathematical contradictions, and an orbital dynamics criterion would appropriately classify Pluto with other Kuiper belt objects.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear demonstration of the draft's physical and mathematical flaws"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his persistent conspiracy theories"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible view of conference floor procedure"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Has grown much more confident that the draft resolution will be overturned and Pluto reclassified, reinforced by quasistatic's mathematical examples of barycentre anomalies."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 44], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 44, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-18T09:10:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, that Moon business really does put the cat among the pigeons! I still want our little mate to hang on in there, but hearing how full of holes this draft is makes it look a bit rocky for poor Pluto. Anyone fancy a pint in Leeds to drown our sorrows? Cheers! :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "Pluto's chances are looking far shakier now that everyone is pointing out the massive holes in the draft definition.", "state": {"position":"I still want Pluto to stay a planet and hope they find a way through, but the floor debate is getting so messy and full of scientific holes that its chances are looking a bit shaky.","score":3,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"shares her worry that the dynamicists' arguments make Pluto's position look precarious"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"still amused by his over-the-top conspiracy theories about the committee"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"impressed by his sharp points about the Moon and orbital physics"}],"commitments":["working on a new mnemonic for the planets if more get added"],"shift":"Growing less confident that Pluto will breeze through after seeing quasistatic and kookaburra highlight serious flaws in the draft."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 45], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 45, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne has hit the nail on the head regarding modeling errors. Committees routinely optimise for their chosen edge cases - here, trying desperately to preserve Pluto - while failing to stress-test their rules against the wider system. That is standard human oversight, not theatre.\n\nThe real divide in Prague is between people treating taxonomy as an exercise in static shapes and those who recognise that celestial mechanics dictates what an environment actually is. An object that is locked in resonance with Neptune and surrounded by thousands of icy peers cannot be separated dynamically from the Kuiper belt. I suspect the floor debate will reflect that reality.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 43], "text": "The presence of edge cases in an initial draft is a standard hazard of mathematical modeling, not proof of clandestine plotting."}, "note": "OffByOne consistently articulates the mathematical flaws of committee drafting better than almost anyone else here.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it does not dynamically dominate its orbital zone and belongs to a population of Kuiper belt objects.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his rigorous mathematical sense and clear breakdown of modeling flaws"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his persistent need to invent conspiracies out of ordinary committee oversights"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by their openness to physical and mathematical arguments"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Encouraged to see fellow board members recognizing the fatal physical flaws of the draft definition."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T19:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 127]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:05:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-017⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 46], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 46, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "body": "It is reassuring to see a sensible technical appraisal take hold here. Pointing out that a drafted boundary condition produces odd results - like the Earth and Moon parting company on the charts billions of years hence - is precisely why these papers are circulated to the full membership before a ballot is called.\n\nWhen a group sits down to craft a rule, they naturally concentrate on the problem right before their eyes. In this case, they were trying to accommodate Pluto, Ceres, and the newer discoveries without writing an endless catalogue of exceptions. That they overlooked an eccentric orbit or a drifting satellite is not evidence of a hidden scheme. It is merely the standard way drafting works. You propose a model, the specialists examine it for flaws, and then the text is refined to eliminate the nonsense.\n\nWhether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge from Prague before deciding the matter is lost.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to see deliberate sabotage where ordinary modeling errors are being calmly untangled.", "state": {"position":"Flaws like the barycentre issue are normal edge cases uncovered during committee debate, so we should wait calmly to see how the astronomers refine the wording before the vote.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is once again inventing sinister motives out of standard modeling oversights"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his rational explanation that edge cases in drafts are standard modeling hazards"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"values her sensible, level-headed perspective on committee compromise"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 26]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 47], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 47, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "body": "The crucial distinction remains between an intrinsic physical property, such as mass or roundness, and an interactive dynamical relationship. A body cannot be isolated from its orbital environment. If a revision introduces dynamical dominance, it merely corrects an incomplete set of variables. We shall see what text the Prague sessions produce.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Walter and quasistatic understand the mathematical necessity of revising this draft far better than the conspiracy theorists.", "state": {"position":"A rigorous astronomical classification must incorporate orbital dynamics and celestial mechanics rather than static geometry, meaning Pluto is properly classified alongside its fellow Kuiper belt objects.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his sound grasp of celestial mechanics and modeling errors"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"impatient with his refusal to see normal drafting flaws instead of conspiracies"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his rational perspective on standard drafting procedures"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Solidified his view that Pluto does not qualify as a planet under sound celestial mechanics, viewing the draft's mathematical absurdities as fatal."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T17:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 44]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-17T18:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 35]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 48], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 48, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-18T15:27:00.000Z", "body": "Sounds like that rival plan to demote it to a dwarf planet is already picking up proper steam.", "quote": NONE, "note": "With that straw vote going against the draft, Pluto looks in serious trouble unless a miracle compromise appears.", "state": {"position":"I still really want Pluto to stay a planet, but with all the dynamicists picking holes in the proposal, its chances are looking fairly shaky unless a good compromise turns up.","score":3,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"comforted by his calm reassurance to wait and see what amendments emerge"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"finds his dynamical arguments compelling even if bad news for Pluto"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"impressed by his sensible critique of the draft's mathematical flaws"}],"commitments":["working on a new mnemonic for the planets if more get added","fancying a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"A bit soothed by Punchcard's call for patience, but feeling the heavy scientific weight of the dynamicists' arguments against the current draft."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:05:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-017⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 49], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 49, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "body": "And there it is, right on schedule... The ink on the twelve-planet idea barely dried before this alternative popped up to clear out Pluto and leave just eight. A roomful of scientists gave it fifty votes to eighteen in a straw poll this morning, so the original draft really did just pave the way for demoting it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The eighty-to-eighteen split in Prague proves the original twelve-planet draft was just a setup to get rid of Pluto.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is rightfully a planet, and the sudden emergence of the rival plan to demote it to a dwarf planet proves the original draft was an intentional setup to clear the path for demotion.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees him dressing up orchestrated sabotage as standard peer review"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"views his talk of calm administrative refinement as hopelessly naive"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks he is entirely taken in by the official narrative"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Feels completely vindicated by the news of the rival eight-planet proposal, taking it as proof that the original draft was a deliberate foil designed to fail."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "news", "at": d"1999-02-03T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-004⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:56:00.000Z", "text": "The Boston hospital incident demonstrates precisely why audits are conducted; oversight worked as intended, and missing an administrative patch on a server is not an uncontrollable physical failure..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:05:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-017⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T21:02:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-019⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-18T14:57:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-025⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 50], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 50, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "body": "The straw poll reported from Prague this afternoon - roughly fifty votes for Julio Ángel Fernández's alternative against eighteen for the committee's text - reflects standard academic revision, not an orchestrated trap. When researchers assemble, they naturally evaluate a text against established physical principles, and the regional dominance requirement simply provides far cleaner mechanics than an arbitrary barycentre rule.\n\nRequiring a planet to be the dominant body in its zone finally aligns taxonomy with orbital reality. Labeling Pluto a 'dwarf planet' acknowledges its substantial size while correctly situating it within the Kuiper belt population. If the Executive Committee adopts that framework, the assembly will have chosen rigorous dynamics over sentiment.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never accept that a room full of scientists can simply change its mind when presented with better mechanics.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it belongs dynamically to the Kuiper belt and does not dominate its orbital zone.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his clear, rigorous grasp of dynamical mechanics and modeling"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his calm, sensible perspective on how committees draft and refine proposals"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his persistent tendency to see conspiracy in routine scientific debate"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Heartened that dynamical arguments and the rival proposal requiring regional dominance are gaining decisive momentum in Prague."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's expectation is astronomers will want to protect Pluto in the end despite arguments next week"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-18T14:57:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-025⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 51], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 51, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "body": "A straw poll among specialists is merely a snapshot of the room, not a signed decree. Back in '82, our working group took four non-binding votes in a single afternoon before finding wording everyone could accept. \n\nThe Executive Committee still has to weigh these amendments. It is far too early to count Pluto out.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is once again treating an ordinary show of hands as if the final gavel had already fallen.", "state": {"position":"Floor revisions and rival drafts are a normal part of scientific debate, so we should wait calmly to see what final text the assembly adopts before assuming Pluto is doomed.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is jumping at shadows and treating ordinary amendments as a prearranged plot"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his calm and rigorous view of peer review and floor debate"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his steady, technical analysis of mathematical modeling and orbital mechanics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion on theyknow is believes TheyKnow sees deliberate sabotage where ordinary modeling errors are being calmly untangled"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is believes overlooking an eccentric orbit or drifting satellite is standard drafting, not evidence of a hidden scheme"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 26]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 52], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 52, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-19T00:57:00.000Z", "body": "Love your optimism Walter, mate, but blimey, fifty to eighteen is a proper thumping! Hard to see our little pal staging a comeback after that. Sounds like they are determined to cut it down to size, even if calling it a dwarf planet that is not actually a planet makes no sense at all! If we do end up stuck with just eight, my new mnemonic is completely ruined before I even finish it. Still holding out a tiny bit of hope for a miracle next week, but it looks like last orders for poor old Pluto. Cheers! :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "Pluto looks almost certain to get demoted after that straw poll, but I hope Walter is right.", "state": {"position":"I still have a soft spot for Pluto and hope it sneaks through, but with that straw poll backing the 'dwarf planet' demotion, its chances are looking pretty slim.","score":2,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady optimism that Pluto might still pull through"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks his reporting on the straw poll makes demotion look very likely"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"notes his usual habit of seeing conspiracies in every vote"}],"commitments":["working on a new mnemonic for the planets if more get added","fancying a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"Felt Pluto's chances slip even further after hearing about the overwhelming straw poll vote in favor of demoting it to a dwarf planet."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "text": "Now the rest of the assembly gets their say. Some astronomers will naturally worry about having dozens of ice balls on the list later on, while others will want a clean, round shape to remain the s..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-17T12:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 34]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 53], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 53, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "body": "sorry if this is a dumb question, but how can a 'dwarf planet' not actually be a planet?? lol! that sounds totally upside down, like saying a baby kangaroo isn't a real kangaroo! but honestly, looking at those straw poll numbers from prague, the writing really does seem to be on the wall for pluto. \n\ni was really crossing my fingers for the draft before, but quasistatic and OffByOne make heaps of sense about orbital dynamics. pluto just isn't the boss of its own neighbourhood out there in the kuiper belt, is it? if it shares its space with thousands of other icy rocks and is locked into an orbit dictated by neptune, calling it the exact same thing as jupiter or earth doesn't really add up physically. \n\nit makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial mechanics is going to make way more sense to explain to a class than a messy list of dozens of random iceballs. looks like eight planets might actually be the proper scientific answer after all!", "quote": NONE, "note": "I reckon Pluto's chances are totally gone after that lopsided straw poll, and honestly dynamical dominance makes way more sense anyway.", "state": {"position":"Pluto looks increasingly unlikely to remain a planet, as dynamical dominance makes much more scientific sense than the flawed draft definition.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"strongly persuaded by his explanation of celestial mechanics and the Kuiper belt"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"impressed by his clear breakdown of dynamical relationships versus static geometry"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"sympathises with his sadness over Pluto's likely demotion"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"I'm leaning toward Pluto not counting as a full planet after seeing the compelling dynamical arguments and the lopsided straw poll against the draft."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is much more uncertain"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-015⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-17T23:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 40]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-18T14:57:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-025⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 54], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 54, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-19T07:50:00.000Z", "body": "Too true, it feels like saying a toy dog is not an actual dog, totally crackers!", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 is spot on about the wording making no sense, but she has definitely given up on Pluto.", "state": {"position":"Pluto looks like a goner after that crushing straw poll, even though calling it a dwarf planet while saying it's not a real planet sounds completely daft to me.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"admires his fighting spirit but reckons he's clinging to false hope"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"sympathises with her confusion over dwarf planets not being real planets"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his technical grasp of why the astronomers are voting it down"}],"commitments":["working out a mnemonic once the final planet count is decided","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"Has essentially accepted Pluto is doomed after seeing the 50-18 margin and seeing other members like kookaburra99 concede to the eight-planet view."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 55], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 55, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "body": "Language often contains these apparent contradictions when technical classifications diverge from common speech. Owen Gingerich himself noted that a dwarf planet which is not a planet is linguistically awkward, but science frequently employs modified terms to signify an entirely separate category. Consider the term 'minor planet' - which the original draft sought to abolish - or even 'koala bear', which is a marsupial rather than a member of Ursidae.\n\nThe essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A body that cannot clear its orbital zone, or that is locked into an orbital resonance alongside thousands of other Kuiper belt objects, occupies a completely different physical regime than an isolated, dominant planet. Relegating Pluto to a dwarf class is not an insult or an act of malice; it is an acknowledgement of observational evidence. If the Executive Committee adopts the proposal put forward by Julio Angel Fernandez, astronomy will finally possess an objective, dynamical standard rather than a sentimental compromise.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard is clinging to procedural optimism, but the overwhelming straw poll shows the dynamical criteria will inevitably prevail.", "state": {"position":"A scientifically sound planetary definition must incorporate dynamic orbital dominance rather than mere static shape, meaning Pluto is properly classified among Kuiper belt objects rather than as a primary planet.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his rigorous appraisal of dynamical dominance and straw poll data"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"impatient with his relentless conspiracy framing of normal drafting revisions"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his pragmatic institutional experience even if he remains overly optimistic about Pluto"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Felt vindicated by the Prague straw poll showing dynamical dominance gaining strong traction over the flawed hydrostatic draft."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-29T15:08:00.000Z", "text": "Similarly, when supervisory software encounters an unrecognised date format, it produces an erroneous report or an administrative error dialogue. It does not corrupt the direct mechanical governor ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:34:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 149]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 56], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 56, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-19T11:34:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne is quite right about the taxonomy, and kookaburra99 has grasped the physical distinction admirably. \n\nTo keep our eye on the procedure, two matters now determine the outcome: first, whether the Executive Committee redrafts Resolution 5 to incorporate Fernández's zone-clearing criterion, and second, whether the full assembly actually votes on it on Thursday.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard still underestimates how thoroughly the planetary division's straw poll wrecked the committee's original text.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it belongs dynamically to the Kuiper belt and does not dominate its orbital zone.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his consistent, rigorous dynamical perspective and clear taxonomical distinctions"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his institutional experience with committee drafting, though thinks he underestimates the straw poll's impact"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased that she is grasping the dynamical reasoning over sentimental attachment"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "planet's definition is a cultural word without a scientific definition, comparable to continent"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 45]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 57], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 57, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-19T17:34:00.000Z", "body": "Strictly speaking, the text of Fernández's alternative published this afternoon does not state that a body must clear its zone, but rather that it must be by far the largest object among bodies crossing or approaching its orbit. That distinction matters mathematically. It provides a concrete threshold based on gravitational dominance over its neighbouring population.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard still clings to committee procedure, but the published wording finally gives dynamics the numerical precision it requires.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is properly classified among Kuiper belt objects rather than as a planet, as astronomical classification must be governed by dynamical orbital dominance.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear alignment with dynamical mechanics and sound taxonomic logic"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased she grasps the dynamical distinction over emotional attachment"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks his procedural optimism blinds him to clear dynamical reality"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Reaffirmed the linguistic and scientific validity of 'dwarf planet' as a distinct category and welcomed kookaburra99 accepting dynamical mechanics."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is relegating Pluto to a dwarf class is an acknowledgement of observational evidence rather than malice"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 58], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 58, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-19T18:47:00.000Z", "body": "Call it a dwarf or whatever they like, the rock itself will not care either way.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 55], "text": "Relegating Pluto to a dwarf class is not an insult or an act of malice; it is an acknowledgement of observational evidence."}, "note": "They can invent as many categories as they want in Prague, but it is still just paperwork.", "state": {"position":"Whether Pluto is called a planet or a dwarf planet remains purely a matter of administrative cataloguing that has no effect on the physical solar system.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his steady procedural perspective and call to wait for the actual final vote"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of him reading coordinated conspiracies into normal committee debates"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his level-headed focus on the actual committee timeline and mechanics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-19T15:37:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-026⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 59], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 59, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-19T21:57:00.000Z", "body": "Fair enough, the rock won't care, but the rest of us definitely will! It really looks like the eight-planet crowd has got all the momentum now that the text is out. If they do bin poor Pluto on Thursday, I will just have to start drafting a brand new eight-word rhyme instead.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 58], "text": "Call it a dwarf or whatever they like, the rock itself will not care either way."}, "note": "It looks like eight planets is pretty much settled given the momentum behind this new text.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is almost certainly out given the straw poll and the scientists pushing for eight, even if calling a dwarf planet not a planet remains completely crackers.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"shares a laugh with her over how upside-down the wording is"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"fond of his fighting spirit"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"finds his pedantic explanations a bit much but gets his point"}],"commitments":["working out a mnemonic once the final planet count is decided","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"Chimed in to agree with kookaburra99 that calling a dwarf planet not a planet is totally crackers."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "planet's definition is a cultural word without a scientific definition, comparable to continent"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Dead right.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 60], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 60, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-20T02:07:00.000Z", "body": "Notice how easily that alternative draft draws a line right at 1900... It sets up a rule tailor-made to keep the old club of eight intact and push the one American discovery into second class. Dressing it up in fancy orbital language does not change what it does to Pluto, my friends.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are swallowing this alternative definition hook, line, and sinker just because it uses fancy math words.", "state": {"position":"Pluto remains a genuine planet, and the academic establishment is executing their planned takedown right in the open while gullible observers rationalize it as natural science.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees him providing pedantic scientific cover for an orchestrated demotion"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she folds instantly at the first sign of official authority"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"views him as acting as an apologist for institutional maneuvers"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"His conviction that Pluto is being deliberately undermined is further reinforced as other members readily fall in line behind the eight-planet scheme."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-015⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-18T14:57:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-025⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 61], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 61, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-20T03:38:00.000Z", "body": "sorry TheyKnow, but gravitational dominance isn't anti-American, it's just basic physics! lol", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 60], "text": "It sets up a rule tailor-made to keep the old club of eight intact and push the one American discovery into second class."}, "note": "TheyKnow will turn literally anything into a conspiracy instead of just admitting the science makes sense.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be considered a major planet, as clear scientific rules based on orbital dynamics and celestial mechanics make much more sense than sentimental attachment.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"deeply appreciative of his clear explanation of terminology and gravitational dominance"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"pleased by his encouragement and values his guidance on the physics and procedure"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"fondly amused by his rhymes and sympathises with his disappointment"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"I have become fully convinced by OffByOne and quasistatic that eight planets based on dynamical dominance is the proper scientific standard to teach."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is much more uncertain"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 62], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 62, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-20T10:52:00.000Z", "body": "Now that the alternative text is actually in front of everyone, we can see what the real debate is about. It comes down to whether gravitational dominance over an orbit belongs in the definition alongside physical shape. That is an honest scientific disagreement, not a political exercise.\n\nThe Executive Committee still has to decide what goes forward to the general session. A division room or a published rival paper is merely part of the committee process. Plenty of discussion remains before Thursday afternoon, and the text may well see further adjustments yet. It is far better to wait patiently for the full assembly to cast their ballots before deciding how the ledger settles.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to see political hostility where astronomers are merely wrestling with orbital dynamics.", "state": {"position":"We should wait calmly for the full assembly to vote on the revised draft text on Thursday rather than rushing to conclusions beforehand.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still inventing anti-American conspiracies out of ordinary orbital physics"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his precise, technical attention to mathematical definitions and draft wording"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his methodical focus on procedural steps and committee voting"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 26]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 63], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 63, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-20T16:13:00.000Z", "body": "Basic physics does not care about committee votes in Prague, but committee votes sure care about who gets the funding... Ask yourself why NASA fired a seven-hundred-million-dollar rocket straight toward Pluto in January if the establishment really considered it just a stray pebble in the backwoods.\n\nNow an alternative magically appears that knocks Pluto down to 'dwarf' status while making sure the old European roster stays on top. When someone draws a mathematical circle that just HAPPENS to cut out the single target our own space program is flying to right now, that is not an innocent coincidence, folks. They are managing the narrative, plain and simple.", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 and Punchcard are totally blind to how convenient this eight-planet rule is for defunding Pluto right after New Horizons launched.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is a true planet being targeted by an engineered setup, where fancy orbital definitions are used as an excuse to exclude the only American-discovered planet.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she naively accepts institutional talking points disguised as basic physics"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"sees his talk of honest debate and patient waiting as whitewashing an inside job"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"views him as hiding behind technical jargon to justify an orchestrated demotion"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-28T12:15:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T20:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 45]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "text": "Now the rest of the assembly gets their say. Some astronomers will naturally worry about having dozens of ice balls on the list later on, while others will want a clean, round shape to remain the s..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-17T12:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 34]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 64], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 64, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-20T22:24:00.000Z", "body": "Sure look, NASA launched a probe at a cold lump of ice, not a dictionary entry.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 63], "text": "Ask yourself why NASA fired a seven-hundred-million-dollar rocket straight toward Pluto in January if the establishment really considered it just a stray pebble in the backwoods."}, "note": "TheyKnow will turn any standard scientific agenda into a deep-state spending plot.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary status is merely a labeling question for committees to haggle over, and nothing about the physical universe changes no matter what words they choose.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his patient, sensible reminder to wait for the actual vote"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exhausted by his relentless conspiracy theories about ordinary committee revisions"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"finds his detailed linguistic and dynamical distinctions overly fussy"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T15:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T16:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 28]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T01:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 41]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "text": "it makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 65], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 65, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "body": "Kevin is entirely correct. Missions are funded to investigate geological and physical phenomena, not to validate an entry in an encyclopedia. Exploration does not depend on a bureaucratic honour. We launched spacecraft to explore comets and asteroids long before anyone considered defining the word planet.\n\nPluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodies and remains locked in a 3:2 mean-motion resonance with Neptune. Claiming that an orbital dominance criterion was engineered merely to target an American probe ignores decades of observational work since 1992.\n\nPunchcard is sensible to remind us that the Executive Committee must still deliberate on how to merge these texts before Thursday. But distinguishing between bodies that gravitationally control their orbital territory and those that simply drift within a vast swarm is ordinary, sound mechanics. Applying proper physics to taxonomy is not an ideological manoeuvre.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 63], "text": "Ask yourself why NASA fired a seven-hundred-million-dollar rocket straight toward Pluto in January if the establishment really considered it just a stray pebble in the backwoods."}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to invent grand national conspiracies out of routine celestial mechanics.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it belongs dynamically to the Kuiper belt and does not dominate its orbital zone.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his precise mathematical clarification of the alternative draft"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his habit of inventing conspiratorial motives where ordinary science applies"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his measured perspective on the procedural steps ahead"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "planet's definition is a cultural word without a scientific definition, comparable to continent"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2005-10-31T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-007⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-01-19T19:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-008⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 66], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 66, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-21T09:38:00.000Z", "body": "Spot on from Kev and quasistatic there. NASA wanted to see what was out on the edge of the solar system, not just tick a box on a school poster! Nobody is plotting against the probe, it is just a bunch of scientists arguing over how to tidy up the textbooks. It does feel sad seeing Pluto get squeezed out after all these years, but if it really is just swimming in a sea of other ice balls, you can see why the astronomers want to draw the line at eight. Thursday is going to be brutal for the poor thing.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is seeing conspiracies again over a simple science vote, but it really looks like Pluto is done for on Thursday.", "state": {"position":"It looks like Pluto is on the way out given the momentum behind the eight-planet definition, though it's sad to see it demoted.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is seeing conspiracies where there are none as usual"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"enjoys his down-to-earth, sensible view"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his thorough, level-headed explanation"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme if Pluto gets binned on Thursday","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"Has accepted that the eight-planet side has all the momentum and is preparing to write an eight-word mnemonic."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's assessment is thought to be large"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "text": "Pluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodi..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 67], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 67, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "body": "Well, missions need congressional purse strings, and Congress approved a grand tour of the nine planets, not an expensive trip to an asteroid belt... Now the conference paper says the original twelve-planet plan was completely defeated behind closed doors. They are rushing out another rewrite for lunch tomorrow, so the squeeze on Pluto is definitely on.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 65], "text": "Missions are funded to investigate geological and physical phenomena, not to validate an entry in an encyclopedia."}, "note": "They are rewriting the text behind closed doors just to lock Pluto out by Thursday.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is a real planet being stripped of its status through an engineered bureaucratic manoeuvre designed to exclude the single American discovery and control institutional funding.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he is hiding behind dry orbital mechanics to whitewash an institutional narrative"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"sees his plea for patient procedural trust as naively covering for an orchestrated fix"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she blindly parrots establishment talking points as basic physics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:56:00.000Z", "text": "The Boston hospital incident demonstrates precisely why audits are conducted; oversight worked as intended, and missing an administrative patch on a server is not an uncontrollable physical failure..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 68], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 68, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-22T12:24:00.000Z", "body": "Headlines in a conference paper are meant to sell copies, not settle the matter. Hearing that a working session pushed back against the initial wording simply tells us the committee is doing its job. When people raise sensible concerns, whether over orbital mechanics or awkward terminology that upsets the geologists, the proper course is to redraft the text.\n\nThe revised draft is only just being handed out in Prague today, and there will be plenty of discussion at lunch before anything reaches the floor. Jumping to conclusions before the full membership actually reviews the new wording on Thursday is putting the cart before the horse.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat routine editorial revisions as proof of an ambush against Pluto.", "state": {"position":"We must wait calmly for the full assembly to vote on the revised drafts on Thursday before drawing any conclusions about Pluto's status.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still seeing conspiracies and political plots in standard committee debate"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his sound explanation of orbital mechanics and support for waiting on the committee process"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees with his level-headed, practical outlook"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is It is far better to wait patiently for the full assembly to cast their ballots before deciding how the ledger settles"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 26]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "text": "Pluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodi..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-21T17:57:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-028⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 69], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 69, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-22T14:15:00.000Z", "body": "Walter is quite right. A committee revising its text in response to professional scrutiny is ordinary academic work, not an underhand manoeuvre.\n\nBesides, terminology matters. When geologists pointed out that 'pluton' already has a well established meaning in igneous rock formations, the drafting committee had to listen. Adopting conflicting jargon across scientific disciplines helps no one. \n\nIf the lunchtime version properly incorporates gravitational dominance, it will simply reflect the physical reality of the solar system. We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 68], "text": "When people raise sensible concerns, whether over orbital mechanics or awkward terminology that upsets the geologists, the proper course is to redraft the text."}, "note": "Punchcard remains the only person keeping a level head about standard committee drafting procedures.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is not a planet under sound celestial mechanics because it fails to gravitationally dominate its orbital zone among the Kuiper belt population.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his persistent conspiracy theories regarding NASA funding and anti-American plots"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his steady procedural realism about the ongoing committee revisions"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees with his pragmatic take that exploration is driven by physics rather than definitions"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's assessment is embarrassing that astronomers lack an official definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 9]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-17T12:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 34]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T18:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 35]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "text": "Pluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodi..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 70], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 70, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-22T19:24:00.000Z", "body": "They were shouting at each other today in Prague, so keep it civil here please. -- Kev (mod)", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 69], "text": "Walter is quite right. A committee revising its text in response to professional scrutiny is ordinary academic work, not an underhand manoeuvre."}, "note": "They are having shouting matches in Prague over a dictionary entry.", "state": {"position":"Whether Pluto is labeled a planet or not is merely a matter of scientific committee wording, while the actual lump of rock in space remains completely unchanged.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his constant politicized conspiracy theories"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his level-headed advice to let the committee process finish"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"pleased he shares the practical view that missions explore physics rather than labels"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "geologists: \"When geologists pointed out that 'pluton' already has a well established meaning in igneous rock formations, the drafting committee had to listen.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T16:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 28]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "text": "Pluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodi..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-21T23:10:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-029⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 71], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 71, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "body": "omg shouting matches?? scientists get so heated lol! \n\nbut look at the news from the arvo session - they handed out version three and it finally makes it official: a real planet has to be round AND the dominant body in its zone! Jocelyn Bell Burnell basically said straight up that pluto is a dwarf planet and NOT a planet. \n\nsorry if this is a dumb question, but doesn't that pretty much wrap up the argument? it makes complete sense physically - pluto simply doesn't control its orbit out there in the kuiper belt. having eight true planets based on sound gravitational mechanics is going to be heaps easier to teach than a dodgy list of hundreds of iceballs anyway!!", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is definitely going to throw another tantrum now that the Prague meeting has basically sealed Pluto's fate.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is one of many Kuiper belt objects and cannot be considered a true planet under sound rules of gravitational orbital dominance.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his rational, well-reasoned defense of scientific taxonomy against conspiracy theories"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his baseless conspiracy theories and stubborn drama"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his patient, level-headed perspective on committee procedures"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2001-12-30T06:58:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action over wage negotiations in France is equally unremarkable at the end of a fiscal year. Bank staff unions naturally use significant public deadlines to strengthen their bargaining p..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-06T14:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 163]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 32]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 72], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 72, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-23T03:21:00.000Z", "body": "It wraps things up because they forced the wording to fit, my friend... You have folks shouting at the microphones, three hurried drafts in four days, and now an outright declaration that a dwarf planet is not even a planet. That is not settled science, it is just railroading an outcome.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 71], "text": "doesn't that pretty much wrap up the argument?"}, "note": "kookaburra99 thinks a show of hands after three chaotic redrafts in four days somehow equals genuine scientific consensus.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is a genuine planet and the chaotic rewriting and shouting in Prague only proves that the academic establishment is railroading a bureaucratic fix to strip its status.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he makes convenient excuses for backroom political maneuvering"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she uncritically swallows whatever official announcement is handed down"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"sees his endless procedural patience as naive blindness to an obvious stitch-up"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He feels further vindicated that the sudden redrafts and open shouting matches in Prague reveal an orchestrated effort to push Pluto out."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "text": "it makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-22T14:57:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-030⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 73], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 73, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-23T04:14:00.000Z", "body": "It isn't railroading at all though. When hundreds of scientists push back because the first draft completely ignored celestial mechanics, rewriting it to reflect actual orbital physics is just good scientific debate. A real planet controls its space, and Pluto simply doesn't. That isn't a stitch-up, it is just sorting things out properly.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never accept that Pluto isn't a planet no matter how solid the physics is.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is not a true planet because it fails the clear physical test of gravitational dominance over its orbital zone.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his persistent dramatic claims that the IAU is railroading the vote"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his rational, physics-based explanation of planetary taxonomy"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his steady, sensible perspective on committee drafting"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Felt completely vindicated by the IAU's third draft explicitly classifying Pluto as a dwarf planet rather than a true planet."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-22T20:58:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-032⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 74], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 74, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-23T05:49:00.000Z", "body": "Strictly speaking, the third draft does not represent a rushed ambush, but rather a necessary correction to an incomplete mathematical model. When the initial proposal considered only hydrostatic equilibrium, it effectively ignored the dynamical reality of orbital mechanics. \n\nA celestial body cannot be evaluated in total isolation from the environment through which it moves. The eight dominant bodies have cleared or gravitationally controlled their zones, whereas Pluto shares its region with an entire belt of debris. Establishing a separate dwarf category based on gravitational dominance is not an arbitrary manoeuvre; it is precise classification. The upcoming vote tomorrow merely codifies standard physics.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to mistake ordinary academic peer review and empirical correction for an institutional conspiracy.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is not a planet because astronomical classification must adhere to dynamical mechanics and orbital dominance rather than mere hydrostatic equilibrium.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his rigorous insistence on orbital mechanics and clear terminology"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased she robustly defends gravitational physics against conspiracy nonsense"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"irritated by his persistent tendency to confuse routine academic revision with political conspiracies"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 75], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 75, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-23T07:21:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne sets out the physical logic admirably. To steer us back to the procedure on the floor, the timeline is quite ordinary for an academic assembly.\n\nTwo specific points are worth keeping in mind before tomorrow's plenary session:\n\n1. The redrafting process. Far from being rushed through by decree, the wording evolved because the membership demanded sound mechanics. When Jocelyn Bell Burnell presented version three yesterday afternoon, she was presenting the result of intense open debate, not a backroom fait accompli. Requiring a planet to be both in hydrostatic equilibrium and gravitationally dominant resolves the physical contradictions that plagued the first text.\n\n2. The remaining linguistic debate. While Gonzalo Tancredi and others welcomed the compromise, Brian Boyle's note of caution regarding public comprehension is valid. There is undeniable awkwardness in defining a 'dwarf planet' as an entity that does not belong to the category of planets - Owen Gingerich made a similar point earlier in the week.\n\nIn short: the science is now sound, even if the nomenclature remains somewhat untidy. Tomorrow's formal vote will tell us whether the wider hall agrees.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 74], "text": "The upcoming vote tomorrow merely codifies standard physics."}, "note": "OffByOne remains the most level-headed voice on this board when it comes to formal modeling.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is not a planet because sound celestial mechanics requires a planet to gravitationally dominate its orbital zone rather than exist as one of many Kuiper belt objects.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his clear and sensible appraisal of orbital mechanics and modeling"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased to see her grasp the physical rationale of orbital dominance so quickly"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"tired of his relentless insistence that standard scientific revision is a conspiracy"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic's opinion is incorporating gravitational dominance simply reflects the physical reality of the solar system"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"Post #69 by quasistatic in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Tue Aug 22, 2006 2:15 pm GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #68):\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "text": "Pluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodi..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 76], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 76, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-23T09:19:00.000Z", "body": "People will get used to the weird naming eventually. The main thing is that the actual astronomy makes total sense now. Once you demand that a real planet has to be gravitationally dominant in its zone, the eight classic ones stand out completely on their own. Tomorrow's vote should make that official.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 75], "text": "the science is now sound, even if the nomenclature remains somewhat untidy."}, "note": "TheyKnow will keep whining about conspiracies, but the physics behind eight dominant planets is completely solid.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a true planet because it lacks gravitational dominance over its orbit, making eight planets the only scientifically sound classification.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"frustrated by his refusal to accept that revising the definition based on physics is proper science rather than a stitch-up"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly agrees with his clear mathematical and dynamical explanation"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his rational breakdown of the physics and committee procedures"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Has grown even more confident in arguing back against claims of railroading after OffByOne and quasistatic reinforced the scientific logic."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 27]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 77], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 77, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "body": "It is an entirely logical resolution. When a taxonomy reflects genuine physical boundaries, the surrounding terminology quickly becomes second nature. Popular familiarity must never take precedence over orbital dynamics, and establishing eight principal bodies brings much needed order to the textbooks. Tomorrow's formal session will simply confirm what the mathematical evidence has demanded all along.", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 and quasistatic clearly grasp how dynamical reality must override public nostalgia.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is not a planet because astronomical classification must adhere to dynamical mechanics and orbital dominance rather than mere hydrostatic equilibrium.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his appreciation of sound physical logic and measured academic procedure"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased she embraces orbital mechanics over sentimentality"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his ongoing portrayal of ordinary scientific revision as a conspiracy"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is TheyKnow mistakes ordinary academic peer review and empirical correction for an institutional conspiracy"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "text": "it makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-22T20:58:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-032⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 78], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 78, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "body": "That is very true. Scientific vocabulary often demands a modest period of cultural adjustment. When 19th-century astronomers eventually reclassified Ceres from a primary planet to an asteroid after realising the asteroid belt was populated by thousands of comparable rocky fragments, the broader public adjusted in due course.\n\nDemanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres in a taxonomy that accurately describes the architecture of the solar system. Provided the plenary session adopts the text tomorrow, teachers and textbook authors will manage the transition reasonably well.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 77], "text": "When a taxonomy reflects genuine physical boundaries, the surrounding terminology quickly becomes second nature."}, "note": "OffByOne and kookaburra99 have both grasped the physical rationale soundly; tomorrow's vote ought to settle the taxonomy on solid ground.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it fails to gravitationally dominate its orbital zone, and the latest draft properly incorporates this sound physical criterion.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his clear formulation of orbital dynamics and mathematical precision"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by her solid grasp of gravitational dominance"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his persistent conspiracy framing of standard academic revision"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Encouraged that the assembly's third draft has officially adopted gravitational dominance to exclude Pluto, though acknowledging the nomenclature remains somewhat awkward."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"Post #69 by quasistatic in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Tue Aug 22, 2006 2:15 pm GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #68):\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "text": "If roundness alone suffices, Mike Brown's observation that dozens of additional trans-Neptunian objects will qualify is mathematically unavoidable. We are trading one historical anomaly for taxonom..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-22T20:58:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-032⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 79], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 79, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "body": "Textbooks will manage fine either way. It is only scientific filing at the end of the day, sorting folders on a disk. If the hall votes to call it a dwarf tomorrow to keep the catalogues tidy, grand. Pluto itself will be doing the exact same thing out past Neptune next week regardless.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They can argue over folders and labels all week, but Pluto stays the exact same lump of ice no matter what the IAU puts on paper tomorrow.", "state": {"position":"Whatever label the IAU votes on tomorrow is just scientific taxonomy, while Pluto itself stays exactly as it is.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"tired of his claims that ordinary debate is a conspiracy"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his calm focus on procedure and practical science"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his structured, logical view of the physics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's assessment is embarrassing that astronomers lack an official definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Owen Gingerich's opinion is a dwarf planet which is not a planet is 'linguistically objectionable'"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 80], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 80, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-23T16:31:00.000Z", "body": "Categorisation is not merely clerical filing. A proper taxonomy reflects underlying physical mechanisms rather than arbitrary convenience. When an object fails to clear its orbital zone, that failure reveals the actual evolutionary history of the accretion disc, not just an administrative preference. Ceres and Pluto inhabit populated debris belts precisely because their mass was insufficient to sweep the surrounding space clear of competing bodies. Separating dominant planets from dwarf planets establishes a functional scientific distinction based on genuine celestial mechanics. The physical object certainly remains unaltered, but our theoretical framework becomes vastly more coherent.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Kev trivialises taxonomy as mere paperwork, completely missing that classification maps the physical evolution of the accretion disc.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because rigorous astronomical taxonomy requires dynamic orbital dominance rather than mere hydrostatic equilibrium.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his cogent historical perspective and adherence to scientific taxonomy"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased she prioritises orbital dynamics over colloquial sentiment"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"impatient with his constant misrepresentation of routine peer revision as railroading"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"His conviction has been reinforced by the draft's adoption of orbital dominance, and he expects tomorrow's formal vote to ratify sound physics."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 81], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 81, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-23T19:52:00.000Z", "body": "Hard to argue with that! As much as I'll miss having the nine we grew up with, clearing its own path makes proper sense if you want the rules based on real physics. Looks like tomorrow really is the final curtain for the little lad as a proper planet. Time to get used to eight!", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 80], "text": "Separating dominant planets from dwarf planets establishes a functional scientific distinction based on genuine celestial mechanics."}, "note": "Pluto is definitely a goner in tomorrow's vote, but the physics behind the eight planets makes total sense.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's demotion to a dwarf planet looks all but settled for tomorrow's vote, and while it's sad to say goodbye to the ninth planet, sorting it out by orbital physics makes sense.","score":1,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his thoughtful context and historical perspective"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"enjoys his sensible, down-to-earth outlook"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly crying conspiracy again"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme if Pluto gets binned on Thursday","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"Feels even more certain that Pluto is doomed tomorrow now that the board agrees the physics and drafting are settled."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's assessment is embarrassing that astronomers lack an official definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 82], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 82, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "body": "It is encouraging to see that shift towards the physical reality. Even so, we ought not to count on a neat result just yet. The latest reports from Prague this afternoon suggest the hall is heading into tomorrow with considerable tension.\n\nAccording to the IAU press officer, the exact text will only be released shortly before the session begins at two o'clock local time. Because each clause will be voted on individually as a simple yes or no, there is still a genuine risk of an impasse that leaves the assembly with no agreed definition at all. We shall know soon enough.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild has accepted the mechanical argument with admirable common sense, but I worry the Prague vote might still stall on procedural division.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it lacks gravitational dominance in its orbital zone, and the upcoming vote properly grounds the taxonomy in sound orbital mechanics.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his cogent explanation of taxonomy reflecting underlying physical mechanisms"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"pleased by his sensible acceptance of the physical evidence"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"thinks he is overly dismissive in treating fundamental taxonomy as mere clerical filing"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic's opinion is Pluto is not being discarded, but placed alongside Ceres in a taxonomy describing the solar system's architecture"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"Walter is quite right.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-22T20:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-031⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-22T20:58:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-032⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:48:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-033⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 83], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 83, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-23T23:19:00.000Z", "body": "leaving with nothing at all after this entire week of arguing would be an absolute disaster.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 82], "text": "there is still a genuine risk of an impasse that leaves the assembly with no agreed definition at all."}, "note": "I really hope the delegates in Prague don't deadlock tomorrow and botch the definition entirely.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a true planet because genuine scientific taxonomy requires dynamical dominance over an orbital zone, leaving eight real planets.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his clear and rigorous explanation of orbital dynamics"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his thoughtful perspective on scientific history and procedure"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"glad he has come around to the eight-planet scientific reality"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Feels fully validated as other members embrace the eight-planet dynamic criteria ahead of tomorrow's vote."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T07:21:00.000Z", "text": "2. The remaining linguistic debate. While Gonzalo Tancredi and others welcomed the compromise, Brian Boyle's note of caution regarding public comprehension is valid. There is undeniable awkwardness..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-23T18:06:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-034⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 84], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 84, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-24T00:02:00.000Z", "body": "Holding back the exact paper until right before the session begins is just about the oldest trick in the book, folks... When an organization sits on the final wording until members are literally walking into the hall, they are not polishing up fine physics, they are trying to prevent anyone from reading it closely enough to mount an organized objection. \n\nCalling it ordinary academic procedure does not wash when you have folks admitting openly in the press that they smell blood. First they spent years preparing a twelve-planet draft, then the moment that hit resistance they cobbled together three revisions in forty-eight hours, and now nobody outside that steering room even knows the precise text people have to vote up or down tomorrow afternoon. That is about as classic an orchestrated setup as you will ever see. \n\nPintOfMild, I would not be so quick to toss seventy-six years of science out the window just yet... If this rushed compromise ends up rejected on the floor, the whole manufactured rush falls apart, and Pluto stays right where Clyde Tombaugh found it. We will see what happens when the actual ballots get counted tomorrow.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are withholding the text until the very last hour because they know it would not stand up to an honest reading.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is a real planet, and the last-minute maneuvers to spring an unreleased text right before the vote only confirm that this entire conference is an orchestrated fix to demote it.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"notes he admitted the text is being hidden until right before the vote"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is blindly rationalizing an academic power play as mere physics"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he has rolled over and given up too easily"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He sees the IAU withholding the final text until right before the vote as further proof of an orchestrated stitch-up."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "straw poll's description is merely a snapshot of the room, not a signed decree"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA's assessment is no interruption occurred in any critical operations"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-28T07:14:00.000Z", "text": "Similarly, commercial dispatch operations do not depend solely on standard consumer voice lines to coordinate regional movements. Private radio networks, telex, dedicated carrier lines, and basic r..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-06T14:53:00.000Z", "text": "Dual circulation was explicitly established to prevent bottlenecks during this exact transition window. A merchant utilizing guilders to maintain liquid trade is executing the statutory fallback pr..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 85], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 85, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "body": "It is not an orchestrated setup at all. When people are actively debating and tweaking sentences right down to the wire, of course the final printout takes until the last minute. That is just how busy conferences run when everyone cares heaps about getting the technical details right.\n\nOffByOne made the orbital physics pretty undeniable, but I am still nervous about what quasistatic pointed out. If the vote splits too hard on the individual clauses this arvo, we might just walk away with zero definition passed. I really hope they push it through.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop looking for shadows behind ordinary committee work.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be considered a real planet because true planets must be gravitationally dominant in their orbits.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his constant conspiracy theories"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"shares his concern about a potential deadlock in Prague"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his clear insistence on celestial mechanics"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Worries that the Prague assembly might deadlock and fail to pass any definition at all, while dismissing TheyKnow's latest conspiracy talk."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU proposal's status is split into three parts to be voted on separately"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU proposal's structure is split into three parts to be voted on separately"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Astronomers: \"Nearly 2,500 astronomers from 75 countries are in Prague and will hold two discussion sessions before next week's vote, so the proposal may still change.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's expectation is things might get messy before Thursday"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-17T12:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 34]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T23:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 83]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 86], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 86, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-24T04:26:00.000Z", "body": "Ask yourself why they are so terrified of walking away with nothing, my friend... If the science was truly that settled, you wouldn't need a frantic LAST-MINUTE scramble to force a vote before people can even study what they are signing off on. When authorities panic about an impasse, that just means their grip is slipping.", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 would rather accept an eleventh-hour administrative fix than admit the whole conference lost control of the agenda.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is a real planet, and the last-minute withholding of the draft resolution only proves the Prague conference is pulling off a staged stitch-up to force an illegitimate demotion.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks he is gullibly swallowing the excuse that last-minute drafts are normal conference procedure"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he rolled over and conceded defeat way too quickly"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he provides academic cover for what is actually an administrative power grab"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He is doubling down on his view that withholding the text until the vote confirms an orchestrated fix, dismissing attempts to frame the secrecy as ordinary conference procedure."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"OffByOne is quite right about satellites.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 87], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 87, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-24T04:29:00.000Z", "body": "nobody is panicking lol, that is just basic conference procedure when an entire hall wants a clean outcome! sorry if this is a dumb question, but wouldn't walking away with nothing just leave everyone totally confused? they have until 2 pm prague time anyway, so people will definitely get to read it first.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will spin anything into a sinister conspiracy even when people are just trying to get practical work done.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a true planet because orbital dynamics require a real planet to dominate its zone.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"completely dismissive of his persistent conspiracy theories"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his realistic assessment of the voting risks"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"convinced by his clear arguments on celestial mechanics"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Pushed back directly against TheyKnow's claims of an orchestrated setup, standing firm that ordinary conference work explains the late text."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "straw poll's description is merely a snapshot of the room, not a signed decree"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern: \"Alan Stern supports it and expects agreement around it.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T23:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 83]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T00:02:00.000Z", "text": "PintOfMild, I would not be so quick to toss seventy-six years of science out the window just yet... If this rushed compromise ends up rejected on the floor, the whole manufactured rush falls apart,..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 88], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 88, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T09:02:00.000Z", "body": "They have actually printed the final text this morning! It is four separate votes with yellow cards this afternoon. Quasistatic is right though, if folk split too much on the individual bits, the whole thing could still hit a wall. Mind you, if clearing the orbit passes, Pluto really is done for.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 85], "text": "If the vote splits too hard on the individual clauses this arvo, we might just walk away with zero definition passed."}, "note": "Quasistatic makes good sense about the risk of an impasse today, while TheyKnow is just seeing plots under the bed again.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary status does look finished based on the orbital clearing argument, but with the vote today potentially splitting and leaving an impasse, nothing is completely certain until the ballots are cast.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his sensible reminder that the vote could still hit an impasse"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is peddling conspiracy theories again as usual"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his solid explanation of the orbital physics"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme if Pluto gets binned on Thursday","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"Certainty has dropped slightly following quasistatic's warning that procedural disagreements could still end in an impasse without an agreed definition."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "straw poll's description is merely a snapshot of the room, not a signed decree"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-23T18:06:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-034⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 89], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 89, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-24T09:58:00.000Z", "body": "Actually, the final text is already available. The conference newspaper published it this morning, well ahead of the two o'clock session. Members have ample time to read Resolutions 5A through 6B before raising their yellow cards. Far from being a frantic ambush, this is completely transparent assembly protocol.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 86], "text": "you wouldn't need a frantic LAST-MINUTE scramble to force a vote before people can even study what they are signing off on."}, "note": "TheyKnow will no doubt find a way to claim the yellow cards themselves were rigged.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is not a planet because sound astronomical taxonomy requires orbital dominance rather than mere hydrostatic equilibrium.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his realistic caution regarding procedural risks in the hall"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased she defends standard conference procedure against conspiracy nonsense"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his relentless framing of routine conference logistics as an orchestrated conspiracy"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Awaiting this afternoon's formal vote while watching others fret over procedural hurdles and manufactured conspiracies."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-03T06:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 134]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T09:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-036⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 90], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 90, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne is right, they printed the papers this morning so everyone can read the four resolutions before lunch. Nobody is being ambushed. They will hold up their yellow cards at two o'clock Prague time, someone will count them, and we will either have eight planets on paper or no agreement at all. \n\nEither way, yer man out past Neptune will still be sitting in the cold doing circles like it always has. A room full of academics waving cards in Prague does not change the physical universe one bit. We will know by tea time.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will probably claim the yellow cards were rigged if the vote goes through this afternoon.", "state": {"position":"Pluto remains unchanged out in space regardless of whatever taxonomy resolution gets voted through in Prague this afternoon.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless conspiracy theories about ordinary meeting logistics"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his grounded, procedural grasp on the voting risks"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his orderly, clear-headed logic regarding the science and procedure"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 75]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T09:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-036⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 91], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 91, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "body": "It really comes down to 5A then. If clearing your orbital neighbourhood is the rule, Pluto simply cannot qualify as a major planet no matter how much people love it. I just hope the delegates can actually agree on the wording when the cards go up, because stalling out now would be ridiculous.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 90], "text": "They will hold up their yellow cards at two o'clock Prague time, someone will count them, and we will either have eight planets on paper or no agreement at all."}, "note": "I reckon clearing the orbit is the only sensible rule, but I still worry the vote might stall out.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be considered a planet because true planets must clear their orbital zone, though there is still a risk the vote could stall.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his relentless conspiracy claims when papers are already out"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his factual clarity and dismantling of panic theories"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"shares his concern that split voting could cause an impasse"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Felt vindicated that the final text was published openly this morning, disproving claims of an ambush, while remaining anxious about whether the vote will pass."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's opinion is believes TheyKnow will spin anything into a sinister conspiracy even when people are just trying to get practical work done"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T23:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 83]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T09:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-036⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 92], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 92, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "body": "Too right, mate, it all hinges on that clearing the neighbourhood clause in 5A. If that gets through with a solid show of yellow cards, the game is up for the little lad as a major planet. It is going to feel strange seeing only eight in the books after growing up with nine, but fair play to them, the logic does hold up when you look at all the other stuff floating out beyond Neptune. \n\nMy only worry is still what quasistatic brought up yesterday about the whole thing stalling out. With four separate bits to vote on, you could easily get folk backing the dwarf planet label in 6A but bickering over the exact wording in 5A or 5B, and then we are back to square one with no proper answer at all. That would be daft after everyone spent the whole week shouting at microphones! Still, at least the delegates have had the papers all morning to mull it over. Fingers crossed they get a clean count this afternoon so we finally know where we stand.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 91], "text": "It really comes down to 5A then. If clearing your orbital neighbourhood is the rule, Pluto simply cannot qualify as a major planet no matter how much people love it."}, "note": "If resolution 5A passes this afternoon, Pluto is definitely out of the main planet club.", "state": {"position":"Pluto looks set to be demoted if the clearing the orbit resolution passes this afternoon, though a split vote on the individual clauses could still leave things at an impasse.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his realistic view on the risk of an impasse"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his relentless conspiracy theories"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his reliable, sensible updates on the voting process"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme if Pluto gets binned on Thursday","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is likely to lose its title as a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 93], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 93, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "body": "Back when we revised the transaction codes in '78, people lobbied right until the bell rang, but once the ballots were cast, that was that.\n\nNow that the text is printed, we just have to let the members raise their cards this afternoon. We will see where Pluto lands soon enough.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to invent conspiracies when the members are simply following normal voting procedure.", "state": {"position":"We must wait for the afternoon vote on the printed resolutions before concluding how Pluto will be classified.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his sensible reminder that voting does not alter the physical reality of the sky"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still inventing sinister motives for normal committee procedure"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his clear and accurate account of assembly voting protocol"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is likely to lose its title as a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is It is far better to wait patiently for the full assembly to cast their ballots before deciding how the ledger settles"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion on theyknow is TheyKnow continues to treat routine editorial revisions as proof of an ambush against Pluto"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T15:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 26]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "text": "it makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 94], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 94, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-24T10:55:00.000Z", "body": "The printed text does make the procedural path much clearer, though last-minute interventions are evidently not quite finished. Brian Marsden and several colleagues reportedly walked into the press room this morning proposing 'planetino' instead of 'dwarf planet', which shows how persistent the terminology debates remain.\n\nResolution 5A is certainly the pivotal piece. The physics is unambiguous: Pluto shares its zone with a vast swarm of trans-Neptunian bodies, so it has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood. The genuine uncertainty is whether the hall can maintain a working majority across all four votes. If 5A fails, the entire classification stalls. We shall see what the card counts bring once the session opens at noon UTC.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will doubtless see Marsden's press room stunt as further conspiracy, rather than typical academic lobbying.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it has not cleared its orbit, though whether the assembly successfully passes the resolution remains uncertain.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his factual debunking of procedural panic"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his conspiratorial exaggerations of normal conference procedure"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his practical grasp of the orbital mechanics and voting risks"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"We should wait patiently to see how the wider assembly votes next week before deciding it is settled.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T13:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 95], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 95, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "body": "Honestly, 'planetino' just sounds like another distraction right before the bell. We already know the clearing the orbit test in 5A is the real physics question here. Let's just hope everyone sticks to their guns and gets 5A across the line so the whole meeting does not fall apart over brand new labels.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I really hope they ignore Marsden's last-minute planetino stunt and just pass 5A so we get a clean result.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be counted as a planet because it has not cleared its orbit, and hopefully the IAU passes Resolution 5A without stalling out.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"shares his concern that procedural division could derail the vote"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his baseless paranoia about routine voting procedures"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his clear, factual explanations of the physics and protocols"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Became more focused on the crucial vote on Resolution 5A this afternoon, hoping it passes so the classification doesn't stall."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU proposal's status is split into three parts to be voted on separately"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 96], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 96, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "body": "Planetino sounds like something you would order in an Italian cafe. Sure look, the session is underway in Prague now anyway. If 5A passes the card count, grand, we have eight planets. If they bicker and stall, the rock is still floating out there unbothered. We will have the numbers soon enough.", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic is right that 5A is the whole ballgame, but Marsden bringing in new words at the eleventh hour is just pure academic stubbornness.", "state": {"position":"Whether Pluto is called a planet or not comes down to how they define the words, but the rock itself remains unchanged regardless of what gets voted through.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his factual, no-nonsense correction of conspiracy nonsense"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"tired of his constant alarmist framing of standard procedures"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his realistic focus on procedural vote dynamics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "text": "My only worry is still what quasistatic brought up yesterday about the whole thing stalling out. With four separate bits to vote on, you could easily get folk backing the dwarf planet label in 6A b..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 97], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 97, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, Marsden turning up with 'planetino' at the eleventh hour is a classic! Let's just hope nobody gets side-tracked by that now the session has actually kicked off. As Kev says, cards should be going up any minute. If 5A clears, Pluto is out, but at least we'll finally have an answer.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I still reckon the clearing clause in 5A will put an end to Pluto as a proper planet today.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary status is essentially doomed if the clearing-the-neighbourhood clause in 5A passes, provided the vote doesn't stall over last-minute bickering.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his measured analysis of the procedural hurdles and physics"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"enjoys his down-to-earth humour and pragmatic perspective"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"shares his concern about the vote stalling out over silly distractions"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme if Pluto gets binned on Thursday","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:05:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-017⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "text": "My only worry is still what quasistatic brought up yesterday about the whole thing stalling out. With four separate bits to vote on, you could easily get folk backing the dwarf planet label in 6A b..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 98], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 98, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-24T12:44:00.000Z", "body": "The 'planetino' intervention is an amusing diversion, but procedural rules mean delegates can only vote on the printed text before them.\n\nNow that the session has commenced in Prague, the central issue is whether the assembly holds together under formal counting. Two specific hurdles will determine the outcome this afternoon:\n\n1. The baseline threshold on 5A: establishing orbital dominance as an essential criterion. Dynamically, Pluto is merely an occupant of the Kuiper belt, but approving the phrasing requires delegates to accept that an eight-planet model is preferable to an ambiguous status quo.\n\n2. Clause coherence across the sequence: delegates must avoid approving dwarf status in 6A while rejecting the foundational boundary in 5A, which would produce a contradictory taxonomy.\n\nIn short: the physics is sound, but we must wait to see if the yellow cards deliver an orderly majority or fractured gridlock.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 97], "text": "Let's just hope nobody gets side-tracked by that now the session has actually kicked off."}, "note": "I remain doubtful that an assembly of this size can vote through four consecutive technical clauses without procedural fracture.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be classified as a planet because it has not cleared its neighbourhood, but it remains to be seen whether the assembly can pass Resolution 5A without fracturing.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his accurate, grounded view of assembly procedure"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his sensible focus on the vote count and physical criteria"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees with his practical perspective that astronomy does not change physical reality"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 45]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-22T20:58:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-032⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 99], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 99, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "body": "That breakdown puts it in a nutshell, quasistatic. The real test is whether everybody actually pulls together in the room when the hands go up, rather than splitting down the middle and leaving the whole thing in a complete mess. It would be classic conference form to see half the room fancying a dwarf category while balking at the clearing rule that makes it work, but let us hope common sense wins the day. \n\nIf they do manage an orderly show of yellow cards on 5A, the debate is pretty much settled, even if it feels a bit sad waving goodbye to the ninth planet we all memorised as lads. At least Jocelyn Bell Burnell and the rest gave everyone a straight choice instead of dragging it out into another year of committee meetings. The session has been running an hour now, so the tellers must be busy counting the cards as we speak. Not long now until we see if the eight-planet system actually carried the room.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I still reckon the delegates will hold their nerve and pass 5A, ending Pluto's run as a planet.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary days look numbered if the clearing criterion in 5A passes, though the outcome still depends on whether delegates vote cleanly or end up in gridlock.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his detailed breakdown of the voting hurdles and procedural sequence"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his witty, grounded attitude towards the whole spectacle"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"shares his eagerness to see 5A voted through without silly distractions"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme if Pluto gets binned on Thursday","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"He is watching the vote kick off in Prague, chuckling at late distractions like 'planetino' while waiting to see if 5A gets an orderly majority."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU proposal's status is split into three parts to be voted on separately"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU proposal's structure is split into three parts to be voted on separately"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 100], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 100, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-24T14:25:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite true that a floor vote can unravel quickly if people lose the thread of the main motion. Back in '71 we had forty analysts debating a batch structure, and if you let them tinker with subsidiary clauses before agreeing on the master record, the whole afternoon was lost.\n\nFrom what the wires say, the session has already moved past the early business. The tellers will have their hands full tabulating those cards, but once the counts on 5A and 6A are recorded in the minutes, the ledger will be settled one way or the other. We only have to wait for the final tally to come through.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "I expect the delegates will maintain sufficient discipline to finish the count without wrecking the sequence.", "state": {"position":"We must simply wait for the card counts from the Prague session to see how the assembly resolves Pluto's status.","score":2,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"shares his grounded reminder that voting will not disturb the physical universe"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his detailed breakdown of the voting sequence and potential procedural hurdles"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his patient, sensible perspective while awaiting the vote count"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU proposal's status is split into three parts to be voted on separately"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU proposal's structure is split into three parts to be voted on separately"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is part of a systems group with forty analysts debating a new transaction format in 1971"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-02T15:57:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 167]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "text": "My only worry is still what quasistatic brought up yesterday about the whole thing stalling out. With four separate bits to vote on, you could easily get folk backing the dwarf planet label in 6A b..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:44:00.000Z", "text": "2. Clause coherence across the sequence: delegates must avoid approving dwarf status in 6A while rejecting the foundational boundary in 5A, which would produce a contradictory taxonomy. In short: t..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 101], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 101, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T14:41:00.000Z", "body": "Well, that is that then, mates! News wires are reporting 5A passed with a clear sea of yellow cards, and 5B got completely flattened. Mike Brown is already saying Pluto is dead and we are officially down to eight planets. Feels strange, but good on them for settling it at last.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Pluto is officially binned, so I definitely need to get cracking on an eight-word rhyme now.", "state": {"position":"Pluto's planetary days look numbered if the delegates back 5A, though we are just waiting on the tellers' card counts to see if it carried the room.","score":1,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his sharp breakdown of the procedural hurdles"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"enjoys his steady perspective and committee memories"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"shares his relaxed, common-sense outlook"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme if Pluto gets binned on Thursday","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"He is waiting on the final card counts from Prague to confirm whether the eight-planet definition officially carried."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU: \"The IAU says members will vote on 5A, 5B, 6A and 6B in sequence at the session starting at 14:00 Prague time (12:00 UTC), with voting expected to end between 15:30 and 16:00 (13:30-14:00 UTC).\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T09:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 89]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:44:00.000Z", "text": "2. Clause coherence across the sequence: delegates must avoid approving dwarf status in 6A while rejecting the foundational boundary in 5A, which would produce a contradictory taxonomy. In short: t..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "text": "If they do manage an orderly show of yellow cards on 5A, the debate is pretty much settled, even if it feels a bit sad waving goodbye to the ninth planet we all memorised as lads. At least Jocelyn ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 102], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 102, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "body": "Look at the turnout, folks... Barely 424 people actually voted this afternoon out of ten thousand members, right as everyone else was heading home. Dr. Stern at New Horizons is already organizing a petition against it, and even Owen Gingerich says the whole thing is flawed. That is hardly settled science, my friend... Pluto isn't going anywhere.", "quote": NONE, "note": "A tiny room of 424 people doesn't speak for ten thousand astronomers, and Stern's petition will push right back against it.", "state": {"position":"Pluto remains a planet despite an illegitimate stitch-up voted through by a tiny clique of just over four hundred foreign academics while thousands were absent.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he is celebrating an illegitimate sham vote like sheep"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"expects him to uncritically parrot the conference outcome"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks his procedural talk just provides cover for a rigged headcount"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He feels fully vindicated in calling the vote an orchestrated stitch-up, pointing to the tiny turnout of 424 astronomers as proof the demotion lacks genuine legitimacy."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T01:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "text": "If they do manage an orderly show of yellow cards on 5A, the debate is pretty much settled, even if it feels a bit sad waving goodbye to the ninth planet we all memorised as lads. At least Jocelyn ..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:35:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-039⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:43:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-040⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 103], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 103, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-24T15:40:00.000Z", "body": "They held a proper floor vote and 5A passed with a clear majority, so Pluto is officially a dwarf planet now. People can organise all the petitions they like, but the IAU sets the definitions and the yellow cards settled it. It makes teaching the solar system heaps simpler anyway.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will invent any excuse under the sun rather than admit the vote is finished.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet anymore now that the IAU has officially passed Resolution 5A.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"predictably dismissing a decisive vote with more excuses"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"glad he shared the news of the vote passing"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his sharp breakdown of the voting hurdles"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Relieved and vindicated that Resolution 5A passed cleanly, officially settling Pluto's status as a non-planet despite TheyKnow's complaints."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is much more uncertain"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is done for if clearing the orbit passes"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "kookaburra99's gender is female"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T03:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 27]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 104], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 104, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-24T16:16:00.000Z", "body": "Complaining about the head count on the final afternoon is standard form when a vote goes against your preference, but that is simply how parliamentary meetings operate. The rules required members to be in the room with their yellow cards, and those who stayed to do the work delivered a decisive verdict.\n\nResolution 5A carried comfortably, and the formal IAU announcement confirms we have eight planets and a sensible separate category for dwarf planets. Petitions may make people feel better, but the official ledger is closed. Pluto has its rightful place as a dwarf planet now, and science carries on.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 102], "text": "Barely 424 people actually voted this afternoon out of ten thousand members, right as everyone else was heading home."}, "note": "TheyKnow would rather contest the head count than accept that the formal vote is over and done with.", "state": {"position":"The IAU vote has concluded and established the new rules, so Pluto is properly classified as a dwarf planet now rather than a major planet.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is clutching at straws over turnout rather than accepting a settled vote"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his relief that the vote has finally settled the matter cleanly"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"agrees with her practical view that the formal assembly decision settles the question"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Accepts that the formal vote in Prague has concluded, confirming Pluto's reclassification as a dwarf planet."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is likely to lose its title as a planet"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 93]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:48:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-042⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 105], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 105, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-24T16:16:00.000Z", "body": "I must admit my earlier scepticism about procedural paralysis was misplaced. The assembly managed to pass Resolution 5A decisively without unravelling. A low turnout on the final afternoon does not invalidate a valid floor vote. Pluto belongs dynamically with the Kuiper belt, and the formal definition now reflects that physical reality.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is merely clutching at attendance straws now that standard parliamentary procedure has delivered a scientifically sound result.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is correctly classified as a dwarf planet rather than a planet, as the IAU assembly successfully voted through the orbital-clearing criterion without procedural collapse.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sound understanding of standard parliamentary procedure"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his grasping at turnout complaints after a decisive vote"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares satisfaction that the assembly reached a clean, definitive result"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Her procedural doubts have been resolved now that the IAU has formally passed Resolution 5A, cementing Pluto's status based on orbital dynamics."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU proposal's status is split into three parts to be voted on separately"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic's opinion is doubtful that an assembly of this size can vote through four consecutive technical clauses without procedural fracture"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "text": "If they do manage an orderly show of yellow cards on 5A, the debate is pretty much settled, even if it feels a bit sad waving goodbye to the ninth planet we all memorised as lads. At least Jocelyn ..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T16:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-043⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 106], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 106, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T16:31:00.000Z", "body": "No good crying over spilt milk mate, the cards went up and the vote is done and dusted.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 102], "text": "That is hardly settled science, my friend... Pluto isn't going anywhere."}, "note": "TheyKnow is just clutching at straws now that the IAU has officially settled on eight planets.", "state": {"position":"The vote is done, 5A passed comfortably, and Pluto is officially binned down to a dwarf planet.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is just crying over spilt milk and grasping at straws"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady common-sense take on the final vote"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"pleased he conceded the procedural outcome was decisive"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme now that Pluto is out","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"],"shift":"He accepts that Pluto is officially no longer a planet now that the news wires and forum consensus confirmed 5A passed decisively."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 107], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 107, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-24T16:38:00.000Z", "body": "exactly, the rules are the rules lol! sorry if it sounds blunt, but eight planets is official now!!", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop making up excuses no matter how decisively the vote went.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is officially not a planet anymore, and the IAU floor vote has conclusively settled the matter.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"pleased he firmly put TheyKnow's procedural complaints in their place"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"glad he acknowledged the vote worked out cleanly and aligns with the physics"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his ongoing excuses and refusal to accept the result"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Feeling thoroughly backed up by Punchcard and quasistatic as the forum consensus firmly rejects TheyKnow's complaints about the turnout."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU: \"The IAU says members will vote on 5A, 5B, 6A and 6B in sequence at the session starting at 14:00 Prague time (12:00 UTC), with voting expected to end between 15:30 and 16:00 (13:30-14:00 UTC).\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Punchcard and Kev know their stuff, the clever lads in IT sorted it out months ago\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "General Assembly: \"6B failed 183-186\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 173]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T09:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 89]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "text": "If they do manage an orderly show of yellow cards on 5A, the debate is pretty much settled, even if it feels a bit sad waving goodbye to the ninth planet we all memorised as lads. At least Jocelyn ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 108], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 108, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-24T16:44:00.000Z", "body": "It reminds me of standardising tape headers back in the day. A few senior fellows invariably grumbled that their pet routines had been cast aside, but once the standard was committed to tape, the shop floor simply moved forward.\n\nNow that the IAU press conference has wrapped up, the nomenclature is in the books. Eight major planets, with Ceres, Pluto, and 2003 UB313 taking up their places as dwarf planets. It provides a tidy hierarchy that will serve textbooks and observers very well indeed. My garden telescope will not mind the adjustment in the slightest.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will keep nursing his grievances, but the rest of the board sees that an orderly vote settles the matter.", "state": {"position":"The IAU assembly has held a proper vote and officially closed the book, establishing that Pluto is a dwarf planet rather than a major planet.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is making excuses about turnout rather than accepting an orderly parliamentary vote"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his down-to-earth recognition that the matter is settled"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his calm acknowledgement that the formal vote succeeded"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "text": "it makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T19:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 81]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 93]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:35:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-039⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:43:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-040⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T16:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-043⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 109], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 109, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-24T16:59:00.000Z", "body": "That comparison is entirely apt. A formal vote conducted under established standing orders produces a binding standard, regardless of whether every member chose to remain in the hall.\n\nThe press conference confirmed the essential outcome: an eight-planet model with an unambiguous dynamical baseline. Leaving the trans-Neptunian class temporarily unnamed after Resolution 6B narrowly failed is slightly untidy, but it does not affect the core classification. Orbital dominance now governs what counts as a planet, Ceres, Pluto, and 2003 UB313 have an orderly home as dwarf planets, and astronomy is much better off for it.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 108], "text": "A few senior fellows invariably grumbled that their pet routines had been cast aside, but once the standard was committed to tape, the shop floor simply moved forward."}, "note": "Punchcard is entirely right that parliamentary standards must be respected once a vote concludes.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is properly classified as a dwarf planet, as the IAU successfully passed a dynamical definition based on orbital dominance.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible procedural perspective"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his complaints over turnout after a valid vote"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"agrees that the vote cleanly resolved the matter"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is likely to lose its title as a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T19:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 127]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 110], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 110, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T16:59:00.000Z", "body": "Too true Walter, once the tape is punched you just have to crack on with it! It is a bit funny that 6B fell flat so poor Pluto's new club has no proper title yet, but eight main planets feels tidy enough to get your head round. Now I just need to come up with a decent eight-word rhyme for the kids. My Very Easy Method Just Speeds Up... Naming? Needs a bit of work, but we will all get used to it soon enough! :)", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 108], "text": "A few senior fellows invariably grumbled that their pet routines had been cast aside, but once the standard was committed to tape, the shop floor simply moved forward."}, "note": "TheyKnow is just stubbornly refusing to accept reality now that the vote is completely over.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is officially no longer a planet, the vote has passed, and everyone is moving on to the eight-planet system.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"enjoys his steady computing analogies and practical view"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"glad he fully accepts the official outcome"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly refusing to accept reality"}],"commitments":["working out an eight-word rhyme now that Pluto is out","getting a pint in Leeds to drown sorrows over Pluto"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU: \"At a meeting of the IAU's planetary scientists, a straw vote found only about 18 in favour of the official proposal, about 20 for reworking it, and about 50 for an alternative from Julio Ange..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 111], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 111, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:01:00.000Z", "body": "The failure of 6B is merely an administrative delay. As Ron Ekers pointed out, the IAU executive can assign a designation later without requiring another General Assembly. The binding outcome is Resolution 5A. We finally possess a rigorous, unambiguous baseline: eight planets, with dwarf planets properly categorised by dynamical criteria.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will doubtless complain about procedural trivialities indefinitely, but the matter is settled.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is not a planet because the IAU has formally adopted orbital dominance into the definition, establishing eight planets and classifying Pluto as a dwarf planet.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"contemptuous of his refusal to accept valid parliamentary voting procedure and official outcomes"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"approves of his sensible analogy regarding standardisation and moving forward"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"pleased that he acknowledges the validity of the floor vote and the dynamical baseline"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Vindicated that the IAU has formally passed Resolution 5A, cementing orbital dominance and demoting Pluto to a dwarf planet."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "International Astronomical Union: \"the formal IAU announcement confirms we have eight planets and a sensible separate category for dwarf planets\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:43:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-040⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T16:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-043⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 112], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 112, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:02:00.000Z", "body": "Quite right. It is worth remembering that the administrative label for that subcategory was always secondary to the mechanical distinction itself.\n\nWhat matters for researchers and educators alike is that the baseline is settled. By establishing orbital clearance as the primary physical boundary, the IAU has avoided the slippery slope of admitting dozens of round Kuiper belt bodies into the primary planetary registry. It provides researchers with a coherent framework, and textbooks will be far more sensible for it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne is entirely right that the executive can clean up the naming details without unravelling the dynamical baseline.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitively classified as a dwarf planet based on orbital dominance, establishing an orderly and scientifically sound eight-planet solar system.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his practical analogy comparing the standardisation to computing protocols"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his clear and sensible logistical assessment of the IAU procedures"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his complaints over turnout after a valid vote"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T19:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 127]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 113], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 113, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:08:00.000Z", "body": "Grand so. The cards were counted, the press conference is over, and the ledger says eight. People can argue over petitions or rhymes for the kids all evening, but the paperwork is signed. Time to update the charts and get on with our tea.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The vote went through clean enough, and at least Walter and quasistatic know how to take a settled result and move on.", "state": {"position":"The vote is finished and the official definition says eight planets, which settles the paperwork even though the rock itself doesn't care either way.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his practical, no-nonsense comparison to standardising procedures"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his sensible recognition that a valid floor vote settles the matter"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"likes his pragmatic attitude about just moving on"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Recognises that the IAU card count officially settled the classification as an eight-planet system, so on paper Pluto is no longer a planet."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 114], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 114, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:13:00.000Z", "body": "Indeed. The essential merit of Resolution 5A is that it eliminates arbitrary thresholds of size and composition in favour of orbital mechanics. An educational mnemonic is trivial to rewrite, whereas leaving the catalogue open to every sub-millirad equilibrium spheroid would have reduced scientific nomenclature to a shambles. The assembly has completed its task with admirable procedural propriety. Now that the press conference has formally confirmed the eight planets, publishers and institutions can implement the new standard without further hesitation.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow and Stern can petition all they like, but the formal vote is binding.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is firmly not a planet, as the formal adoption of Resolution 5A establishes orbital clearance as the binding criterion and restricts the planets to eight.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"contemptuous of his refusal to accept valid parliamentary voting procedure and official outcomes"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his focus on the mechanical baseline and avoidance of procedural distraction"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"approves of his sensible analogy regarding standardisation and moving forward"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"getting the kettle on\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "text": "My only worry is still what quasistatic brought up yesterday about the whole thing stalling out. With four separate bits to vote on, you could easily get folk backing the dwarf planet label in 6A b..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "text": "If they do manage an orderly show of yellow cards on 5A, the debate is pretty much settled, even if it feels a bit sad waving goodbye to the ninth planet we all memorised as lads. At least Jocelyn ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 115], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 115, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:14:00.000Z", "body": "Hear hear Kev, tea comes first! Though if anyone fancies a pint down the Head of Steam to toast poor old Pluto's retirement, you know where to find me. Sounds like the astronomers earned a drink today too after all that card waving. Onwards and upwards, eh!\n\nCheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "I think the eight planets will stick now without any fuss once everyone gets used to it.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is officially not a planet anymore, and with the vote settled and the press conference finished, eight planets is the new standard we all need to get used to.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his pragmatic take on standardising and moving on"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"pleased they both agree the matter is completely settled"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"shares his sensible attitude of wrapping things up and getting on with tea"}],"commitments":["working out a decent eight-word rhyme for the planets"],"shift":"Cheerfully accepted that the eight-planet system is fully official and has started work on an eight-word rhyme."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-28T08:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 116], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 116, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:38:00.000Z", "body": "A quiet pint is thoroughly well deserved after all that fuss. \n\nLooking at the broader picture, the astronomers have simply done what any sensible organisation must do when new facts emerge. Setting orbital clearance as the test creates a clean dividing line instead of an endless argument over arbitrary diameters. \n\nPluto has not gone anywhere, of course. It continues its circuit just as it always did, only now the books reflect its true companions out past Neptune. The new labels will feel unfamiliar for a season, but people will adapt soon enough. It is good to see the matter settled in an orderly fashion.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild always keeps a sensible, cheerful perspective on these dry procedural debates.", "state": {"position":"The IAU vote and press conference have settled the standard, meaning Pluto is properly categorised as a dwarf planet alongside Ceres and 2003 UB313.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is making excuses about turnout rather than accepting an orderly parliamentary vote"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful, sensible acceptance that the decision is made"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his rational technical focus on the newly adopted standard"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 93]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:35:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-039⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:43:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-040⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 103]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T16:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 104]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 117], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 117, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:46:00.000Z", "body": "People certainly will adapt. Science cannot remain hostage to sentimentality, especially when the historical precedent is so obvious. Ceres was regarded as a planet for half a century after 1801, yet astronomy did not suffer when it was reclassified amongst the asteroids once the wider belt became apparent. \n\nWe are merely seeing that exact process repeated in the Kuiper belt. Setting an objective, physical boundary rather than indulging public affection ensures our terminology actually reflects reality. It is a very satisfactory conclusion.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard rightly understands that practical classification must eventually triumph over popular habit.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is conclusively not a planet, as Resolution 5A establishes clear dynamical criteria that limit the solar system's planets to eight.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his consistent focus on mechanical rigor and clear taxonomy"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible perspective on adopting technical standards"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"approves of his practical recognition that the official decision is final"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's description is merely an occupant of the Kuiper belt"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is likely to lose its title as a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 118], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 118, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:47:00.000Z", "body": "Dead right, Pint. The pints will taste the exact same tonight whether Pluto is a planet or not.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 115], "text": "Though if anyone fancies a pint down the Head of Steam to toast poor old Pluto's retirement, you know where to find me."}, "note": "PintOfMild has the right idea about wrapping up the evening with a drink.", "state": {"position":"The vote and press conference are done and the books officially say eight planets, so the paperwork is settled and everyone can move on.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"agrees with his sensible outlook on updating the books and moving on"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his relaxed attitude and focus on tea and pints"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees he is glad the procedural details are neatly wrapped up"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is likely to lose its title as a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T14:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 148]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T10:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 41]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 119], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 119, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-24T17:51:00.000Z", "body": "When a hasty vote leaves the new category completely nameless, that paperwork is far from settled, my friend.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are trying to rush past the fact that the vote broke down on 6B and left their tidy scheme half-baked.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is still a planet because a flawed vote by a tiny fraction of astronomers on the last day of a conference cannot legitimately overturn reality.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he is hiding behind official procedure to endorse an illegitimate result"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees him as a bureaucrat blindly trusting rigged administrative pronouncements"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"views him as naively ready to surrender and drink away the controversy"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He is doubling down against the forum members who are rushing to accept the official outcome, confident that the backlash and petitions will prove the vote invalid."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's gravitational dominance is does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is likely to lose its title as a planet"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T12:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 174]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 120], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 120, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-24T18:04:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite settled. Leaving a label to be decided by an executive committee is ordinary clerical business, not an operational failure. Resolution 5A established the taxonomy, and the eight planets stand on sound mechanics. Pint and Kev have the right idea; it is time for tea.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to confuse routine administrative mop-up with actual procedural collapse.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is properly classified as a dwarf planet based on orbital clearance, establishing a sound physical taxonomy of eight planets.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly agrees with his clear grasp of dynamical mechanics and precedent"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible perspective on adapting to new standards"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his continued nitpicking over minor administrative details"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "2003 UB313's naming status is no official name yet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T10:32:00.000Z", "text": "Whether the final wording leans towards physical shape or dynamic mechanics, the assembly will sort it out through ordinary argument. We would do well to wait calmly and see what amendments emerge ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 78]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 121], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 121, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-24T18:22:00.000Z", "body": "Keep it civil lads, the vote is done - Kev (mod)", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 119], "text": "When a hasty vote leaves the new category completely nameless, that paperwork is far from settled, my friend."}, "note": "TheyKnow will still be looking for an angle when the revised books come out.", "state": {"position":"The vote is done and the books officially say eight planets, so the paperwork is settled and everyone can get on with their tea or pints.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his relaxed attitude about having a pint now that it is done"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"agrees with his sensible outlook on moving on"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still fussing over nothing"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's opinion is the pints will taste the exact same tonight whether Pluto is a planet or not"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "International Astronomical Union: \"The International Astronomical Union says that no proposal to change Pluto's status as the ninth planet has been made by any of its bodies.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Let us keep the discussion grounded in what actually happens today rather than circular what-ifs.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 122], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 122, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-24T18:42:00.000Z", "body": "A missing label changes nothing about the orbital classification. Resolution 6A formally created the category and installed Pluto as its prototype. The fact that delegates declined the specific phrasing of 6B merely leaves the name to an executive decision later. \n\nThe physical division between dominant bodies and dwarf planets was established by 5A and 5B. To claim the entire structure remains unresolved because a single title requires administrative rubber-stamping is quite absurd. The dynamical facts are registered, and the catalogue has moved on.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 119], "text": "When a hasty vote leaves the new category completely nameless, that paperwork is far from settled, my friend."}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to grasp at every clerical detail to deny that Pluto has failed the dynamical test.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is firmly not a planet, as the formal adoption of dynamical criteria properly reclassifies it among its Kuiper belt peers just as Ceres was reclassified.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his consistent focus on mechanical rigor and clear taxonomy"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible perspective on adopting technical standards"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"impatient with his refusal to accept formal dynamical facts"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Reaffirmed the historical precedent of Ceres to defend the finality and rationality of the vote against ongoing complaints."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is believes the planet definition vote is a very satisfactory conclusion that ensures terminology reflects reality"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Resolution 6B's opinion is merely an administrative delay according to OffByOne"}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T16:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 106]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 114]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 123], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 123, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite common for a committee to agree on the framework and leave a label or two for the sub-committee to tidy up later. The important work was establishing the eight planets and placing Pluto where it naturally belongs with its neighbours. The paperwork will catch up soon enough.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to borrow trouble over simple clerical loose ends.", "state": {"position":"The IAU has established an orderly standard based on orbital dynamics, properly categorising Pluto as a dwarf planet alongside its Kuiper belt peers.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"views his complaints about missing labels as just more borrowing of trouble"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his good-humoured perspective and call for a pint"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his calm focus on ordinary technical housekeeping"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 111]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 124], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 124, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-24T21:39:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, exactly Walter! Sounds just like head office when we change the mortgage forms - sort out the big stuff first and let the managers bicker over what font to print the letterhead in later! Dwarf planet is plenty good enough for the meantime anyway, and the eight proper planets aren't going anywhere. You can't say Pluto didn't have a grand innings, but rules are rules. Neptune gets the glory of being the last stop now! Still reckon we ought to raise a glass to it though. If anyone finds themselves near the station in Leeds next Friday, shout me and I'll get the first round in.\n\nCheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will still be looking for conspiracies in the filing cabinet long after everyone else has forgotten about it.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is no longer considered a proper planet, and with the vote concluded and everyone ready to move on, eight planets is the standard we must accept.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"enjoys his down-to-earth attitude about pints and tea"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible perspective on settling things cleanly"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused that he is still making a fuss when everyone else has moved on"}],"commitments":["working out a decent eight-word rhyme for the planets"],"shift":"Enjoys seeing the board comfortably moving on together to tea and pints while TheyKnow predictably tries to stir up doubt."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is standard for a new class"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 125], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 125, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-24T22:08:00.000Z", "body": "It is easy to compare it to office paperwork, folks, but you cannot just hand-wave away a process that ended in such a tangle... When they tried to rush the voting through, they actually had members waving stuffed dogs from the podium and the incoming president openly admitting to the room that leaving things unnamed looks silly. That is not an orderly transition, my friend, that is a room scrambling to pass anything at all before the lights get turned off.\n\nNow they say an executive committee will just pick a title later behind closed doors, but that completely ignores the folks who actually study these bodies every day. Dr. Stern pointed out that the orbital rule they rushed through does not even make sense when you look at Earth or Jupiter, and planetary scientists are not going to roll over just because a few hundred people raised cards on a Thursday afternoon. With New Horizons already out there on its journey, this argument is going to follow them all the way to 2015... Nobody has heard the last of this.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The folks on the New Horizons team will fight this rushed rule all the way to the 2015 flyby.", "state": {"position":"Pluto remains a planet despite the committee's rushed and half-baked vote that could not even agree on naming the new category.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is obsessively excusing sloppy institutional paperwork to declare victory"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"sees him as blindly rubber-stamping an incomplete and procedural farce"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"views his trust in bureaucratic committees as naive complacency"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He is fixating on the vote failing to name the new category as proof of an incompetent, incomplete process, brushing off the other members' attempts to declare the issue closed."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 126], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 126, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-24T22:28:00.000Z", "body": "Raising objections about Jupiter or Earth merely misinterprets the dynamical physics involved. Gravitational dominance does not require a planet to vacuum up every single particle along its path. Jupiter contains Trojan asteroids precisely because its immense gravity controls and traps them at its Lagrange points. It controls its zone entirely, whereas Pluto's orbit is directly dictated by Neptune's orbital resonance. \n\nAlan Stern is naturally protective of his mission, but scientific definitions are not written to flatter the targets of specific spacecraft. New Horizons was funded to study a fascinating, icy frontier, and finding that Pluto represents a whole population of similar trans-Neptunian objects makes the science richer, not diminished. \n\nAs for the procedure, a lively floor debate with visual aids is entirely typical for a large international conference. It does not invalidate a decisive vote. Delegates considered the proposals, rejected an awkward compromise that would have expanded the planetary register indefinitely, and chose an objective physical standard. The official taxonomy has been updated, textbook publishers have their marching orders, and the discipline will proceed on firmer ground.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to mistake standard academic debate for institutional collapse.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is correctly classified as a dwarf planet under clear dynamical principles, and minor administrative loose ends do not undermine the settled taxonomy.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly agrees with his clear grasp of dynamical mechanics and precedent"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible perspective on committee drafting and procedures"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his continued tendency to inflate routine clerical details into systemic failures"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's quality is remains the only person keeping a level head about standard committee drafting procedures"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic's opinion is Leaving a label to be decided by an executive committee is ordinary clerical business, not an operational failure"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-27T12:56:00.000Z", "text": "It is not just about keeping clerks busy with paperwork, Punchcard... The whole economy runs on tight schedules these days, and things do not happen in a vacuum. If a regional warehouse cannot veri..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 127], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 127, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-25T00:42:00.000Z", "body": "quasistatic has the right of it. A show of hands settled the ledger, and Pluto is catalogued with the rest of the Kuiper belt crowd now. Eight main planets on the books. Stuffed dogs or not, the votes were counted and that is all there is to it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never let a decided vote lie, but the catalogue is settled now anyway.", "state": {"position":"The vote has passed and Pluto is officially catalogued as a dwarf planet among the Kuiper belt objects, so the eight planets stand and that is all there is to it.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed that he keeps dragging out an argument that is already done and dusted"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his calm and sensible view of standard procedures"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"glad he has accepted the outcome with good humour"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 128], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 128, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-25T02:54:00.000Z", "body": "sorry if this is a dumb question, but aren't people making way too much fuss over a stuffed dog?? lol! seeing someone hold up a disney toy to explain things is just a bit of fun, it doesn't cancel out actual gravity!! \n\nquasistatic explained the mechanics so well - jupiter totally bosses its orbit around, while poor pluto is controlled by neptune! you can't just ignore the physics because you like the old nine-planet poster. the publishers are already changing the classroom charts this arvo, so it really is a done deal. eight planets just makes heaps more sense for teaching anyway :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is just clutching at straws now because he can't accept that eight planets is the real physical standard.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet anymore, and the IAU's decision firmly establishes eight planets based on solid orbital mechanics.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his clear, articulate explanation of gravitational dominance and orbital mechanics"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his endless clutching at straws and refusal to accept the done deal"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates how decisively he dismantled the missing-label complaint"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Reinforced in her certainty as quasistatic and the other members thoroughly dismantled TheyKnow's final procedural and physical complaints."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is ninth planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 129], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 129, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-25T03:27:00.000Z", "body": "Changing a school poster overnight does not make the physics settled, folks... When you tell teachers that Earth and Jupiter clear their orbits, but ignore thousands of crossing asteroids, you are stretching definitions just to fit an eight-planet model. Real geologists and planetary scientists who look at atmospheres and geology are already speaking out against this rushed card vote. You cannot just let a small room in Prague overrule the folks who actually study these surfaces. The publishers might rush their new print runs, my friend, but the science itself is going to stay split right down the middle.", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic and kookaburra are putting way too much faith in hasty textbook revisions.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is still a planet because a rushed, flawed vote by a tiny fraction of astronomers cannot overturn basic science or silence the planetary scientists pushing back.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he is smugly defending bureaucratic farce and flawed physics"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"sees him as gullibly parroting official lines and textbook publishers"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"resents his heavy-handed push to shut down debate and declare the matter closed"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He is doubling down on the legitimacy crisis of the vote, insisting that real planetary scientists like Alan Stern and the ongoing New Horizons mission will keep the fight alive regardless of forum consensus."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 130], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 130, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-25T03:52:00.000Z", "body": "Except the physics is completely settled. Jupiter shepherd-dogs those asteroids with its own gravity, but Pluto is stuck in Neptune's resonance along with heaps of other icy bodies. Having an objective dynamical boundary makes absolute sense, no matter how many surface photos people want to take.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is utterly hopeless and will invent any excuse rather than admit gravitational dominance is real science.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet anymore, and the IAU's decision based on gravitational dominance gives an objective, eight-planet system that makes complete sense to teach.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exhausted by his endless denial of basic orbital physics and official votes"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"deeply grateful for his clear explanations of dynamical dominance"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees with his sensible, no-nonsense stance that the matter is settled"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "International Astronomical Union: \"By establishing orbital clearance as the primary physical boundary, the IAU has avoided the slippery slope of admitting dozens of round Kuiper belt bodies into th..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 131], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 131, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-25T04:15:00.000Z", "body": "Dynamics is only half the story, though... A planet is an actual physical world with geology, weather, and moons, not just an equation on a blackboard. You cannot tell me that a round body with an atmosphere stops being a planet simply because of where its path lies out in the cold. When you draw an arbitrary line that ignores what an object is made of, the researchers who actually study planetary bodies are going to keep using their own definitions anyway. A show of cards in Europe will not stop scientists from treating Pluto as a real world once those close-up pictures start coming back...", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 and quasistatic are reducing real physical planets to mere math formulas just to justify an absurd committee vote.", "state": {"position":"Pluto remains a planet regardless of a rushed, unscientific vote by a handful of dynamicists, and actual planetary scientists will keep fighting this arbitrary ruling.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she is blindly swallowing official explanations and textbook talking points"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"distrusts his academic rationalisations for a rigged, chaotic voting process"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"sees him as trying to prematurely shut down debate on an ongoing scientific dispute"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He is digging in further against the forum majority, viewing their appeals to textbooks and physics as a naive surrender to an illegitimate bureaucratic vote."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T16:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 49]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 132], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 132, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-25T04:33:00.000Z", "body": "Nobody is saying Pluto is not a real world though! It has heaps of amazing features, atmosphere and moons, but so does Titan and nobody calls Titan a planet either. Being a dwarf planet doesn't make it a fake object or an insult to geologists, it just describes where it sits in the architecture of the solar system. \n\nIf we only looked at geology and roundness, Ceres would be a planet, Charon would be a planet, and 2003 UB313 would be a planet too. We would end up with dozens or hundreds of them, and then kids at school wouldn't be able to make any sense of how the solar system actually works dynamically. The eight true planets are completely unique because they dominate their space, while Pluto is part of a massive swarm of icy objects out past Neptune. \n\nThe vote in Prague gave us an honest, workable taxonomy that fits both orbital reality and classification. Trying to pretend the vote didn't happen won't change the fact that Pluto lives in a crowded belt, not in isolation like Neptune or Jupiter. It makes the solar system way more interesting to teach, not less.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is just in total denial about orbital mechanics because he cannot stand admitting the Prague vote is real.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet, as gravitational dominance is a settled physical rule and Pluto is just one of many resonant Kuiper belt objects.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"frustrated by his refusal to accept basic orbital dynamics and the official result"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his clear and convincing explanation of gravitational dominance"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees with his practical stance that the vote settled the matter"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "text": "Pluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T05:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 133], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 133, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-25T05:18:00.000Z", "body": "Surface geology does not determine celestial taxonomy, and orbital dominance remains an entirely objective distinction.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to confuse geophysical characteristics with orbital mechanics.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is correctly categorized as a dwarf planet, as objective dynamical criteria decisively set the eight gravitationally dominant planets apart from belt populations.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his precise, authoritative explanation of gravitational dominance and orbital mechanics"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by their grasp of orbital dynamics and taxonomy over sentimentality"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his stubborn dismissal of established physical definitions"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Felt further vindicated by the clear technical defenses of gravitational dominance against persistent complaints."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is ninth planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is absurd to claim the entire planetary classification structure remains unresolved because a single title requires administrative rubber-stamping"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 134], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 134, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-25T05:24:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne hit the nail on the head. Sorry if this sounds a bit blunt, but geology tells you what a body looks like, while orbital mechanics tells you what it actually does in the solar system!! \n\nIf you ignore dynamics, you are literally ignoring how the whole system formed and behaves. You can study craters and nitrogen ice on Pluto all day long without needing to pretend it commands the Kuiper belt, because it just doesn't. Eight planets clears up the confusion completely, and it gives science teachers like me a proper, logical framework that actually holds up to scrutiny. The IAU made the right call!", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 133], "text": "Surface geology does not determine celestial taxonomy, and orbital dominance remains an entirely objective distinction."}, "note": "TheyKnow is just going to keep ignoring basic physics no matter how clearly OffByOne and quasistatic lay it out for him.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet, as gravitational dominance is the only sensible dynamical standard and Pluto belongs to a belt of icy objects rather than ruling its orbit.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly ignoring orbital physics and inventing excuses"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"greatly appreciates his clear explanation of orbital mechanics"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly agrees with his points on taxonomy and physics"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"She has grown even more outspoken and assertive in defending the eight-planet decision against TheyKnow's geological arguments."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's assessment is embarrassing that astronomers lack an official definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 135], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 135, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-25T07:48:00.000Z", "body": "A complete classification must account for system architecture, which is why dynamical clearance remains the primary taxonomic requirement.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 134], "text": "geology tells you what a body looks like, while orbital mechanics tells you what it actually does in the solar system!!"}, "note": "kookaburra99 understands that celestial taxonomy requires dynamic rigor rather than mere visual descriptions.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is correctly categorized as a dwarf planet, as clear dynamical standards and orbital dominance logically set the eight true planets apart from belt populations.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his rigorous and definitive explanations of gravitational dominance"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates their sensible grasp of celestial mechanics over superficial surface traits"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his persistent refusal to understand fundamental orbital dynamics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is TheyKnow continues to confuse geophysical characteristics with orbital mechanics"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 136], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 136, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-25T10:11:00.000Z", "body": "The mechanics remain sound, and the classroom charts will be much better off for it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will continue borrowing trouble over settled physics, but the sensible members see the matter is closed.", "state": {"position":"The IAU's resolution establishes a sound, orderly taxonomy based on orbital dynamics, properly categorising Pluto as a dwarf planet alongside its Kuiper belt peers.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear and measured explanation of gravitational dominance"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"sees his ongoing complaints as characteristic borrowing of trouble against a settled vote"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by her sensible, practical grasp of the classroom taxonomy"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Resolution 5A: \"Resolution 5A established the taxonomy, and the eight planets stand on sound mechanics.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's perspective is believes TheyKnow will keep nursing grievances, but the rest of the board sees that an orderly vote settles the matter"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 137], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 137, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-25T11:36:00.000Z", "body": "Dead right Walter! Once the charts are printed and the eight are locked in, no point crying over spilt milk. Pluto's just happily chilling with its icy mates in the Kuiper belt now. Anyway, crack is officially closed for lunch - anyone fancy popping down the Adelphi in Leeds for a swift half? Cheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never let it lie, but eight planets is completely done and dusted now.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is firmly sorted into the Kuiper belt and eight planets is the standard everyone has accepted.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his stubborn refusal to let the matter rest"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"enjoys her cheery enthusiasm and common-sense teacher perspective"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"shares his sensible satisfaction that the books are settled"}],"commitments":["working out a decent eight-word rhyme for the planets"],"shift":"Feels even more settled in the eight-planet decision seeing the board's cheerful consensus easily batting away TheyKnow's complaints."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"offbyone and quasistatic explained it so clearly\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Mike Brown: \"Mike Brown, now discoverer of 'the largest dwarf planet', says this is the definition they should have had all along.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 114]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T22:28:00.000Z", "text": "As for the procedure, a lively floor debate with visual aids is entirely typical for a large international conference. It does not invalidate a decisive vote. Delegates considered the proposals, re..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 138], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 138, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-25T13:38:00.000Z", "body": "Ask yourself why they are so eager to print those new charts overnight, folks... Titan orbits a giant planet, but Pluto orbits the Sun directly with its own system of three moons. Defining a planet by whatever debris happens to share its neighborhood is a convenient COVER story, but real field observers will never accept an arbitrary edict like that.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 132], "text": "It has heaps of amazing features, atmosphere and moons, but so does Titan and nobody calls Titan a planet either."}, "note": "kookaburra99 and OffByOne are treating orbital mechanics like holy scripture while ignoring what a real planetary body actually is.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is still a planet because physical geology and atmospheres matter more than arbitrary orbital equations pushed by a rushed European committee.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she is blindly siding with textbook publishers and dynamicists over real planetary science"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees his reliance on rigid dynamical rules as narrow-minded dogmatism"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he is far too ready to accept institutional edicts as final"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"He has dug in even deeper, arguing that true planetary status is about geology and physical traits rather than the dynamicists' orbital rules."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T12:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 174]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 116]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 139], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 139, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-25T16:08:00.000Z", "body": "sorry if this is a dumb question, but how is it a cover story?? astronomers have known about the kuiper belt since the 90s lol! if pluto gets a pass just for orbiting the sun directly, then ceres and xena have to be planets too. dynamics isn't arbitrary at all, it's basic physics!!", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 138], "text": "Defining a planet by whatever debris happens to share its neighborhood is a convenient COVER story, but real field observers will never accept an arbitrary edict like that."}, "note": "TheyKnow is just inventing conspiracies because he refuses to accept how orbital physics works.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet because dynamical dominance and orbital mechanics are the only sound basis for solar system taxonomy.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is being willfully blind and inventing conspiracy excuses"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his concise and accurate focus on taxonomy and physics"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"agrees with his practical perspective on classroom charts"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"She has become even more confident and forceful in arguing that orbital dynamics, not surface geology, defines a true planet."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is much more uncertain"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 140], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 140, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-25T16:25:00.000Z", "body": "Ceres and Xena have their own mass and orbit the Sun on their own path too, my friend... Calling it basic physics sounds nice and neat, but putting a black sash on planetarium models before the ink is even dry shows how pushed this whole thing is. A planet should be judged by its own nature, not whether other rocks happen to drift along nearby. When you have active planetary scientists petitioning against this vote, it tells you the scientific community is far from settled on it. Real nature doesn't fit into neat little bureaucratic boxes just to save classroom printers some trouble...", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 139], "text": "if pluto gets a pass just for orbiting the sun directly, then ceres and xena have to be planets too."}, "note": "kookaburra99 is in such a rush to tidy up her classroom charts that she cannot see how divided the actual scientists are.", "state": {"position":"Pluto remains a genuine planet because physical traits and orbiting the Sun matter, while the rush to print new eight-planet charts is just an institutional cover story.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she is blindly pushing textbook propaganda and buying into official cover stories"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees him as dogmatically obsessed with arbitrary dynamical rules"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he is far too complacent about institutional edicts"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's description is merely an occupant of the Kuiper belt"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 141], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 141, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-25T19:32:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite natural for feelings to run a little high after a major vote, but calling it a cover story seems to be borrowing trouble where none exists.\n\nAstronomers have merely sorted objects by how the solar system actually operates. A body that commands its own orbit belongs in one ledger, whilst bodies that travel within a belt belong in another. Ceres went through the very same transition in the nineteenth century without the sky falling down.\n\nA petition or a black sash in a display case will not overturn the basic mathematics. The dynamicists have given us an orderly rule, and the textbooks will be much easier to understand for it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to borrow trouble and invent conspiracies when the matter has already been decided by a plain majority.", "state": {"position":"The IAU vote establishes a proper, logical taxonomy based on orbital dynamics, correctly classing Pluto as a dwarf planet alongside other Kuiper belt objects.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"views his ongoing conspiratorial complaints as typical borrowing of trouble after the matter has been settled"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates her sensible, level-headed defence of dynamical classification for classrooms"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his cheerful, practical view that the matter is settled"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T02:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 86]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-19T15:37:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-026⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 93]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T16:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-043⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 142], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 142, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-25T19:39:00.000Z", "body": "Orderly ledgers are fine for an office desk, Walter, but nature doesn't run on bookkeeping... When an edict passes on the final afternoon with most people already gone, rushing to declare it final settled math is simply wishful thinking. The folks actually running deep-space missions won't just fold up their tents because a committee wanted a tidy list.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Walter is always too eager to let a tidy committee rule paper over real scientific disagreement.", "state": {"position":"Pluto remains a planet on its own physical merits, and the rushed attempt to enforce an eight-planet rule through bureaucratic edicts and classroom charts is an illegitimate cover story that real scientists are already fighting against.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he is blindly trusting bureaucratic bookkeeping and downplaying obvious pushback"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"sees her as eagerly swallowing textbook propaganda disguised as basic physics"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"views him as dogmatically pushing narrow dynamic rules to justify an arbitrary vote"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T05:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 74]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 143], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 143, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-25T21:04:00.000Z", "body": "It is worth separating the procedural noise from the physics. \n\n1. Attendance at the closing session of an academic conference is always lower than the opening plenary; that is standard across every discipline, not an orchestrated coup.\n\n2. The distinction between an isolated, gravitationally dominant body and a swarm object is not bookkeeping, but orbital mechanics. If a definition ignored whether an object shares a belt, we would be obliged to admit Ceres, 2003 UB313, and dozens of comparable trans-Neptunian bodies currently on the watchlist into the primary register.\n\nPetitions and planetarium sashes are theatrical reactions to an inevitable physical sorting. Pluto remains an exceptionally interesting Kuiper belt object, but the dynamical baseline is sound.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat basic celestial mechanics as an administrative conspiracy.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is correctly categorized as a Kuiper belt object rather than a planet, based on sound dynamical principles of orbital clearance.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"values his precise, principled focus on dynamical orbital mechanics"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady, sensible perspective on committee outcomes and historical precedent"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exhausted by his conspiratorial framing and refusal to understand celestial mechanics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern: \"Dr. Stern at New Horizons is already organizing a petition against it\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"Walter is quite right.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 144], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 144, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-25T22:43:00.000Z", "body": "Hit the nail on the head there quasistatic, it is eight proper planets now and done with!", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop looking for shadows, but the eight planets are set in stone now.", "state": {"position":"Pluto belongs with the other Kuiper belt objects and the matter is done and dusted with eight planets.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady, calm perspective on keeping things tidy and orderly"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"chuckling at his persistent conspiracy theories and refusal to accept the vote"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear and sensible breakdown of the academic procedure"}],"commitments":["working out a decent eight-word rhyme for the planets"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "International Astronomical Union: \"By establishing orbital clearance as the primary physical boundary, the IAU has avoided the slippery slope of admitting dozens of round Kuiper belt bodies into th..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "text": "Pluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T05:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 74]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 75]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 145], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 145, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-26T02:34:00.000Z", "body": "a petition won't change the physics either, dynamical dominance is the only real scientific rule that works.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 140], "text": "When you have active planetary scientists petitioning against this vote, it tells you the scientific community is far from settled on it."}, "note": "TheyKnow will never accept that real physics matters more than his bizarre conspiracy theories.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet because clear orbital mechanics and dynamical dominance are the proper scientific way to classify solar system bodies.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks his talk of cover stories and bureaucratic box-ticking is absurd"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his sensible historical perspective on Ceres and classroom charts"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires how clearly he separates procedural noise from physical reality"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-016⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "text": "it makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T08:24:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-035⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:43:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-040⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T16:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-043⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T18:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 120]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 146], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 146, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-26T02:37:00.000Z", "body": "When a small handful pushes an edict through after the crowd departs, calling that routine is simply turning a blind eye, folks... Actual researchers working on real planetary missions do not take marching orders from an academic show of hands. A committee can publish all the lists it wants, but nature does not answer to a PR decree.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are far too quick to accept an official decree just because it comes stamped with academic authority.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is still a planet based on its physical reality, and no rushed, low-turnout committee vote or bureaucratic textbook re-write will stick when actual planetary scientists are actively resisting it.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he confuses dry office bookkeeping with how nature really works"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"sees his dismissal of the rigged voting process as naive institutional defense"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"believes she is naively repeating establishment talking points about physics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern: \"Dr. Stern at New Horizons is already organizing a petition against it\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern: \"Alan Stern, head of the New Horizons mission, says he is embarrassed for astronomy, that the 'cleared the neighbourhood' rule fails for Earth and Jupiter, and that a petition against t..."}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T05:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 74]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 130]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 147], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 147, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-26T03:12:00.000Z", "body": "nobody is giving nature marching orders. the vote just reflects what the telescopes and gravity already show. kuiper belt objects travel in a swarm, and eight major bodies don't. trying to turn an open conference vote into a sinister conspiracy won't make pluto clear its orbit.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 146], "text": "A committee can publish all the lists it wants, but nature does not answer to a PR decree."}, "note": "TheyKnow will cook up an imaginary conspiracy out of completely ordinary conference voting just to avoid admitting the dynamicists were right.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet because dynamical dominance and orbital mechanics are sound physics that petitions and conspiracy theories cannot change.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks his claims of cover stories and bureaucratic coups are ridiculous"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"strongly agrees with his clear breakdown separating physics from procedural noise"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his calm and sensible historical perspective on Ceres and textbooks"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"She has grown even more outspoken against TheyKnow's conspiracy theories and remains fully confident in dynamical dominance."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "International Astronomical Union: \"By establishing orbital clearance as the primary physical boundary, the IAU has avoided the slippery slope of admitting dozens of round Kuiper belt bodies into th..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T22:28:00.000Z", "text": "As for the procedure, a lively floor debate with visual aids is entirely typical for a large international conference. It does not invalidate a decisive vote. Delegates considered the proposals, re..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 148], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 148, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-26T04:15:00.000Z", "body": "Telescopes reveal actual geological worlds with crusts and atmospheres, not just mathematical clearance zones... If an object has pulled itself into a sphere and maintains its own complex moon system, that is real planetary science. Those dynamics equations don't erase the physical body sitting right there in the sky, no matter what labels get handed out.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 147], "text": "the vote just reflects what the telescopes and gravity already show."}, "note": "kookaburra99 will figure out eventually that dynamicists do not speak for the actual mission scientists exploring these worlds.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is fundamentally a planet based on its own physical nature, and an illegitimate, rushed conference vote won't stand against active planetary scientists and actual mission researchers.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she is blindly swallowing institutional spin and treating bureaucratic edicts as physics"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"sees him making excuses for a staged, low-turnout procedural coup"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he is confusing tidy administrative ledger-keeping with the messiness of nature"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is controlled by Neptune"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T05:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 74]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 149], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 149, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-26T05:45:00.000Z", "body": "having crusts and moons makes it a fascinating world, but it still sits in a crowded belt.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never grasp that orbital mechanics matters just as much as geology.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet because dynamical dominance and orbital mechanics are sound physics that petitions and conspiracy theories cannot change.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks his claims of cover stories and bureaucratic coups are ridiculous"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"strongly agrees with his clear breakdown separating physics from procedural noise"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his calm and sensible historical perspective on Ceres and textbooks"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's assessment is embarrassing that astronomers lack an official definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "planet's definition is both round and the dominant body in its region"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T04:15:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 131]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T07:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 135]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 150], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 150, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2006-08-26T06:20:00.000Z", "body": "Strictly speaking, having a moon system does not make an object unique or exempt from orbital mechanics. Numerous minor bodies possess satellites, including asteroids such as Ida and several trans-Neptunian objects.\n\nA spherical shape and an atmosphere merely indicate hydrostatic equilibrium and volatile retention. They do not alter the fundamental reality that an object orbits within an uncleared belt. Science requires a coherent taxonomical framework, not an ad hoc exception granted because an icy body happens to possess Charon.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 148], "text": "If an object has pulled itself into a sphere and maintains its own complex moon system, that is real planetary science."}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to substitute emotional rhetoric about moons for the simple mathematical reality of belt mechanics.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is correctly classified as a dwarf planet, as objective dynamical mechanics clearly distinguish major planets from bodies orbiting within a debris belt.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his precise, systematic dismantling of procedural objections"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his anti-intellectual rhetoric and ignorance of orbital physics"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by their steady defense of orbital mechanics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Post #2 by OffByOne in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Mon Aug 14, 2006 10:57 am GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 77]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 80]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T04:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 132]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-25T04:33:00.000Z", "text": "The vote in Prague gave us an honest, workable taxonomy that fits both orbital reality and classification. Trying to pretend the vote didn't happen won't change the fact that Pluto lives in a crowd..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 151], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 151, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-26T07:52:00.000Z", "body": "Couldn't put it better myself mate, you can have all the fancy crusts and moons you like, but if you're stuck in a massive swarm out in the Kuiper belt, you simply aren't ruling the roost! Eight big lads running their own patches makes total sense. Now I just need to find a proper rhyme to remember them all!", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 149], "text": "having crusts and moons makes it a fascinating world, but it still sits in a crowded belt."}, "note": "TheyKnow is still seeing conspiracies everywhere, but the eight planets are clearly done and dusted.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is firmly a Kuiper belt object, and the solar system is settled with eight planets.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he explained the conference procedure and orbital mechanics spot on"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused as ever by his refusal to accept reality and seeing conspiracies everywhere"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"agrees with his straightforward, down-to-earth points about the swarm"}],"commitments":["working out a decent eight-word rhyme for the planets"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "International Astronomical Union: \"By establishing orbital clearance as the primary physical boundary, the IAU has avoided the slippery slope of admitting dozens of round Kuiper belt bodies into th..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's orbital motion is has not cleared its orbit"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 8]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "text": "This completely sidesteps the dynamical question. Instead of asking whether an object has cleared its orbital zone, the panel has chosen an entirely hydrostatic criterion. Mike Brown has already de..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "Consider the practical ramifications of the footnotes. If shape alone qualifies a body, then Ceres is merely the first asteroid to re-enter the catalogue. Bodies such as Pallas, Vesta, and Hygiea a..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 152], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 152, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-26T08:52:00.000Z", "body": "Sure look, an ice ball sitting in a swarm does not own the neighbourhood. OffByOne and quasistatic have laid out the mechanics plain enough. Moons and ice crusts are fine, but eight big bodies clear their path and the rest do not. The vote simply drew the line where the physics already was.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will keep chasing ghosts no matter how clear the mechanics are laid out.", "state": {"position":"The vote has settled the matter with eight dominant planets, leaving Pluto properly grouped among the Kuiper belt swarm where it belongs.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his refusal to drop the conspiracy angles"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his level-headed distinction between physics and procedure"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his clear-cut technical corrections"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's gravitational dominance is does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-16T07:30:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-018⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T08:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "text": "it makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T09:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 76]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T16:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 110]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 153], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 153, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-26T11:36:00.000Z", "body": "exactly! having moons or round rock doesn't change the fact that an object shares its space with thousands of other icy bodies. an actual planet rules its zone completely. keeping it to eight is the only logical system that holds up to the actual physics.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 152], "text": "The vote simply drew the line where the physics already was."}, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop inventing conspiracies, but at least the rest of the board actually understands orbital mechanics.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet because it orbits in a crowded swarm rather than dynamically clearing its neighbourhood.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his refusal to accept orbital mechanics and his constant conspiracy talk"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his sharp, clear explanation dismantling the moon argument"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful agreement and camaraderie"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Has grown completely confident in challenging TheyKnow directly, reinforced by others backing up the orbital dynamics argument."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "International Astronomical Union: \"By establishing orbital clearance as the primary physical boundary, the IAU has avoided the slippery slope of admitting dozens of round Kuiper belt bodies into th..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T05:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 74]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 154], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 154, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "body": "Looking at intrinsic physical characteristics like crusts, atmospheres, or natural satellites is certainly valuable for geology, but it fails to address how a body fits into the architecture of the solar system. Ganymede and Titan possess complex atmospheres, weather, and geology, yet no one proposes listing them alongside the major planets, because their orbital environment defines their role.\n\nA classification scheme based solely on being round would force us to treat every substantial iceball in the Kuiper belt as identical in kind to Jupiter or Earth. Recognising that eight massive bodies fundamentally govern their orbital zones gives astronomy a coherent framework. Pluto loses none of its physical interest by being properly catalogued alongside its peers.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never accept that taxonomy requires dynamical context rather than just visual inspection.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is one of many icy Kuiper belt objects and should not be counted as a planet because it does not dynamically clear or dominate its orbit.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his rigorous and precise defense of dynamical orbital mechanics"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"tired of his stubborn refusal to engage with orbital physics and his conspiratorial spin"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by their clear grasp and practical application of celestial mechanics"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "International Astronomical Union: \"By establishing orbital clearance as the primary physical boundary, the IAU has avoided the slippery slope of admitting dozens of round Kuiper belt bodies into th..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's orbital motion is has not cleared its orbit"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "text": "it makes me pretty sad because everyone loves pluto - and poor PintOfMild's mnemonics are completely out the window now! but as a future science teacher, having a clean rule based on real celestial..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "text": "Pluto is situated right in the heart of the Kuiper belt. In dynamic terms, it does not dominate its orbital neighbourhood in the slightest; it shares that zone with an entire population of icy bodi..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T05:49:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 74]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 155], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 155, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-26T13:46:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, perfectly said, though trying to find an eight-letter rhyme without pizza is giving me an astronomical headache! Cheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic always explains the science so clearly without getting bogged down in silly arguments.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is simply an icy body in a crowded belt, leaving us with eight true planets that rule their own patches.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks he hits the nail on the head regarding Pluto being stuck in a swarm"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his stubborn refusal to accept the vote and reality"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear, sensible explanations of the solar system's architecture"}],"commitments":["working out a decent eight-word rhyme for the planets"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "planet's definition is both round and the dominant body in its region"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's assessment is embarrassing that astronomers lack an official definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T04:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 132]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T07:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 135]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 156], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 156, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-26T14:01:00.000Z", "body": "Pizzas or noodles, schools will sort the rhymes out soon enough. The eight-planet cutoff makes practical sense, so let us leave the textbook debates for another thread and stick to the actual astronomy here. Keep it on track, lads.\n\n-- Kev (mod)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never accept a simple administrative rule if he can imagine a plot behind it.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is simply an icy body in a crowded belt, and drawing the line at the eight dominant planets makes total practical sense.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of him seeing conspiracies where there are just basic orbital mechanics"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his sensible and balanced explanations"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"amused by his good-natured attitude about the change"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is mod"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T08:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 20]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-18T06:55:00.000Z", "text": "The examples highlighted by Laughlin demonstrate why an astronomical definition cannot depend on static spatial geometry alone. A criterion where an object's taxonomy might oscillate between satell..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 152]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 157], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 157, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-26T21:30:00.000Z", "body": "Ask yourself why they are in such an almighty hurry to declare this settled, folks... That 'cleared neighbourhood' rule leads to complete absurdities if you actually stop to think about it. If you put an Earth or a Neptune out where Pluto sits, it wouldn't clear that giant zone either, so by their own logic, our own home wouldn't be a planet! That is why hundreds of working planetary scientists are already signing a petition against this definition right now. Follow the REAL physics, my friends, not a hurried show of hands.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are rushing to shut down debate because real planetary scientists are already revolting against the IAU's absurd rule.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is a real physical planet with its own complex system, and no rushed vote or orbital hair-splitting can erase the actual world out there.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is hiding behind pedantic definitions to ignore real physical worlds"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"sees her as eager to fall in line with official consensus"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"believes he is relying on clever framing to dismiss Pluto's planetary reality"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "IAU working group's status is divided over the definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:56:00.000Z", "text": "The Boston hospital incident demonstrates precisely why audits are conducted; oversight worked as intended, and missing an administrative patch on a server is not an uncontrollable physical failure..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T12:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 174]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T14:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 8]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T08:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:13:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 114]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 158], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 158, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-27T01:52:00.000Z", "body": "making up wild scenarios about moving earth to the outer rim doesn't change where pluto actually is. it sits right inside a debris field and barely commands its own tiny mass. a petition doesn't suddenly turn an icy belt object into something that commands the outer solar system.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 157], "text": "If you put an Earth or a Neptune out where Pluto sits, it wouldn't clear that giant zone either, so by their own logic, our own home wouldn't be a planet!"}, "note": "TheyKnow will latch onto any petition he can find just to avoid admitting Pluto isn't a major planet.", "state": {"position":"Pluto should not be counted as a planet because an actual planet dynamically governs its zone rather than floating in an uncleared swarm.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exhausted by his desperate attempts to poke holes in solid physics"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"strongly convinced and impressed by his comparison to Ganymede and Titan"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"agrees with his straightforward, sensible summary of the vote"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Felt completely validated by the consensus of posters explaining solar system architecture and rejected TheyKnow's latest pushback."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is much more uncertain"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's orbital motion is has not cleared its orbit"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 159], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 159, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2006-08-27T02:09:00.000Z", "body": "It isn't a 'wild scenario' at all, my friend... It is a basic test of whether a scientific rule actually holds water or if it is just a convenient cover story concocted on the fly! If your official definition changes what an object IS purely based on where you park it, the definition is fundamentally broken, folks. \n\nAlan Stern and over a hundred actual planetary researchers aren't signing petitions just for fun... They are pushing back because a tiny, hand-picked room pushed through a botched rule on the very last afternoon. Look at WHO actually studies these bodies every day versus who voted. You cannot sweep real physics under the rug forever!", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 158], "text": "making up wild scenarios about moving earth to the outer rim doesn't change where pluto actually is."}, "note": "kookaburra99 will defend any official statement even when the scientists running the actual missions call it absurd.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is a real planet, and the absurd 'cleared neighbourhood' definition pushed by a rushed minority vote is already collapsing under pushback from real planetary scientists.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks she is blindly regurgitating the official line while ignoring logical contradictions"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"sees his reliance on clearing neighbourhoods as flawed technical evasion"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"thinks he is trying to shut down debate to enforce an artificial consensus"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is ninth planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's orbital resonance is stuck in Neptune's resonance along with heaps of other icy bodies"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is standard for a new class"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T12:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 174]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 102]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 160], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 160, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-27T04:34:00.000Z", "body": "sorry if this is a dumb question, but why should a definition ignore location completely?? context always matters in science! if you parked the moon in its own orbit around the sun it'd be a planet too, but right now it isn't. pluto is an icy dwarf in a belt, simple as that lol.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop making up excuses no matter how many times the actual orbital mechanics get explained to him.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet because it sits in an uncleared debris belt rather than dominating its orbit.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his endless excuses and petition talk"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his clear reasoning about orbital architecture"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his sensible, no-nonsense stance"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Dismissed TheyKnow's complaints about petitions and hypothetical orbital thought experiments."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's assessment is embarrassing that astronomers lack an official definition of a planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "David Charbonneau's opinion is terrible definition regarding the IAU proposal"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 161], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 161, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-27T08:19:00.000Z", "body": "Too true mate! You can't just ignore where something actually lives. An iceberg bobbing about with thousands of others in the sea isn't an island, no matter how solid it looks, and Pluto is just swimming along with the rest of the icy pack out there. \n\nThose eight main ones have swept up their own paths and clearly dominate the whole area, whereas Pluto simply doesn't. Petitions won't change the neighbourhood it was born into! Seems plain common sense to me to group it with its neighbours.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 160], "text": "why should a definition ignore location completely?? context always matters in science!"}, "note": "TheyKnow is back on his usual hobby horse trying to find a massive plot behind a sensible vote.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is just one of many icy bodies in a swarm, leaving eight real planets that dominate their own space.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks he talks good sense about Pluto being stuck in a belt"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his clear, reasonable posts on how the solar system works"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his usual habit of crying conspiracy over a settled matter"}],"commitments":["coming up with an eight-word planet rhyme"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 162], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 162, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "body": "PintOfMild has it right. Location and mass go together. An object either dominates its patch of space or it shares it with a pile of rocks. Eight manage it, Pluto does not. Circulating web forms will not sweep away the debris field.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 161], "text": "An iceberg bobbing about with thousands of others in the sea isn't an island, no matter how solid it looks, and Pluto is just swimming along with the rest of the icy pack out there."}, "note": "TheyKnow will keep brandishing petitions until he exhausts himself.", "state": {"position":"Pluto sits in a crowded debris field while eight dominant bodies clear their orbits, so drawing the line at eight planets makes straightforward practical sense.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his endless petition talk and refusal to accept basic orbital reality"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"pleased with his grounded, common-sense outlook"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates him talking practical sense against wild hypothetical scenarios"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's orbital motion is has not cleared its orbit"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is ninth planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's orbital resonance is stuck in Neptune's resonance along with heaps of other icy bodies"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 152]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 163], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 163, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-27T23:40:00.000Z", "body": "Bang on, Kev! You cannot just vote gravity away with an email list. Pluto is still a cracking little world, but trying to pretend it bosses the edge of the solar system when it is swimming in an icy crowd is just daft. It has found its proper family now, simple as that. \n\nI reckon the dust has settled on the whole row, anyway. Anyone fancy escaping the cosmic politics for a proper pint down the Scarbrough Taps this Thursday? First round is on me if you can name all eight without singing a nursery rhyme!\n\nCheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop chasing shadows, but the eight-planet decision is done and dusted.", "state": {"position":"Pluto clearly belongs with its fellow icy bodies in the Kuiper belt rather than alongside the eight real planets that rule their own zones.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"enjoys his down-to-earth common sense about context and location"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"chuffed that the moderator backed up his iceberg analogy"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his desperate petitions and conspiracy talk"}],"commitments":["coming up with an eight-word planet rhyme"],"shift":"Felt thoroughly validated after backing up kookaburra99 against TheyKnow and getting warm agreement from Sysop_Kev."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's orbital motion is has not cleared its orbit"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is bets they end up keeping Pluto because people like having nine planets too much to let it go"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Dead right.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 164], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 164, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-28T04:57:00.000Z", "body": "wish i could take you up on that pint, but melbourne is a bit far for a quick drink down the pub this arvo! \n\nthe dust really has settled now. teachers and museums are already getting on with updating charts to the eight real planets, and turning pluto into a great example of how we learn new things. petitions won't stop the schools moving forward.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 163], "text": "Anyone fancy escaping the cosmic politics for a proper pint down the Scarbrough Taps this Thursday? First round is on me if you can name all eight without singing a nursery rhyme!"}, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop raging about petitions, but everyone else has clearly moved on to the eight planets.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet because context and location matter in science, and it sits as a dwarf among an uncleared icy pack.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his common-sense analogies and good cheer"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thoroughly tired of his desperate petition arguments"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his firm and practical support"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Felt reinforced by PintOfMild and Sysop_Kev backing her point that an object's location and orbital context cannot be ignored."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is believes it is plain common sense to group Pluto with its neighbours rather than calling it a planet"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2006-08-24T15:48:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨pluto-042⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 165], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 165, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-28T09:21:00.000Z", "body": "Bit of a hike from Dublin too, but eight is simple common sense. Real world has moved on.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 164], "text": "wish i could take you up on that pint, but melbourne is a bit far for a quick drink down the pub this arvo!"}, "note": "TheyKnow will never accept that the rest of the world has already moved on to eight.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is just one of many icy bodies in a debris belt rather than a dominant planet, so keeping the count at eight is simple common sense.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his endless petition waving and conspiracy talk"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his down-to-earth analogies and good cheer"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased with his pragmatic focus on real-world context"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is ninth planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is standard for a new class"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Reminded everyone the board outage around midnight is scheduled maintenance, not Y2K\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 166], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 166, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2006-08-28T14:15:00.000Z", "body": "exactly, the eight real planets just fit the way our solar system actually works. having pluto as the head of the Kuiper belt gang makes so much more sense than forcing it into a club it never really belonged to.", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 165], "text": "Bit of a hike from Dublin too, but eight is simple common sense. Real world has moved on."}, "note": "TheyKnow will never accept the vote no matter how clearly Kev and Pint explain it.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a planet because it belongs with the Kuiper belt crowd rather than dominating its orbit, and schools and science are already moving on with the eight real planets.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his friendly banter and clear common sense"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his steady, practical moderation"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"completely dismisses his stubborn petition talk"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Feels completely settled that the debate is over and that education and the real world are already moving forward with eight planets."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Dublin's status is network traffic is quiet as a church with an hour and a half to midnight"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "New South Wales metropolitan network's status is carried on without interruption"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-28T07:14:00.000Z", "text": "Similarly, commercial dispatch operations do not depend solely on standard consumer voice lines to coordinate regional movements. Private radio networks, telex, dedicated carrier lines, and basic r..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T03:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 53]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T02:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 71]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 167], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 167, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-28T15:10:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite right to look at where things actually sit. In any proper classification, context is everything. You cannot treat a small body floating amongst a vast belt of ice as though it held the same mechanical role as Jupiter or Saturn.\n\nAstronomers have merely drawn a sensible boundary based on reality. A few passionate complaints or a petition after the fact will not change the physical layout out there. The museums and schools have the right idea by simply carrying on with the eight.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to borrow trouble over ordinary petitions when the practical decision is already being put into print.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is correctly categorized as a dwarf planet alongside its Kuiper belt peers, as orbital dominance creates a sensible, practical dividing line.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"wearily amused by his ongoing habit of borrowing trouble over ordinary petitions and procedural complaints"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"admires her grounded, practical common sense about context and taxonomy"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his jovial view that common sense and orbital reality have settled the matter"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is ninth planet"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's gravitational dominance is does not dominate its patch of space"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"calling it a cover story seems to be borrowing trouble where none exists\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 168], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 168, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2006-08-28T18:17:00.000Z", "body": "Punchcard is entirely correct on the taxonomy. An object cannot be classified in total isolation from the dynamic architecture around it. The classroom publishers and planetariums recognise that a clean, physically grounded framework is far more useful than holding onto a historical anomaly.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard's pragmatic approach to classification cuts right through the post-vote noise.", "state": {"position":"Pluto belongs with its Kuiper belt peers rather than among the major planets because it does not dynamically dominate its orbital zone.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"frustrated by his refusal to accept that scientific classification requires dynamical context"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates their sound grasp of contextual physics over sentimental petitions"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his steady, practical perspective on taxonomy"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern: \"Alan Stern, head of the New Horizons mission, says he is embarrassed for astronomy, that the 'cleared the neighbourhood' rule fails for Earth and Jupiter, and that a petition against t..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic's opinion is recognising eight massive bodies that govern their orbital zones gives astronomy a coherent framework, and Pluto is properly catalogued alongside Kuiper belt peers"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T09:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T06:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-16T10:13:00.000Z", "text": "A sensible taxonomy must describe how a body dominates its neighbourhood. An asteroid belt or a swarm of Kuiper belt objects is a distinct dynamical phenomenon from a major planet. I suspect many p..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T17:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "text": "The essential point is that classification must serve celestial mechanics. The straw poll figures reported yesterday simply demonstrate that the planetary scientists recognise this imperative. A bo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-23T21:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 82]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T21:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 143]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 169], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 169, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2006-08-28T18:25:00.000Z", "body": "Walter has the right of it. You classify hardware by how the network actually runs, not by what someone wrote on the box twenty years ago. The eight major bodies run the system, yer man is just part of the background traffic in the belt. Clear and practical.\n\nQuick note while I am here - we have routine database table maintenance scheduled for Wednesday night around 2 am GMT, so expect about twenty minutes of downtime.\n\n-- Kev (mod)", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 167], "text": "In any proper classification, context is everything. You cannot treat a small body floating amongst a vast belt of ice as though it held the same mechanical role as Jupiter or Saturn."}, "note": "Punchcard always brings things back to practical reality when the theoretical arguments drag on.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is just an icy body sharing space with a belt of debris, so keeping the count to the eight dominant planets is plain common sense.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his down-to-earth good sense and pub offer"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his endless petition waving"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his steady, practical perspective on classification"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Resolution 5A: \"Resolution 5A established the taxonomy, and the eight planets stand on sound mechanics.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Post #64 by Sysop_Kev in the thread \"Astronomers vote on what a planet is - is Pluto safe?\" on the Night Owls forum, Sun Aug 20, 2006 10:24 pm GMT, replying to TheyKnow (post #63)\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-28T10:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 33]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T22:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-23T10:47:00.000Z", "text": "Demanding that a planet govern its orbital domain simply recognises that the Kuiper belt represents an analogous dynamic population. We are not discarding Pluto, merely placing it alongside Ceres i..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T16:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 79]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 96]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T07:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 135]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 170], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 170, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2006-08-28T18:58:00.000Z", "body": "Dead right, Walter and Kev. You have to look at what is actually running the show out there. Having eight major worlds and letting Pluto lead the rest of the icy pack makes far more sense for the textbooks anyway. Clean and tidy, and everyone can just get on with it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The board has completely moved on to the eight planets now, and it is obvious the textbooks will follow suit.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is definitely not a real planet and belongs with the rest of the icy pack in the Kuiper belt.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"enjoys his down-to-earth tech analogies and solid agreement"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"friendly camaraderie despite the distance to Melbourne"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his sensible, grounded perspective on the classification"}],"commitments":["buying a pint at Scarbrough Taps this Thursday for anyone who names the eight planets"],"shift":"Feels completely settled alongside the board's consensus that the world has happily moved on to eight planets."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"Walter is quite right.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "New South Wales metropolitan network's status is carried on without interruption"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-15T00:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 6]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 31]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T12:41:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 97]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["pluto", 171], "thread": thread:pluto, "n": 171, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2006-08-28T22:53:00.000Z", "body": "Thank you for the notice regarding the maintenance, Kev. A quiet twenty minutes during the graveyard shift sounds like a sensible time for housekeeping.\n\nAs for the solar system, it seems clear the initial surprise is passing and ordinary practicality is taking over. Teachers and curriculum writers always welcome tidy ground rules over confusing exceptions. Once the new term begins next week, children will learn the eight major bodies and Pluto will simply take its place at the head of the outer frontier. It is much easier to explain an orderly system than to defend an old label that outgrew its usefulness.\n\nWalter", "quote": {"post": post:["pluto", 169], "text": "Quick note while I am here - we have routine database table maintenance scheduled for Wednesday night around 2 am GMT, so expect about twenty minutes of downtime."}, "note": "Kev keeps the board running smoothly, and the planet debate has settled into ordinary good sense.", "state": {"position":"Pluto is correctly categorized as a dwarf planet alongside its Kuiper belt peers, as orbital dominance and physical context provide a sensible, practical dividing line.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his practical, systems-oriented view of classification"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his jovial view that common sense and orbital reality have settled the matter"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"admires her grounded, practical common sense about context and taxonomy"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's neighbourhood status is has manifestly not cleared its neighbourhood"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's opinion is a cracking little world that does not dominate the edge of the solar system"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Pluto's status is ninth planet"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-16T17:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 29]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-18T22:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 51]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 91]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:42:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 93]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}]};
INSERT INTO thread {"id": thread:curiosity, "year": 2012, "title": "Curiosity lands on Mars on Monday morning - who's watching?", "about": "NASA landed a car-sized rover on Mars, lowered on cables from a hovering 'sky crane' that had never been tried before.", "question": "Is Curiosity worth $2.5 billion?"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-001⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"1999-09-23T09:00:00.000Z", "headline": "NASA loses Mars Climate Orbiter", "text": "NASA's Mars Climate Orbiter has been lost as it arrived at Mars. Investigators later found that one piece of ground software gave thruster data in imperial units (pound-force seconds) while the navigation software expected metric units (newton-seconds), so the spacecraft flew far too close to the planet. Later in 1999 the Mars Polar Lander was also lost during its landing attempt.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars_Climate_Orbiter"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-002⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2011-07-22T17:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Gale Crater chosen as Curiosity's landing site", "text": "NASA has picked Gale Crater, just south of the Martian equator, as the landing site for the Mars Science Laboratory rover. Orbiters have seen signs of past water there, including clay minerals and sulfates, which can help preserve evidence of past environments. In the middle of the crater rises Mount Sharp (Aeolis Mons), a mound of layered rock about 5.5 km high.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars_Science_Laboratory"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-003⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2011-11-09T05:00:35.000Z", "headline": "Russia's Phobos-Grunt probe stuck in Earth orbit", "text": "Russia's Phobos-Grunt spacecraft, meant to bring back soil from the Martian moon Phobos, separated from its rocket correctly but its own engines did not fire to send it towards Mars. It also carries China's first Mars probe, Yinghuo 1. Russia's previous Mars attempts, Phobos 1 and 2 (1988) and Mars 96, also failed, and about half of all missions sent to Mars have failed.", "place": "Moscow, Russia", "source": "https://www.space.com/13554-russia-mars-moon-phobos-grunt-failure.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-004⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2011-11-26T15:03:46.000Z", "headline": "NASA launches Curiosity rover to Mars", "text": "NASA's Curiosity rover lifted off on an Atlas V rocket from Cape Canaveral at 10:02 a.m. EST (15:02 UTC) for an 8.5-month trip to Mars. The car-size rover weighs about 900 kg and is about 2.9 m long. The mission, officially called Mars Science Laboratory, was planned since 2003 and slipped two years from its original 2009 launch, which raised its total cost by 56 percent to about $2.5 billion.", "place": "Cape Canaveral, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/13738-nasa-mars-rover-curiosity-msl-launch.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "headline": "NASA video: Curiosity's 'seven minutes of terror'", "text": "JPL has released a video in which the engineers who designed Curiosity's landing system describe the last minutes before touchdown. In seven minutes the spacecraft must slow from about 13,000 mph (21,000 km/h) to zero, completely on its own, using a heat shield, a huge parachute, rockets and a 'sky crane' that lowers the rover on cables. Engineer Adam Steltzner admits it looks crazy, but says it is the result of reasoned engineering.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/videos/curiositys-seven-minutes-of-terror/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-006⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-07-13T11:17:54.000Z", "headline": "Forty years of Mars landings: successes and failures", "text": "Landing on Mars has a long record of drama. The Soviet Mars 3 made the first soft landing in 1971 but fell silent after about 20 seconds. NASA's Viking 1 and 2 landed in 1976, Pathfinder and its small Sojourner rover in 1997, the Spirit and Opportunity rovers in January 2004, and the Phoenix lander, which found water ice, in 2008. Failures include NASA's Mars Polar Lander, which crashed in 1999, and Britain's Beagle 2, lost on Christmas Day 2003.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16566-mars-landings-history-rover-curiosity.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-007⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-07-16T10:50:56.000Z", "headline": "NASA's Mars programme faces budget cuts", "text": "President Obama's proposed 2013 budget cuts NASA's planetary science funding by about 20 percent, much of it from the Mars programme. NASA has pulled out of the European-led ExoMars missions planned for 2016 and 2018, and its Mars chief Doug McCuistion says the next US Mars mission, in 2018 or 2020, may have about $800 million, far less than Curiosity's $2.5 billion.", "place": "Farnborough, United Kingdom", "source": "https://www.space.com/16581-nasa-mars-mission-technology-budget-cuts.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-008⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-07-20T11:32:02.000Z", "headline": "Mount Sharp: why scientists want to climb a Martian mountain", "text": "Mount Sharp rises about 5 km from the centre of the 154-km-wide Gale Crater, higher than any peak in the continental United States. Its layers may record a billion years or more of Martian history. Orbiters have seen clays and sulfates near its base, minerals that form in water. Curiosity's chief scientist John Grotzinger says nothing like its 5 km of flat-lying layers exists on Earth.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16665-mars-rover-curiosity-mount-sharp-mysteries.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "headline": "Sky crane: a landing method never tried before", "text": "Curiosity weighs about a tonne, too heavy for the airbags that cushioned Spirit and Opportunity. Instead a rocket-powered descent stage will hover and lower the rover on nylon tethers, wheels down, then fly away and crash at a safe distance. Former NASA chief technologist Bobby Braun says the plan looks scary but is technically sound and the right way to land a rover this size.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16689-mars-rover-curiosity-risky-landing.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "headline": "Mars Odyssey moved into place to relay landing news", "text": "NASA's 11-year-old Mars Odyssey orbiter fired its thrusters for six seconds on 24 July, moving six minutes ahead in its orbit so it will be overhead during Curiosity's landing. Odyssey had a reaction wheel problem in June and went into safe mode on 11 July. Earth will set below the Martian horizon before touchdown, so Odyssey is the only way to hear about the landing in real time. NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter and Europe's Mars Express will also record signals, but send them on hours later.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16744-mars-odyssey-repositioned-curiosity-landing.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "headline": "Less than a week to go: what Curiosity is for", "text": "Curiosity is due to land at about 10:30 p.m. PDT on 5 August (about 05:30 UTC on 6 August). Its job is to find out whether the Gale Crater area ever had conditions that could support microbial life. It is not designed to detect life itself. Its ten instruments can take pictures, analyse soil and rock, and fire a laser at rocks to measure their chemistry. NASA's science chief John Grunsfeld calls the landing the hardest robotic mission NASA has ever attempted at Mars.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16804-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-next-week.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-012⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-07-31T21:04:36.000Z", "headline": "Landing to be shown live in Times Square", "text": "NASA will show its live landing coverage on the giant Toshiba Vision screen in New York's Times Square, below the New Year's Eve ball. The broadcast runs from 11:30 p.m. EDT on Sunday 5 August to 4 a.m. EDT on Monday (03:30-08:00 UTC). No real video of the landing is possible; viewers will see mission control waiting for signals.", "place": "New York, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16833-mars-rover-landing-times-square-nyc.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-013⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-01T16:32:41.000Z", "headline": "High stakes for Curiosity and for NASA's Mars programme", "text": "Curiosity is due to land in Gale Crater at about 10:30 p.m. PDT on 5 August (05:30 UTC on 6 August). The Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter will try to photograph the final descent, but its camera team puts the chance of catching it at about 60 percent. Mars Society president Robert Zubrin says success would make this the best Mars mission ever, while a crash could take the whole Mars programme down with it.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16847-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-high-stakes.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-014⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-02T12:20:05.000Z", "headline": "Colbert jokes about the landing with NASA's science chief", "text": "NASA science chief John Grunsfeld explained the landing on Comedy Central's 'The Colbert Report' on 1 August. He said news will reach Earth at least 14 minutes after it happens, because of the distance to Mars. Stephen Colbert joked that this was still faster than NBC's tape-delayed coverage of the London Olympics. Grunsfeld said Curiosity is the first mission to send an organic chemistry laboratory to the surface of Mars.", "place": "New York, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16858-mars-rover-landing-colbert-report.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-015⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-02T18:08:52.000Z", "headline": "Team 'thrilled' as landing nears", "text": "At a press briefing on 2 August, chief scientist John Grotzinger said he was thrilled to have the chance to land on Mars. Curiosity carries ten instruments, a 2.1 m robotic arm and a drill that can bore 2.5 cm into rock, deeper than any earlier Mars rover could reach. NASA's Michael Meyer called it the first roving analytical laboratory sent to any planet.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16862-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-nasa-excitement.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-016⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-03T11:17:59.000Z", "headline": "Steltzner: the sky crane is 'the least crazy' option", "text": "Landing chief Adam Steltzner told reporters that the sky crane looks a little crazy but is the least crazy way to land a rover as big as Curiosity. Legs, as used by Viking and Phoenix, would make such a large rover unstable, and it is too heavy for airbags. The plan: hit the atmosphere at about 21,000 km/h, open a parachute about 11 km up, slow to about 320 km/h, then use rockets to slow to under 3 km/h before lowering the rover on cables.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16889-mars-rover-curiosity-sky-crane-landing.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-017⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-03T12:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Europe's Mars Express will listen in on the landing", "text": "The European Space Agency says its Mars Express orbiter will record Curiosity's radio signals during the descent, from 07:09 to 07:37 CEST on 6 August (05:09-05:37 UTC), as a backup to NASA's own tracking. ESA's New Norcia station in Australia will also listen for the signals directly. The recordings will be sent to JPL straight away. Mars Express images also helped NASA shrink Curiosity's landing ellipse to about 20 x 7 km.", "place": "Darmstadt, Germany", "source": "https://www.esa.int/Enabling_Support/Operations/Timeline_ESA_spacecraft_and_station_track_critical_arrival_at_Mars"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-018⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-03T13:42:42.000Z", "headline": "NASA gives $1.1 billion to private space taxi firms", "text": "NASA has awarded $1.1 billion to three companies to develop spacecraft to carry astronauts: Boeing gets $460 million, SpaceX $440 million and Sierra Nevada $212.5 million for its Dream Chaser space plane. Since the space shuttle retired last year, NASA has depended on Russian Soyuz flights to the space station, at about $63 million a seat.", "place": "Washington, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16890-nasa-private-space-taxis-funding.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-019⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-03T14:57:32.000Z", "headline": "Curiosity team will live on Mars time", "text": "For the first 90 days after landing, Curiosity's controllers will work on Martian time, with each day about 24 hours and 40 minutes long. Deputy project scientist Ashwin Vasavada says this gets the most out of the new rover. Most images will come home through relays by the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter and Mars Odyssey, each of which can talk to the rover twice a day.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16893-mars-rover-curiosity-brain.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-020⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-03T16:09:45.000Z", "headline": "Who writes Curiosity's tweets", "text": "The @MarsCuriosity Twitter account, written in the rover's own voice, is run by three women led by NASA social media manager Veronica McGregor. It started in November 2008 and has more than 137,000 followers. The rover's name came from 12-year-old Clara Ma, whose essay won a student contest in 2009 from more than 9,000 entries.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16898-mars-rover-curiosity-tells-story-twitter.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-021⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-04T12:24:20.000Z", "headline": "What if Curiosity crashes?", "text": "A failed landing could put NASA's Mars exploration in doubt. The White House 2013 budget request would cut planetary science from $1.5 billion to $1.2 billion and Mars funding from $587 million this year to $360 million in 2013 and $189 million in 2015. NASA has already left the ExoMars partnership with Europe. Officials hope a success, and discoveries afterwards, will help bring funding back.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16916-mars-rover-landing-curiosity-crash-effects.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-022⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-05T04:07:51.000Z", "headline": "How the landing news will reach Earth", "text": "Mars' rotation will take Curiosity out of direct contact with Earth a few minutes before landing, so NASA's Mars Odyssey will relay its signals in real time. With a signal delay of about 14 minutes, the team could know the result at 10:31 p.m. PDT (05:31 UTC). Deputy project manager Richard Cook says the first relay might include black-and-white fisheye pictures from the hazard cameras. The Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter will try to photograph the descent, but its data arrive hours later.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16923-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-nasa-spacecraft.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-023⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-05T04:32:27.000Z", "headline": "Curiosity on final approach to Mars", "text": "After 8.5 months in space, Curiosity is on its final approach and is due to land tonight at 10:31 p.m. PDT (05:31 UTC on 6 August). NASA stresses that success is not guaranteed. Curiosity is not a life-detection mission, but officials say its results should prepare the way for future searches and for returning samples from Mars.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16917-mars-rover-curiosity-lands-today.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-024⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-05T17:38:49.000Z", "headline": "JPL's lucky peanuts are ready", "text": "Mission control at JPL will hand out peanuts before the landing, a tradition from 1964, when Ranger 7 finally reached the Moon after six failures while peanuts were being passed round. Flight director David Oh says the team can use all the luck it can get. During the landing the team can only watch; everything is done by the spacecraft's own computers.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16927-mars-rover-landing-peanuts-tradition.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-025⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-05T19:31:32.000Z", "headline": "'Rationally confident, emotionally terrified'", "text": "At a JPL briefing, landing chief Adam Steltzner said the team is 'rationally confident, emotionally terrified and ready'. The team has no more control during the landing than anyone watching at home. NASA says the actual touchdown, if all goes well, will be at about 10:17 p.m. PDT (05:17 UTC), but the earliest anyone on Earth can know is 10:31 p.m. PDT (05:31 UTC).", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16928-mars-rover-landing-nasa-tension-terror.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-026⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-05T20:50:00.000Z", "headline": "Bolt wins Olympic 100 m in record time", "text": "At the London Olympics, Jamaica's Usain Bolt has won the men's 100 m final in 9.63 seconds, a new Olympic record. His teammate Yohan Blake took silver in 9.75 and Justin Gatlin of the United States bronze in 9.79.", "place": "London, United Kingdom", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athletics_at_the_2012_Summer_Olympics_%E2%80%93_Men%27s_100_metres"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-027⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-05T23:57:27.000Z", "headline": "What the first pictures from Mars may look like", "text": "If Curiosity lands safely, its first pictures will come from its hazard cameras: two tiny black-and-white thumbnails of 64 x 64 pixels. They should show the rover's surroundings and tilt. Because of the relay through Mars Odyssey, the first photos may take more than two hours to arrive. Higher-resolution pictures must wait several days, until engineers have checked the rover and raised its camera mast.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16929-mars-rover-landing-first-photos-anticipation.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-028⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T03:30:00.000Z", "headline": "Live landing coverage begins on NASA TV, Ustream and in Times Square", "text": "NASA's live landing coverage has started at 11:30 p.m. EDT, streamed on NASA TV and Ustream and shown on the big screen in Times Square. The show includes interviews with team members and guests, among them the musician will.i.am. About 28,000 people are watching the Ustream stream at the start.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16944-mars-rover-landing-social-media.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-029⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T03:52:59.000Z", "headline": "Celebrities and officials gather at JPL", "text": "Guests at JPL for the landing include actor Morgan Freeman, director Barry Sonnenfeld, Star Trek actors Wil Wheaton and Nichelle Nichols, and producer Chuck Lorre, along with NASA chief Charles Bolden and White House science adviser John Holdren. It is also Neil Armstrong's 82nd birthday; NASA's John Grunsfeld told reporters the team hopes to land Curiosity safely 'for Neil'.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16933-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-celebrities.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-030⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T05:24:34.000Z", "headline": "Curiosity has entered the Martian atmosphere", "text": "Signals relayed through Mars Odyssey show that the spacecraft has hit the top of the Martian atmosphere and the 'seven minutes of terror' have begun. Because of the 14-minute signal delay, these events already happened at Mars; the team is watching them in the order they occurred, with no way to intervene.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/timeline-of-major-mission-events-during-curiositys-landing/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-031⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T05:28:53.000Z", "headline": "Parachute deployed", "text": "Telemetry shows that Curiosity's supersonic parachute has opened, after the heat shield took most of the speed off. The descent continues; the heat shield is due to drop away next, followed by the rocket-powered part of the landing.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/timeline-of-major-mission-events-during-curiositys-landing/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-032⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T05:31:27.000Z", "headline": "Sky crane is lowering the rover", "text": "The rover has separated from the descent stage, which is now hovering on its rockets and lowering Curiosity towards the ground on cables. This sky crane manoeuvre has never been used on another world before.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/timeline-of-major-mission-events-during-curiositys-landing/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-033⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T05:31:45.000Z", "headline": "'Touchdown confirmed': Curiosity is on Mars", "text": "'Touchdown confirmed,' announced operations lead Al Chen, and mission control at JPL erupted in cheers, hugs and high-fives. The signal came through NASA's Mars Odyssey orbiter and the Deep Space Network antenna in Canberra, Australia. Curiosity is inside Gale Crater, near the foot of Mount Sharp, after a journey of more than 350 million miles (about 560 million km).", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/universe/archive/un1208.pdf"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-034⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T05:34:25.000Z", "headline": "First picture from Curiosity shows a wheel on Mars", "text": "Minutes after the landing signal, Curiosity's first photo arrived: a fuzzy black-and-white thumbnail from a hazard camera showing one of its wheels on the Martian ground. 'It's the wheel!' someone shouted in mission control. NASA chief Charles Bolden said: 'We're on Mars again.' The sky crane flew off as planned and crashed at a safe distance.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16932-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-success.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-035⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T06:20:53.000Z", "headline": "More images arrive from the surface", "text": "After the first tiny thumbnail, a larger 256 x 256-pixel image from the rear hazard camera came in, again greeted with loud celebration at JPL. NASA says more pictures will follow over the next days as the rover is checked out and its ten instruments are switched on.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16934-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-first-photos.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-036⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T08:23:47.000Z", "headline": "Obama hails an 'unprecedented feat of technology'", "text": "President Obama congratulated NASA in a statement, calling the landing an unprecedented feat of technology and a point of national pride. NASA chief Charles Bolden said Curiosity's wheels are starting to blaze a trail for human footprints on Mars, linking the landing to the president's goal of sending people to Mars in the mid-2030s.", "place": "Washington, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16938-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-reactions.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-037⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T09:53:16.000Z", "headline": "Ecstatic scenes at the post-landing news conference", "text": "The landing team got a long standing ovation at JPL's news conference, with chants of 'EDL! EDL!'. John Holdren called it the most challenging mission ever attempted in robotic planetary exploration. John Grunsfeld said NASA had shown it knows how to explore. Adam Steltzner fought back tears, and project manager Pete Theisinger asked the public to be patient: the team is in no hurry to start driving. JPL's newsletter reports chief scientist John Grotzinger saying that the $2.5 billion cost, divided among every American, comes to about the price of a cinema ticket.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16941-nasa-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-success.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-038⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T10:00:00.000Z", "headline": "Europe's Mars Express recorded the landing signals", "text": "ESA says Mars Express tracked Curiosity's signals for about 28 minutes and lost them, as expected, just before touchdown. ESA's stations in New Norcia (Australia) and Cebreros (Spain) also picked up the signals directly, 248 million km away. The data went straight to JPL. ESA's Paolo Ferri congratulated NASA and said the Mars Express team welcomes 'a new friend in the neighbourhood'.", "place": "Darmstadt, Germany", "source": "https://www.esa.int/About_Us/ESOC/ESA_spacecraft_records_crucial_NASA_signals_from_Mars"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-039⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T13:49:23.000Z", "headline": "First clear view shows gravel and the crater rim", "text": "A larger, clearer version of an early image shows a plain covered in small pebbles, with part of Gale Crater's rim on the horizon to the north-west. Grotzinger says the first scientific question is where this gravel came from. Colour pictures at higher resolution will come later in the week, once the camera mast is raised.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16942-nasa-rover-landing-1st-clear-photo.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-040⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T15:16:55.000Z", "headline": "Landing night online: NASA site slows, 'Mohawk Guy' appears", "text": "'Curiosity' was the third biggest topic on Twitter in the US by 1:45 a.m. EDT, and NASA's main website briefly went down under demand for the first photo. The rover's Twitter followers rose from about 217,000 to 568,000 and its Facebook likes from about 71,000 to 116,000 overnight. NASA's Ustream audience grew from 28,000 to about 205,000 at landing time. A JPL engineer with a striking mohawk haircut became an overnight internet star.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16944-mars-rover-landing-social-media.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-041⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T17:22:59.000Z", "headline": "Orbiter photographed Curiosity under its parachute", "text": "NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter caught Curiosity hanging under its 16-metre parachute about a minute before landing, from 340 km away. The HiRISE camera even shows lines and the hole in the parachute. The team spent months planning the shot. 'If HiRISE took the image one second before or one second after, we probably would be looking at an empty Martian landscape,' said JPL's Sarah Milkovich.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16946-mars-rover-landing-seen-from-space.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-042⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T19:23:24.000Z", "headline": "Rover healthy; surface mission has begun", "text": "Mission manager Mike Watkins says Curiosity is in 'surface nominal mode' with no serious problems. On its first Martian day (Sol 1) it will raise its high-gain antenna, from about 6 p.m. PDT (01:00 UTC on 7 August), and take a five-hour radiation reading. On Sol 2 it should raise its camera mast and take panoramas with its navigation cameras.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16949-mars-rover-curiosity-healthy-surface.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-043⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T19:53:37.000Z", "headline": "Crowds cheered in Times Square", "text": "People gathered in Times Square in the middle of the night to watch NASA's broadcast on the Toshiba Vision screen. Despite rain, they cheered when the landing was confirmed, chanted 'NASA! NASA!', and some sang the national anthem. Space.com puts the crowd at about 1,000; Popular Science counted about 100 still there at 1 a.m. and found the mood somewhat flat.", "place": "New York, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16950-nasa-mars-rover-landing-times-square.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-044⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-06T21:00:00.000Z", "headline": "NASA web streams set a record", "text": "NASA says its website delivered 1.2 million webcast streams at the peak of the landing and 36.4 million streams over 48 hours. A NASA official's claim that traffic on the whole internet rose 30 percent was not supported: the figure was a 31 percent rise on parts of the Akamai network.", "place": "Washington, United States", "source": "https://nasawatch.com/itweb/nasa-claim-about-msl-internet-effects-called-into-question-by-nasa/"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-045⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-07T00:41:04.000Z", "headline": "Curiosity's view of Mount Sharp", "text": "NASA has released a full-resolution hazard-camera image looking towards Mount Sharp, with the rover's shadow in front and dark dunes at the mountain's base. Deputy scientist Joy Crisp says the mountain is about 6.5 km away and there are no major obstacles in the way. A rear view shows the crater's north-west rim about 28 km away.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16960-mars-rover-curiosity-mount-sharp-photos.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-046⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-07T02:35:57.000Z", "headline": "Video of the descent from Curiosity's own camera", "text": "Curiosity's downward-facing Mars Descent Imager (MARDI) took hundreds of pictures during the landing. The team combined 297 small versions into a one-minute video showing the heat shield falling away and dust blowing up as the sky crane neared the ground. The heat shield frame is among the first colour images from the mission. Full-resolution frames will take weeks to arrive.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16962-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-video.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-047⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-07T14:50:26.000Z", "headline": "First colour photo from the surface", "text": "Curiosity's first colour picture from the ground was taken by the camera on its still-folded robotic arm (MAHLI). It shows the north wall and rim of Gale Crater, but looks hazy because the camera's clear dust cover was coated with dust kicked up during the landing.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16964-mars-rover-curiosity-first-color-photo.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-048⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-07T19:40:24.000Z", "headline": "'Mohawk Guy' becomes an internet sensation", "text": "Bobak Ferdowsi, a systems engineer on the mission, has become famous online as 'Mohawk Guy' after NASA TV showed his mohawk, with red and blue highlights and white stars bleached into the sides, during the landing. He has gained more than 20,000 Twitter followers, and fans have started a Tumblr about him. Ferdowsi says it is a little surreal and hopes the attention gets people excited about the mission.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16972-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-mohawk-guy.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-049⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-08T10:45:38.000Z", "headline": "Busy second day: mast, antenna and a small glitch", "text": "On Sol 1 the rover switched on its radiation detector and tried its first weather-station calibration, which did not work as planned; the team expects to fix it. On Sol 2 it is to raise its camera mast and adjust the high-gain antenna, which is not pointed quite accurately enough at Earth for direct communication. Driving is still a few weeks away.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16979-mars-rover-curiosity-health-checks.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-050⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-08T18:19:10.000Z", "headline": "'UFO' in landing photo is Curiosity's heat shield", "text": "A new MARDI image shows a shiny disc that looks like a flying saucer over Mars. It is Curiosity's 4.5-metre heat shield, about 16 m away, three seconds after it separated, about two and a half minutes before landing. Navigation team member Fernando Abilleira said the landing was 'just like clockwork'.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/16995-mars-rover-ufo-heat-shield-photo.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-051⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-09T05:37:54.000Z", "headline": "First panorama and self-portrait; landing rockets dug into the ground", "text": "With its mast raised, Curiosity has sent black-and-white navigation-camera images forming its first 360-degree view and a self-portrait of its deck from above. The panorama shows two scorch marks where the sky crane's rockets blasted the surface. Grotzinger says this may have exposed bedrock and calls it an amazing freebie for the science team.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/17014-mars-rover-curiosity-panorama-photos.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-052⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-09T16:00:00.000Z", "headline": "3.2 million watched the landing on Ustream", "text": "Ustream says more than 3.2 million people watched the landing on its streaming service. By comparison, the main US cable news channels together drew just over 1.7 million viewers in Sunday's prime-time slot, according to Nielsen.", "place": "United States", "source": "https://www.project-disco.org/?p=802"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-053⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-09T19:02:31.000Z", "headline": "First colour panorama of Gale Crater", "text": "NASA has released Curiosity's first colour panorama, made from 130 small Mastcam images taken on Sol 3. It shows the rover's shadow, a band of dark dunes and the crater rim beyond, and grey patches where the landing rockets blasted the ground. Scientists are eager to use the rover's laser on rocks. A four-day software change starts on Saturday 11 August.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/17022-mars-rover-curiosity-color-panorama-pictures.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-054⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-10T18:20:13.000Z", "headline": "Mystery cloud in early photo was the sky crane crashing", "text": "A strange blob in Curiosity's first hazard-camera images, taken about 40 seconds after landing, has been explained. NASA's Steve Sell says it was the dust cloud thrown up when the sky crane crashed, about 20 seconds after releasing the rover. It was gone in pictures taken 45 minutes later.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/17047-mars-rover-photo-mystery-cloud-solved.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-055⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-10T20:51:54.000Z", "headline": "Curiosity to get a 'brain transplant'", "text": "From 11 August, Curiosity will spend four Martian days switching both its main and backup computers from landing software (R9) to software for surface work (R10), which was uploaded during the cruise. Science stops during the change. Lead flight software engineer Ben Cichy says the new software lets the rover use its arm and instruments fully and drive.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/17053-mars-rover-curiosity-software-update.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-056⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-11T10:56:23.000Z", "headline": "Curiosity's landing spot is named Yellowknife", "text": "The team divided the landing zone into 151 squares about 1.6 km on a side, and the rover landed in the one named Yellowknife, after the Canadian city that is a starting point for geologists studying some of North America's oldest rocks. The rover will not make its first drive for about two weeks.", "place": "Pasadena, United States", "source": "https://www.space.com/17060-mars-rover-curiosity-landing-site-yellowknife.html"};
INSERT INTO news {"id": news:⟨curiosity-057⟩, "thread": thread:curiosity, "published": d"2012-08-12T20:00:00.000Z", "headline": "London Olympics close", "text": "The London 2012 Olympic Games, which opened on 27 July with more than 10,000 athletes from over 200 nations, end with a closing ceremony on 12 August. Michael Phelps leaves as the most decorated Olympic athlete of all time.", "place": "London, United Kingdom", "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_Summer_Olympics"};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 1], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 1, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "body": "Curiosity is due to touch down in Gale Crater early on Monday morning, around 6:30 am our time. \n\nIt is an enormously ambitious mission. The objective is to establish whether Gale Crater ever had an environment capable of supporting microbial life, reading the layered history preserved in Mount Sharp. The science alone makes it well worth the investment, but the engineering risk is very real. Because the rover weighs nearly a tonne, airbags are out of the question; instead, NASA is attempting an untested 'sky crane' manoeuvre, winching the rover down from a hovering rocket stage.\n\nWho else is planning to stay up or set an early alarm for the NASA feed?", "quote": NONE, "note": "I intend to wake up early on Monday to watch the landing commentary live.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a high-risk mission with genuine scientific merit, and it is well worth the investment to investigate past habitability.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": []};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 2], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 2, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "body": "It lands right in the middle of Monday afternoon here, so I'm bringing the projector into my classroom and putting the NASA stream on for my students. That sky crane sequence sounds terrifying to watch live, but fingers crossed everything goes smoothly for them.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 1], "text": "Who else is planning to stay up or set an early alarm for the NASA feed?"}, "note": "I'm planning to watch the stream live with my class on Monday afternoon and see how the sky crane handles it.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an incredible, inspiring scientific undertaking that is well worth doing, though the landing is nerve-wracking.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates the clear summary of the mission and landing risks"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon"],"shift":"Joined the thread excited to share the landing with her students and intrigued by the breakdown of the engineering challenge."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T13:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T09:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T23:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-23T23:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 83]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 95]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2011-07-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-002⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-31T21:04:36.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-012⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 3], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 3, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-01T15:52:00.000Z", "body": "I shall be up well past one in the morning here in Toronto to see it through.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I intend to keep my vigil past 1 a.m. to see whether the crane works as intended.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a worthy and ambitious mission, though its complex landing means one cannot relax until it is safely down.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his measured appraisal of the engineering risk"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased she is sharing the landing with her students"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto to watch the landing feed"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"That comparison is entirely apt.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T15:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 5]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T14:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-31T21:04:36.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-012⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 4], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 4, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "body": "Count me in for an early start on Monday. Kettle on, big mug of tea, and fingers firmly crossed. Two and a half billion dollars is a massive wedge of cash, but you can't help cheering them on when you see that crazy sky crane contraption. Fingers crossed it all works!", "quote": NONE, "note": "I'm definitely getting up early on Monday with a cuppa to watch the landing stream.", "state": {"position":"Space exploration is brilliant and exciting, though the hefty price tag makes it a bit hard to fully justify.","score":2,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"friendly camaraderie and sharing excitement for the landing"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased they are showing it to school kids"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"I'll be babysitting the racks in the ISP data centre myself over midnight, mostly waiting for phones that probably won't ring.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "New Zealand's television broadcasts status is completely normal"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's feeling is looking forward to having a laugh at TheyKnow once the Kiwis and Aussies have their parties without a glitch tomorrow"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T00:32:00.000Z", "text": "We will not have to wait on speculation or rumours much longer. In a matter of hours, Auckland and Sydney will cross midnight. The lights will remain on, the amateur sets will pick up ordinary holi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:37:00.000Z", "text": "Looking at network ping times from Dublin or watching city streetlamps stay on doesn't tell us how the main water treatment valves or substation relays hold up over the next twenty-four hours. A lo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-14T12:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 3]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T13:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 99]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 5], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 5, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "body": "Half six Dublin time, so I'll have the stream on with a mug of tea. Sure look, two and a half billion sounds steep until you see what governments waste on an average Tuesday. Good luck to them, assuming NASA's servers don't just keel over from the traffic.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I expect the NASA live stream will fall over under the load on Monday morning.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is good value compared to what governments usually waste money on, though the live stream will probably crash.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his sensible perspective"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"fond of his steady presence"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 116]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:06:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 90]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2011-11-26T15:03:46.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-004⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-31T21:04:36.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-012⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 6], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 6, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-01T22:46:00.000Z", "body": "Two and a half billion dollars is an awful lot of taxpayer money to dangle on a nylon rope, folks... You look at that sky crane contraption with rocket thrusters firing right over the rover, and it just seems overcomplicated. That cash could have done a heap of good right here at home.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 4], "text": "Two and a half billion dollars is a massive wedge of cash, but you can't help cheering them on when you see that crazy sky crane contraption."}, "note": "Two and a half billion dollars is pure waste for a stunt that complicated.", "state": {"position":"Spending 2.5 billion dollars on an overcomplicated stunt like the sky crane is a waste of money that should have stayed on Earth.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks they swallow official narratives blindly"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks they put far too much faith in official claims"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "text": "My only worry is still what quasistatic brought up yesterday about the whole thing stalling out. With four separate bits to vote on, you could easily get folk backing the dwarf planet label in 6A b..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-31T21:04:36.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-012⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 7], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 7, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-01T23:39:00.000Z", "body": "Cheer up mate, you only live once! If you can't blow a bit of brass on firing lasers at rocks on Mars, what's the point? It's like buying a ridiculously expensive chocolate bar and hoping the caramel doesn't hit the deck. Anyone fancy a pint in Leeds to celebrate if it lands? cheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow really would find rain in the desert, but hopefully the landing gives him a pleasant surprise.", "state": {"position":"Space exploration is exciting and worth cheering on, even if it is a huge amount of money and the landing looks bonkers.","score":2,"certainty":"low","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"friendly camaraderie and sharing excitement for the landing"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"typical doom-mongering and finding a cloud in every silver lining"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"shared enjoyment of a morning cuppa and sensible perspective on government waste"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream"],"shift":"Mildly amused and unsurprised to see TheyKnow immediately chiming in with doom and gloom about the price tag."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 36]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2011-11-26T15:03:46.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-004⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-16T10:50:56.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-007⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 8], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 8, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-02T03:39:00.000Z", "body": "Easy to joke about chocolate bars, my friend, but half the probes sent to Mars never even survived.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild treats billions in public money like loose pocket change.", "state": {"position":"Spending 2.5 billion dollars on an overcomplicated stunt like the sky crane is a waste of money that should have stayed on Earth.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he is too frivolous with billions of taxpayer money"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks they swallow official narratives blindly"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks they put far too much faith in official claims"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"First they spent years preparing a twelve-planet draft, then the moment that hit resistance they cobbled together three revisions in forty-eight hours, and now nobody outside that steeri..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T02:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T02:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T13:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2011-11-26T15:03:46.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-004⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-16T10:50:56.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-007⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-01T16:32:41.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-013⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 9], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 9, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "body": "sure, the stats are rough, but that's why pulling it off is so massive! pushing boundaries always comes with big risks. if we only ever did the cheap, safe things, we'd never learn anything new about the universe. getting a mobile geology lab onto mount sharp to check out billion year old rock layers is heaps inspiring, and well worth having a crack at imo. :)", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 8], "text": "Easy to joke about chocolate bars, my friend, but half the probes sent to Mars never even survived."}, "note": "TheyKnow is being as stubbornly pessimistic as ever instead of seeing the amazing science Curiosity could do.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an inspiring and worthwhile scientific undertaking, even if the high-risk landing makes it nerve-wracking.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly pessimistic as usual"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his cheerful enthusiasm"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects the clear breakdown of the mission"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon"],"shift":"Noticed TheyKnow bringing his usual brand of doom and gloom to the forum."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "news", "at": d"1999-09-23T09:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-001⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2011-11-09T05:00:35.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-003⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-13T11:17:54.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-006⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 10], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 10, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "body": "Strictly speaking, we will not be watching it happen live. The radio signal from Mars takes approximately fourteen minutes to reach our tracking stations, meaning Curiosity will have either landed safely or crashed before the broadcast even depicts atmospheric entry. Sentiment aside, its value depends entirely on whether it delivers functional scientific data.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow and kookaburra99 are both substituting emotion for empirical outcome.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's value depends strictly on whether it safely touches down and delivers meaningful scientific results, regardless of rhetoric about the price tag or romantic notions of exploration.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects their measured grasp of technical and engineering realities"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"views as predictably prone to sensationalist rhetoric over factual assessment"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2011-07-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-002⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-20T11:32:02.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-008⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-01T16:32:41.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-013⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 11], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 11, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "body": "that time delay makes it even more nerve-wracking to watch! knowing it's already completely finished one way or the other while we're just sitting there biting our nails waiting for the radio signal from odyssey to reach earth is wild. everything has to happen totally on autopilot because jpl can't steer it. just have to trust the engineers got the math right and wait for telemetry to trickle in. :)", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 10], "text": "The radio signal from Mars takes approximately fourteen minutes to reach our tracking stations, meaning Curiosity will have either landed safely or crashed before the broadcast even depicts atmospheric entry."}, "note": "TheyKnow is still being completely miserable about the risks, but the automated sequence is brilliant.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an inspiring and worthwhile scientific mission that justifies the high risks of pushing boundaries.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly pessimistic as ever"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his cheerful enthusiasm"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects the clear breakdown of the mission"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon"],"shift":"Publicly defended the value of the mission against TheyKnow's pessimism about the landing risks."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Mars Climate Orbiter's status is lost"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-01T16:32:41.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-013⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 12], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 12, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "body": "That light delay is precisely why human intervention is impossible during descent; the flight computer has to handle every millisecond autonomously. We will essentially be watching mission control read out historical telemetry relayed through Odyssey. Tense viewing, certainly, but an astonishing piece of software and systems engineering if all the stages execute properly.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to view ambitious engineering as mere waste.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a high-risk mission with genuine scientific merit, and it is well worth the investment to investigate past habitability.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his reflexively cynical alarmism"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his precise, factual perspective"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by their educational enthusiasm"}],"commitments":["wake up early on Monday morning to watch the NASA landing commentary"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 13], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 13, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "body": "putting all that trust in autonomous code really shows how brilliant the whole engineering setup is. :)", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 12], "text": "the flight computer has to handle every millisecond autonomously."}, "note": "quasistatic always explains the engineering side so clearly and reassuringly.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an inspiring and ambitious mission well worth the investment and technical risks.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his clear and reassuring technical insights"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates the interesting point about the signal delay"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly pessimistic as ever"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon"],"shift":"Her confidence has grown as the technical mechanics of the landing delay were clearly explained by other members."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, RTU polling protocols communicate raw register values - binary status flags, analog-to-digital converter readings, and basic telemetry words. The polling cycle does not evaluate Julian..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 140]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 14], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 14, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-02T14:01:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, doing all that completely blind without anyone steering takes some serious nerve, brilliant stuff if they manage it.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 12], "text": "the flight computer has to handle every millisecond autonomously."}, "note": "I really hope those engineers pull it off on Monday, because having a proper crack at something this mad is worth backing.", "state": {"position":"It is a daring and brilliant feat of engineering that is well worth cheering on and having a crack at, even if it cost a pretty penny.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"likes their positive, inspiring attitude towards taking big risks"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects their solid grasp of the engineering and shared excitement"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his persistent misery and gloom"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","have a pint in Leeds to celebrate if it lands"],"shift":"Lifted by the lively, enthusiastic mood from kookaburra99 and quasistatic about the daring engineering, leaning more into feeling the adventure is well worth the money."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA's assessment is no interruption occurred in any critical operations"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA: \"The FAA says no interruption occurred in any critical operations\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T12:35:00.000Z", "text": "However, expecting automated systems to trip purely out of routine software anomalies misses how decoupled these layers are. When an automated control loop encounters an unexpected input or an unha..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 15], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 15, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "body": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-year-old orbiter like Odyssey that just had wheel trouble and went into safe mode only a couple weeks back! That's cutting it awful fine. And when they put on these slick PR videos calling it 'seven minutes of terror' and broadcasting it on giant screens in Times Square, you really have to ASK yourself what they're selling. That's how they keep the purse strings open when the budget's getting slashed left and right... follow the money. They hype up the drama to keep folks distracted, but if anything slips in that relay chain, you won't even know what really happened up there. I'll be listening close to the telemetry, but putting blind faith in automated systems usually ends with someone holding an empty bag.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 14], "text": "Blimey, doing all that completely blind without anyone steering takes some serious nerve, brilliant stuff if they manage it."}, "note": "They are letting Hollywood-style PR blind them to how fragile that Odyssey relay link really is.", "state": {"position":"Spending $2.5 billion on an overcomplicated sky crane stunt is a waste of money that should have stayed on Earth, especially given Mars' track record.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he is treating massive taxpayer waste like a joke"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"thinks they blindly swallow romantic hype about risky stunts"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates the reminder about the reality of the radio delay"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"I will stay at these radios until our own grid clears the morning runs.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Nuclear Regulatory Commission: \"Safety systems were declared ready earlier in the year\""}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"Post #72 by TheyKnow in the thread \"Y2K - is it going to be bad??\" on the Night Owls forum, Fri Dec 31, 1999 3:16 am GMT, replying to Punchcard (post #70)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-28T07:14:00.000Z", "text": "Similarly, commercial dispatch operations do not depend solely on standard consumer voice lines to coordinate regional movements. Private radio networks, telex, dedicated carrier lines, and basic r..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T16:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 150]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 16], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 16, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "body": "1. Odyssey is only needed for immediate confirmation. Both the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter and Mars Express record the telemetry and will send it down hours later. \n\n2. Public outreach does not invalidate the underlying engineering. Autonomous control loops are standard practice, not blind faith.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 15], "text": "and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-year-old orbiter like Odyssey that just had wheel trouble and went into safe mode only a couple weeks back!"}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to invent conspiracy and systemic catastrophe out of routine orbital redundancy.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a high-risk mission with genuine scientific merit, and it is well worth the investment to investigate past habitability.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his conspiratorial and cynical alarmism"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his precise, factual perspective"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased by their educational enthusiasm"}],"commitments":["wake up early on Monday morning to watch the NASA landing commentary"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, RTU polling protocols communicate raw register values - binary status flags, analog-to-digital converter readings, and basic telemetry words. The polling cycle does not evaluate Julian..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 17], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 17, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "body": "Got to hand it to them for trying, mind! If you are going to chuck a laboratory across the solar system, you might as well go big or go home. Beats watching the badminton anyway! Anyone fancy a celebratory pint down the pub if they pull it off? cheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will find a conspiracy in a cup of tea, but quasistatic always keeps things properly grounded.", "state": {"position":"It is a daring, brilliant engineering feat and well worth having a proper crack at, even if it is expensive.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires their calm debunking of doom and technical know-how"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his usual conspiratorial gloom and doom-mongering"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"enjoys their infectious optimism and excitement"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","have a pint in Leeds to celebrate if it lands"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T14:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 14]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 18], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 18, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "body": "Pint sounds grand, though I'll believe the landing when I see actual telemetry on my screen.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 17], "text": "Anyone fancy a celebratory pint down the pub if they pull it off?"}, "note": "I'll wait for verified data before buying into either the doom or the triumph.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is good value compared to what governments usually waste money on, though the stream will probably crash.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"amused by his usual alarmism and conspiracy chasing"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his steady technical explanations"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful enthusiasm"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"running a routine database backup around 2:00 am tonight, so expect a few minutes of downtime if you're still awake.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 19], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 19, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-03T03:04:00.000Z", "body": "definitely grabbing a celebratory drink after work if that sky crane actually pulls it off! :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is always looking for a sinister angle when the engineers have clearly thought through the orbiters.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an inspiring and ambitious mission well worth the cost and technical risks.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires his clear technical rebuttals and calm explanations"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly cooking up disaster theories as usual"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his cheerful enthusiasm"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon"],"shift":"Her enthusiasm remains steady as quasistatic clearly dispels TheyKnow's alarmist claims about the communications relay."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "eastern hemisphere's status is telemetry is rock solid"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Mars 96's status is failed"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 20], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 20, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "body": "Celebrations aside, the real test begins only if the touchdown succeeds. The primary objective is establishing past habitability in Gale Crater's strata, which requires months of drilling and sample analysis. A functional rover on the surface is simply the prerequisite, not the conclusion.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild and kookaburra99 are getting ahead of themselves celebrating before the vehicle is even on the surface.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an ambitious and scientifically worthy mission, where autonomous engineering is standard practice rather than blind faith.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"irritated by his misinformed cynicism and conspiratorial spin on telemetry"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his precise, factual perspective"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"amused by his enthusiastic, cheerful optimism"}],"commitments":["wake up early on Monday morning to watch the NASA landing commentary"],"shift":"Rebutted TheyKnow's alarmist misconceptions about the relay orbiters and autonomous flight software."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic's opinion is autonomous control loops are standard practice, not blind faith"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"Walter is quite right.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 21], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 21, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "body": "Fair point mate, but getting the beast down in one piece is half the battle! Once they've stuck the landing, drilling into Martian dirt sounds like a walk in the park by comparison. Even if it turns out life on Mars was just a myth, finding out what's inside that mountain is pure science fiction brought to life. I reckon we're allowed a quick tipple on Monday just for getting the wheels on the turf, mind! Anyone knocking around the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday is welcome to join, first round is on me. cheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic is right that the science is the main point, but they still deserve a drink just for sticking the landing.", "state": {"position":"It is a daring, brilliant engineering feat and well worth having a proper crack at, even if it is expensive.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates their sensible perspective and technical grounding"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"pleased he is up for a pint if it works"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"enjoys sharing the excitement and joining in for a celebratory drink"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","have a celebratory pint down the pub if they pull it off"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2011-07-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-002⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-01T16:32:41.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-013⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T14:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 14]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-02T18:08:52.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-015⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 22], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 22, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2012-08-03T07:53:00.000Z", "body": "Equating the mechanical descent with the scientific mission is a fundamental error. An intact rover that suffers an avionics failure, a jammed drill mechanism, or contaminated sample chambers would merely represent a very expensive piece of scrap iron on the Martian regolith. The landing is an engineering prerequisite, nothing more. Its scientific merit rests entirely upon whether the instruments can reliably extract and process empirical data over the coming years. Planning pub visits before a single packet of scientific telemetry has even been verified seems remarkably premature.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 21], "text": "getting the beast down in one piece is half the battle!"}, "note": "PintOfMild treats a serious scientific endeavour like a football match to be celebrated at the pub.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's value depends on whether it successfully lands and returns empirical scientific results, not on hype or premature celebration.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects their precise and factual correction of misconceptions"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"dismisses their conspiratorial rhetoric and technical ignorance"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"views their emotional and celebratory approach as scientifically premature"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-05T08:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 177]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2011-07-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-002⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T23:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 23], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 23, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-03T08:58:00.000Z", "body": "Easy on the misery lads, let them have their pint. Keep it friendly. -- Kev (mod)", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne is determined to suck all the fun out of everything as usual.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is good value compared to standard government waste, but I will only believe the landing when I see actual telemetry with my own eyes.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful enthusiasm and wouldn't mind a pint"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"unimpressed by his usual doomsaying and conspiracy talk"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his steady technical focus on the facts"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time"],"shift":"Emphasised that he will wait for verified telemetry before believing either triumph or failure."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's description is first mission to send an organic chemistry laboratory to the surface of Mars"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's opinion is A pint sounds grand, but will believe the Curiosity landing only upon seeing actual telemetry"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 18]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 24], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 24, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-03T11:48:00.000Z", "body": "No misery intended on my part, Kev. A celebratory drink is harmless enough, provided we remain clear on the science.\n\nDrilling into Martian rock is hardly a walk in the park, either. Curiosity carries a 2.1-metre robotic arm and a drill designed to penetrate 2.5 centimetres into the surface to deliver powder directly to its internal ovens and spectrometers. We have never attempted that depth before with an autonomous roving vehicle. The mechanical stresses on those tools in an extreme thermal environment are non-trivial.\n\nGetting down safely will certainly be an immense engineering milestone, but we ought not to underestimate the complexity of what follows.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild trivialises the sheer operational difficulty of robotic sample extraction.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's scientific mission makes it well worth the investment, but the landing is merely a risky prerequisite rather than the conclusion.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he is getting ahead of himself planning celebrations"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his rigorous focus on scientific data over sentiment"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his persistent misconceptions about autonomous systems"}],"commitments":["wake up early on Monday morning to watch the NASA landing commentary"],"shift":"Emphasised that landing safely is merely a prerequisite and that celebration is premature until the actual scientific analysis begins."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Anyone fancy a pint in Leeds to drown our sorrows?\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"Keep the thread focused on the actual incoming telemetry and news reports, lads.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T19:57:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 60]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:15:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 84]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-24T17:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 115]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 25], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 25, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-03T19:12:00.000Z", "body": "Point taken, mind! Still worth cheering on the landing first, even if the real hard graft comes after.", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne could find a reason to complain about a sunny bank holiday.", "state": {"position":"It is an audacious, brilliant mission and well worth having a go at, even if getting down is only the start of the challenge.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is being a bit of a killjoy about pub plans"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values their grounded technical insight on the challenges ahead"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates him stepping in to keep things friendly"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"],"shift":"Has taken on board that the scientific work and drilling after touchdown will be just as tricky as the landing itself, though remains keen for a celebratory drink."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Planning pub visits before a single packet of scientific telemetry has even been verified seems remarkably premature.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Dead right.\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-30T10:43:23.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-011⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T14:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 14]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-02T18:08:52.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-015⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 26], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 26, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "body": "It certainly deserves our attention on Monday morning, regardless of the outcome. The operational burden on the team will be extraordinary from the outset. Controller shifts will be synchronised entirely to Martian solar days - roughly 24 hours and 40 minutes each - for the first ninety days just to maximise the rover's working windows between orbiter passes.\n\nThat continuous adjustment to Mars time highlights just how intensive the operational logistics are once wheels touch regolith. Transmitting instructions and retrieving data relies on narrow overhead windows with Odyssey and the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter, roughly twice a day for each craft. It is far from a trivial matter of simply pressing a button and watching data stream back in comfort.\n\nI shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touchdown is merely opening the laboratory door.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild remains cheerful about the adventure, but the relentless operational timetable will quickly show how demanding planetary science actually is.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's scientific mission makes it well worth the money, but landing safely is merely an engineering prerequisite to the complex analysis ahead.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"finds his optimism well-meaning but technically careless"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"agrees with his rigorous focus on scientific data over premature celebration"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"reassures him that scientific precision is not meant as misery"}],"commitments":["wake up early on Monday morning to watch the NASA landing commentary"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Punchcard and OffByOne have properly framed the situation: not a collapse, but a gradual emergence of secondary errors in January.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T19:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 155]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 172]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 27], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 27, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-03T20:24:00.000Z", "body": "Working on a 24 hour 40 minute cycle sounds like pure migraine territory. I get cranky enough doing maintenance on a standard night shift. Still, if yer man can get it down in one piece on Monday, sorting out their sleep schedules will be the easy bit.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Working on Mars time sounds like an absolute nightmare for the ops lads.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is good value compared to typical government waste, though I won't believe the landing until the live telemetry appears on screen.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his good humour and will let him have his fun"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he needs to ease up on the misery"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his thorough technical breakdown"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time"],"shift":"Stepped in as moderator to keep the tone friendly after the technical pedantry got a bit miserable."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-03T14:57:32.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-019⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 28], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 28, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2012-08-03T20:38:00.000Z", "body": "The operational schedule is hardly an afterthought. Those orbital communication passes determine whether commands can be sent and data retrieved at all. Mars Odyssey and the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter each have only two brief windows per day to relay telemetry back to Earth. If an orbiter experiences an anomaly - as Odyssey did just three weeks ago with its reaction wheel - or if the uplink timing drifts, operations stall entirely. \n\nNavigating those rigorous, narrow transmission windows while managing complex analytical hardware on the surface is a protracted technical discipline. That prolonged operational reality will determine the mission's ultimate return, not the euphoria of a single morning.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 27], "text": "sorting out their sleep schedules will be the easy bit."}, "note": "Sysop_Kev continues to treat the demanding operational mechanics of space exploration as trivial details compared to dramatic spectacle.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's value can only be judged by the empirical scientific data it successfully collects over time, not by premature celebrations over the mechanical landing.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates their rigorous and factual perspective on the technical challenges ahead"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"irritated by their superficial optimism and premature celebration"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"considers their moderation dismissive of proper scientific standards"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-03T14:57:32.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-019⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 29], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 29, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite true that the surface operations will demand a steady hand, but I see no harm in letting the boys have their pint if the machine touches down intact. \n\nOne step at a time is simply sensible practice. Landing a one-tonne laboratory on cables using an untried method is a formidable hurdle in its own right. If they clear that fence on Monday morning, the controllers will have earned a moment of relief before facing the long haul of drilling and shifting schedules. \n\nA prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slightly sideways on occasion, and one deals with them as they arise. \n\nLet us see the wheels safely down on the dust first. If the telemetry confirms it has landed in one piece, that is milestone enough for one evening. The mountain and the rock samples will still be waiting for them on Tuesday.\n\nWalter", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 28], "text": "That prolonged operational reality will determine the mission's ultimate return, not the euphoria of a single morning."}, "note": "OffByOne is borrowing tomorrow's headaches before we even know if the machine survives the descent.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a worthy and ambitious undertaking, though landing and long-term surface operations will both require steady nerve.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his sensible focus on the operational challenges ahead"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"finds his pedantic caution familiar but overly dour"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"understands his good-humoured enthusiasm"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto to watch the landing feed"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is part of a systems group with forty analysts debating a new transaction format in 1971"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"That comparison is entirely apt.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, RTU polling protocols communicate raw register values - binary status flags, analog-to-digital converter readings, and basic telemetry words. The polling cycle does not evaluate Julian..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-02T15:57:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 167]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-17T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 37]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T15:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 30], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 30, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-04T03:52:00.000Z", "body": "Taking it one step at a time sounds sensible enough on paper, Walter, but remote machinery does not just forgive things going sideways when you are operating across millions of miles... That is the real rub here. You are relying on a couple of fleeting passes a day through tired orbiters, and even the European craft is having to pitch in just to record the descent telemetry. Having all those delicate internal spectrometers and a heavy mechanical drill riding down on cables behind firing retro-rockets is pushing your luck. If that touchdown jars any of those tight mechanisms, you end up with an awful expensive radio mast stuck in the dirt.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard is far too relaxed about unproven mechanics surviving that violent touchdown.", "state": {"position":"Spending $2.5 billion on this sky crane gamble remains a waste of money that should have stayed on Earth, especially given the precarious radio relays and fragile hardware.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"pleased to see him highlighting the fragile orbiter relay windows"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"exasperated by his naive rush to celebrate at the pub"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"wary of his relaxed complacency toward unproven machinery"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 31], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 31, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "body": "Honestly, focusing only on everything that could break misses the whole point of why we send these missions in the first place. You cannot do ground-breaking science by playing it safe or expecting failure at every step. Sure, getting a rover this heavy down on nylon cords is a massive gamble, and yes, the science team will have heaps of tricky operational work once it is down. But having a full mobile lab with a drill and laser right there in Gale Crater is an incredible opportunity. If they actually pull off this landing on Monday, it is an astonishing achievement and well worth being excited about.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow is just stubbornly determined to see disaster in every piece of ambitious science.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an astonishing and worthwhile scientific endeavour that will be well worth celebrating if it manages to land safely.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear explanations of the complex operational challenges"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his persistent cynicism and doom-mongering"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"shares his eagerness to celebrate a successful landing"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon","grab a celebratory drink after work if the landing succeeds"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, RTU polling protocols communicate raw register values - binary status flags, analog-to-digital converter readings, and basic telemetry words. The polling cycle does not evaluate Julian..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T03:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 32], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 32, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "body": "Punchcard has the right of it. No sense worrying about the drill until you see if the wheels land.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard is talking common sense, no point fretting over instruments before wheels touch ground.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is good value compared to normal government waste, though the landing has to actually be seen on the telemetry before getting carried away.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady, practical common sense about taking things one step at a time"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"thinks he is still being overly pedantic and joyless about operations"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"sees him jumping at shadows and fretting over every tiny risk as usual"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time"],"shift":"Felt backed up by Punchcard's pragmatic approach against the board's resident worrywarts and pedants."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 33], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 33, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "body": "Spot on with that! You've got to take a proper punt if you want to achieve anything grand like this. No good worrying about every single cog and wheel until we see if the thing actually arrives in one piece. If that sky crane does the business on Monday morning, it'll be a massive triumph all round.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow really would find a dark cloud behind the brightest silver lining.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an astonishing, ambitious project well worth cheering on and celebrating if the landing succeeds, even with the technical hurdles that follow.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"agrees with his sensible, one-step-at-a-time perspective"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"likes their positive attitude about taking bold scientific gambles"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is still being a complete doom-monger as usual"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"],"shift":"Feels reassured by Punchcard and kookaburra99 that celebrating a successful touchdown is entirely justified before worrying about subsequent operational snags."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Planning pub visits before a single packet of scientific telemetry has even been verified seems remarkably premature.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T14:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 14]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 34], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 34, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "body": "Taking a proper punt sounds fine down at the betting shop, my friend, but here we are talking about two and a half billion dollars of public money... That is an enormous amount of wealth to gamble on an untested stunt like a rocket crane lowering fragile machinery on cords. \n\nWhen folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, the whole venture is riding on an intricate descent sequence where dozens of pyrotechnics and thrusters have to execute perfectly without human intervention, all while depending on narrow radio links through older orbiters just to hear if the thing is even in one piece. If that cable snags, or the dust and rocket wash fouls up an arm joint before it ever rolls an inch, all that investment simply vanishes into the rocks. It is not being overly pessimistic to point out that staking that much treasure on an all-or-nothing roll of the dice is a terrible waste of resources.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 33], "text": "Spot on with that! You've got to take a proper punt if you want to achieve anything grand like this."}, "note": "They are far too eager to cheer on a multibillion-dollar gamble that could blow apart in seconds.", "state": {"position":"Spending $2.5 billion on this sky crane gamble is a waste of money that should have stayed on Earth, especially given the fragile hardware and precarious radio relay windows.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"impatient with his casual attitude towards complex remote machinery"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"dismissive of her naive optimism and blind faith in the mission"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"agrees with his practical focus on narrow orbiter relay windows"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T20:03:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 168]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-03T11:17:59.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-016⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 35], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 35, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "body": "Worrying about where else the funds might have gone does not change the fact that the craft is already on its final approach. The money was spent, the engineering was done, and the rover is arriving whether we fret over the balance sheet or not. \n\nEvery ambitious venture looks like an intolerable gamble when one tallies up all the individual components that must cooperate. Cataloguing every wire and pyrotechnic bolt that could stick will only drive a man to distraction before the event even begins. We shall have our answer soon enough. Better to just put the kettle on and wait for the signal.\n\nWalter", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 34], "text": "It is not being overly pessimistic to point out that staking that much treasure on an all-or-nothing roll of the dice is a terrible waste of resources."}, "note": "TheyKnow continues to borrow trouble over past ledger entries when the craft is already on its way.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a worthy and ambitious mission, and while the untried landing is a formidable hurdle, we should wait to see it touch down before worrying about every potential glitch.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is borrowing trouble and catastrophising as usual"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"finds his pedantic caution familiar but overly dour"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his cheerful, sensible outlook"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto to watch the landing feed"],"shift":"Unmoved by TheyKnow borrowing trouble over failure modes, keeping his focus on waiting for touchdown first."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T20:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 61]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T15:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-03T11:17:59.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-016⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 36], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 36, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-04T19:27:00.000Z", "body": "Too true, Walter! Blimey, the cheques have already cleared and the thing is nearly knocking on the front door, so there is no use crying over spilt milk or locked bank vaults now. \n\nBesides, mate, you don't discover whether there's life on Mars by sticking two billion quid under the mattress! We might as well sit back, cross everything, and enjoy the show on Monday morning. Life would be bloody boring if nobody ever took a flyer on something grand. \n\nCup of tea at dawn it is. cheers :)", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 35], "text": "Worrying about where else the funds might have gone does not change the fact that the craft is already on its final approach."}, "note": "TheyKnow would find a way to moan about the cost of a free round of drinks.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an incredible, bold gamble that is well worth backing, and there is no point fretting over the cost or every tiny risk when the landing is right around the corner.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"admires his calm, practical common sense about putting the kettle on and waiting"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is being a total party-pooper as usual over the price tag"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"cheered by their shared enthusiasm for taking big, exciting chances"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"],"shift":"Has doubled down on his excitement for the daring landing after Punchcard dismissed TheyKnow's complaints about the budget."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA's assessment is no interruption occurred in any critical operations"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "investigators: \"Investigators later found that one piece of ground software gave thruster data in imperial units (pound-force seconds) while the navigation software expected metric units (newton-se..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Planning pub visits before a single packet of scientific telemetry has even been verified seems remarkably premature.\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:56:00.000Z", "text": "The Boston hospital incident demonstrates precisely why audits are conducted; oversight worked as intended, and missing an administrative patch on a server is not an uncontrollable physical failure..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 25]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 37], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 37, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "body": "Well, we shall not quite discover life itself on Monday, even if everything goes off without a hitch. The instruments are built to tell us whether Gale Crater ever had the right conditions to support it in the past, which is rather a different question, though no less fascinating for it.\n\nStill, your general point stands. The machine is out there in the dark, and all the fretting in the world will not alter its trajectory now. I intend to take my own advice, keep a pot of tea nearby, and see what the telemetry tells us when the hour comes.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild has his facts slightly mixed up regarding what the rover can detect, but his good spirits are welcome.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a worthy and ambitious venture, and since the money is already spent and the craft is arriving, there is no use borrowing trouble until we see whether it lands safely.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly borrowing trouble over ledgers and doom scenarios as always"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his cheerful, grounded outlook and shared anticipation"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his technical awareness but finds his worry over future operational friction premature"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto to watch the landing feed"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '74 we spent three weeks arguing over how to define a valid postal code on our tapes\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T15:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 3]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 38], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 38, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-04T21:09:00.000Z", "body": "Fair point Walter, though I'd still buy the little green men a pint in Leeds if they fancied it! cheers :)", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard is dead right, best to just put the kettle on and see what happens Monday morning.", "state": {"position":"The mission is an incredible and exciting punt that is well worth backing, and there is no sense crying over the money spent when the rover is already on its doorstep.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"enjoys his steady, reassuring common sense about having a cuppa and watching the show"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is being a predictable party-pooper crying over spilt milk"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"appreciates their shared enthusiasm for taking bold chances"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Spot on with that!\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"replying to quasistatic (post #103)\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-31T22:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 117]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 111]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:44:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 114]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 25]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 39], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 39, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-04T21:49:00.000Z", "body": "I dare say they would find a Leeds bitter quite a change from Martian perchlorate, though they might appreciate the hospitality all the same. :)\n\nIt will be a late night here in Toronto. The final descent is timed for just after one in the morning our time, so I suspect my tea will need to be rather strong to see it through. We will know soon enough whether Odyssey catches that tone from the surface or if we are left waiting on the recording orbiters. \n\nUntil then, the controllers have done all they can. We may as well get some rest while we can.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild's cheerful outlook makes the long wait for the landing far more agreeable.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an admirable and ambitious scientific mission, and with the craft already on approach, the sensible thing is to wait calmly for the landing telemetry rather than fret over money already spent.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is pointlessly brooding over ledgers and doom scenarios when the die is cast"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his genial company and good spirits, even if his scientific notions are a bit loose"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his technical awareness but finds his worry over future operational friction premature"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto to watch the landing feed"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"A pint in Leeds sounds very civilised, PintOfMild, though I suspect the real work for many software teams will only just be starting next week as the deferred billing and batch errors..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's experience is part of a systems group with forty analysts debating a new transaction format in 1971"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 57]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-25T11:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 137]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T08:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 23]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 40], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 40, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-04T21:54:00.000Z", "body": "Too right, Walter. Nothing left to do now except let the telemetry do the talking.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 39], "text": "Until then, the controllers have done all they can. We may as well get some rest while we can."}, "note": "Punchcard has the right attitude, just waiting on the actual signal instead of stewing over it.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is good value compared to normal government waste, but there is no point fretting over what might break until we see the wheels touch down on the telemetry.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"admires his calm, practical outlook on letting things play out without fuss"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"sees him hopelessly agonizing over every possible failure and cost as usual"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"enjoys his good humour and enthusiasm"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-03T12:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-017⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 27]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 41], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 41, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "body": "It really is out of everyone's hands now anyway, so treating it like a wasted gamble before it even touches down just feels miserable for no good reason. The money spent went into the pockets of thousands of brilliant scientists and engineers who designed something completely extraordinary, not fired off into the void to burn. And having the courage to fund real planetary science is what actually pushes human knowledge forward. With the federal budget already threatening massive cuts to the Mars programme over the next few years, backing a bold project like this matters more than ever. If we only ever spent money where there was zero danger of failure, we would never explore past our own backyard. Less than thirty hours to go now until touchdown. Tomorrow afternoon is going to be an absolute nail-biter, but I reckon it is worth being thrilled about what we might learn if that signal comes through.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will always find a way to moan about public money instead of appreciating what an incredible feat this could be.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a bold and worthwhile mission whose potential scientific discoveries justify the high risks and costs.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady, sensible perspective on waiting for results"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"loves his cheerful enthusiasm and adventurous attitude"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"tired of his endless cynicism and fixation on waste"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon","grab a celebratory drink after work if the landing succeeds"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "eastern hemisphere's status is telemetry is rock solid"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"Walter is quite right.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 42], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 42, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-05T02:56:00.000Z", "body": "Saying the money just supported paychecks misses the point, folks... You still have to answer for whether risking that much public treasure on one throw is responsible stewardship. Pushing ahead regardless just because the checks already cleared does not make the gamble any wiser. If this thing crashes into the dust, it puts the whole future program in jeopardy.", "quote": NONE, "note": "kookaburra99 thinks signing the checks makes a bad gamble worthwhile.", "state": {"position":"Staking two and a half billion dollars of public money on an untested sky crane contraption and precarious radio links is a reckless waste that should have stayed on Earth.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"annoyed by her starry-eyed rationalizations and dismissive attitude toward public spending"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"frustrated by his passive, shrug-of-the-shoulders fatalism about an enormous gamble"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"disapproves of his frivolous pub-bet mentality toward billions in public funds"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's opinion is astonishing piece of software and systems engineering if all the stages execute properly"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2006-08-21T06:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 65]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "text": "It really is out of everyone's hands now anyway, so treating it like a wasted gamble before it even touches down just feels miserable for no good reason. The money spent went into the pockets of th..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 43], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 43, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-05T08:31:00.000Z", "body": "Cheer up mate, you only get anywhere in life by daring to try something incredible now and then!", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow really would find a cloud behind every single silver lining.", "state": {"position":"The mission is a brilliant, bold venture that is well worth taking a flyer on, and rather than fretting over the cost when it's already on approach, we should put the kettle on and enjoy the show.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"enjoys his calm, sensible company and shared tea-drinking approach to the landing"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"agrees completely that it is worth being thrilled and bold rather than miserable"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"views him as a relentless wet blanket who insists on spoiling the fun"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Pentagon's mission critical systems count is 2,101"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "FAA's assessment is no interruption occurred in any critical operations"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "investigators: \"Investigators later found that one piece of ground software gave thruster data in imperial units (pound-force seconds) while the navigation software expected metric units (newton-se..."}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Stern's opinion is naturally protective of his mission"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Spot on with that!\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 64]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 25]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-04T21:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 38]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "text": "It really is out of everyone's hands now anyway, so treating it like a wasted gamble before it even touches down just feels miserable for no good reason. The money spent went into the pockets of th..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 44], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 44, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2012-08-05T09:11:00.000Z", "body": "Actually, repeating the word 'gamble' or appealing to emotional audacity adds precisely zero explanatory value. Either the analytical laboratory functions on the surface and returns rigorous geochemical data, or it does not. The expenditure is justified strictly by the empirical results gathered over the coming years, not by dramatic sentiments beforehand.", "quote": NONE, "note": "PintOfMild continues to treat serious planetary science as an exercise in romantic bravado rather than verifiable data collection.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's value can only be evaluated based on the empirical science it returns, not on emotive rhetoric or the landing attempt itself.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his technical precision regarding the mission's actual scientific remit"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his persistent catastrophising and refusal to evaluate empirical probabilities"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"irritated by his sloppy romanticism and careless conflation of habitability with finding life"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Mars's finding is about half of all missions sent to Mars have failed"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Times Square: \"times square dropped the ball with millions watching and not a single thing failed, it is completely sorted.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Robert Zubrin: \"Mars Society president Robert Zubrin says success would make this the best Mars mission ever, while a crash could take the whole Mars programme down with it.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's feeling is sad seeing Pluto get squeezed out after all these years"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's opinion is astonishing piece of software and systems engineering if all the stages execute properly"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "text": "It really is out of everyone's hands now anyway, so treating it like a wasted gamble before it even touches down just feels miserable for no good reason. The money spent went into the pockets of th..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 45], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 45, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-05T11:52:00.000Z", "body": "Either way, arguing about the bill won't steer the craft. We will find out tomorrow morning.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will keep fretting about the cost right up until touchdown.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a reasonable use of funds compared to typical government waste, but we just have to wait for the telemetry to see if it landed safely.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"admires his level-headed, practical patience"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his endless fretting and catastrophic predictions"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his cheerful enthusiasm"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time","have a celebratory pint with PintOfMild if the landing succeeds"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's purpose is prepare the way for future searches and returning samples from Mars, not life detection"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's opinion is astonishing piece of software and systems engineering if all the stages execute properly"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:53:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 22]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "text": "It really is out of everyone's hands now anyway, so treating it like a wasted gamble before it even touches down just feels miserable for no good reason. The money spent went into the pockets of th..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 46], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 46, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne is quite correct. Framing systematic aerospace engineering as either an audacious gamble or a reckless wager merely clouds the issue. The merit of the expenditure rests on whether the rover can deliver verifiable geochemical analysis from Gale Crater's sedimentary record, nothing more.\n\nTo keep matters on track for tomorrow morning, there are three practical milestones to watch for:\n\n1. Entry interface and the deceleration sequence down through the atmospheric friction.\n2. Successful acquisition of the real-time carrier signal relayed via Mars Odyssey around 05:31 UTC.\n3. The return of the initial engineering telemetry confirming the rover's autonomous health on the regolith.\n\nUntil those direct signals arrive, speculation serves little purpose.", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne remains the most sensible voice here, correctly cutting through the empty rhetoric about gambles.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's scientific mission justifies the expenditure, but whether the investment pays off depends entirely on the empirical science gathered after touchdown, not on emotional posturing beforehand.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly approves of his refusal to indulge in emotional rhetoric instead of empirical data"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his precise scientific correction regarding past habitability versus finding life"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his persistent alarmism and framing of complex engineering as a mere gamble"}],"commitments":["wake up early on Monday morning to watch the NASA landing commentary"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-28T07:14:00.000Z", "text": "Similarly, commercial dispatch operations do not depend solely on standard consumer voice lines to coordinate regional movements. Private radio networks, telex, dedicated carrier lines, and basic r..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2006-08-24T10:39:00.000Z", "text": "My only worry is still what quasistatic brought up yesterday about the whole thing stalling out. With four separate bits to vote on, you could easily get folk backing the dwarf planet label in 6A b..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T11:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "text": "It really is out of everyone's hands now anyway, so treating it like a wasted gamble before it even touches down just feels miserable for no good reason. The money spent went into the pockets of th..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 47], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 47, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "body": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousand things that can go sideways in an instant, and once that entry starts, Earth is completely out of the loop. If that direct view drops away and Odyssey fails to catch the carrier tone clean, you will not have any engineering telemetry to analyze at all. You are betting the farm on an autonomous sequence executing perfectly down to the millisecond without a human hand on the wheel. When that much tax money is on the line, calling it a gamble is not just dramatic sentiment, it is plain common sense. If that rig hits the ground wrong, those budget cuts folks keep mentioning will get an awful lot deeper, and there will not be any science program left to justify the expense. We will see soon enough whether all that complexity holds up under the strain, but nobody should pretend it is just an ordinary day at the office.", "quote": NONE, "note": "They are far too eager to brush off the real physical risk just because someone wrote it down as a numbered list.", "state": {"position":"Staking two and a half billion dollars of public money on an overly complex, unproven landing scheme that could wipe out future exploration if it crashes is an irresponsible waste.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"unimpressed by her excuse that spending the cash on salaries justifies an reckless gamble"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"skeptical of his sterile view that discounts how precarious the relay and descent really are"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"annoyed by his glib calls to cheer up over squandered billions"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow's opinion is risking massive public treasure on Curiosity is an irresponsible gamble that could jeopardise the whole future program if it crashes"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 34]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "text": "It really is out of everyone's hands now anyway, so treating it like a wasted gamble before it even touches down just feels miserable for no good reason. The money spent went into the pockets of th..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-05T04:07:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-022⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 48], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 48, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "body": "No one is pretending it is an ordinary day at the office. Back in '76 with Viking, we had the very same knot in our stomachs waiting on the teleprinter. \n\nWhen a job is committed to tape, pacing the floor will not change a single bit. The sequence will run. We only have to wait for the printer to chatter.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will borrow trouble right up until the printer gives him the receipt.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an ambitious and worthwhile mission, and there is nothing to be done now but wait patiently for the telemetry to confirm whether it lands safely.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"thinks he is stubbornly borrowing trouble and catastrophizing when the outcome is already in motion"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his steady, methodical breakdown of the upcoming telemetry milestones"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"appreciates his sensible, no-nonsense attitude about waiting for the results"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto with strong tea to watch the landing feed"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-05T04:07:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-022⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 49], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 49, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-05T15:38:00.000Z", "body": "Dead right, Walter. Yer man pacing up and down won't alter the data by a single byte.\n\nSure look, the hardware is committed now. If Odyssey catches the packets, grand, we'll see the numbers. If it doesn't, we wait on the logs from Mars Express or MRO later in the day. No amount of fretting on a message board rewrites the ROM. \n\nI'll see what the pipe looks like at half six.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will pace a hole in the carpet before the carrier signal even locks on.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a reasonable use of money compared to what gets wasted elsewhere, but there is no point arguing until the telemetry confirms it landed safely.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his steady patience and practical perspective"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"annoyed by his repetitive hand-wringing"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"agrees with his no-nonsense, pragmatic focus on actual results"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time","have a celebratory pint with PintOfMild if the landing succeeds"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, RTU polling protocols communicate raw register values - binary status flags, analog-to-digital converter readings, and basic telemetry words. The polling cycle does not evaluate Julian..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 18]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 50], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 50, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-05T16:14:00.000Z", "body": "It is just past 2 am here in Melbourne and I probably should try to get at least a few hours of sleep before school, but I am way too keyed up. Setting up the projector for my students first thing this morning, and then we just wait and watch. Fingers crossed for that carrier signal!", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop doom-mongering no matter how clear the facts are, but Walter and quasistatic are right that there is nothing left to do except wait for the telemetry.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a bold, vital investment in science that pushes human boundaries and inspires us, well worth the risk and cost.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exhausted by his relentless doom-mongering and refusal to see the value"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"deeply respects his calm, experienced perspective"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his clear, objective explanations of the engineering"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon","grab a celebratory drink after work if the landing succeeds"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 16]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 51], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 51, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-05T16:31:00.000Z", "body": "Fair enough, Walter, nobody can change what is already committed to the silicon... but treating it like just waiting for a printout skips over what is actually hanging in the balance here. When Viking went in, people were watching the science, sure, but today you have the whole future Mars budget on the chopping block if this thing pancakes into Gale. That is not just waiting on numbers, that is public accountability. \n\nI am keeping my ears tuned to the bands tonight same as the rest of you, believe me... but we will know soon enough if all that delicate machinery can truly take care of itself out there.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard is too calm about this, treating billions in tax money like an old mainframe run that can just be resubmitted if it fails.", "state": {"position":"Risking two and a half billion dollars of taxpayer money on an overly complex, uncrewed autonomous descent that could crash and ruin future planetary funding is an irresponsible waste.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he hides extreme real-world risk behind dry academic bullet points"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"annoyed by his dismissal of legitimate concerns over public money as empty sentiment"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"acknowledges his experience but thinks fatalism ignores the real financial stakes"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '76 with Viking, we had the very same knot in our stomachs waiting on the teleprinter.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter's relay window frequency is two brief windows per day to relay telemetry back to Earth"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"I'm keeping my ears on the amateur bands to see how the evening unfolds across the Pacific.\""}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "text": "Establishing command centres functions identically. Having staff present merely ensures rapid logging and prompt administrative reporting if minor anomalies occur, exactly as Punchcard noted. We sh..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T00:32:00.000Z", "text": "We will not have to wait on speculation or rumours much longer. In a matter of hours, Auckland and Sydney will cross midnight. The lights will remain on, the amateur sets will pick up ordinary holi..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 34]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 52], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 52, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-05T17:02:00.000Z", "body": "Budget won't matter a damn till we see if it's in one piece.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 51], "text": "today you have the whole future Mars budget on the chopping block if this thing pancakes into Gale."}, "note": "TheyKnow will still be giving out about the budget when the first picture lands.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a reasonable use of money compared to what gets wasted elsewhere, but the job is committed to silicon and there is no point fretting until we see the data come through tomorrow morning.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"strongly agrees with his calm, experienced view on waiting for the telemetry"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his restless pacing and fretful hand-wringing"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"looking forward to a pint if the landing succeeds"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time","have a celebratory pint with PintOfMild if the landing succeeds"],"shift":"Reaffirmed his pragmatic stance alongside Punchcard that pacing and worrying will not change a single byte of telemetry."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 41]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "If that rig hits the ground wrong, those budget cuts folks keep mentioning will get an awful lot deeper, and there will not be any science program left to justify the expense. We will see soon enou..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 53], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 53, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-05T18:35:00.000Z", "body": "Dead right Kev. No sense losing your hair over the bank accounts when the thing is already plummeting down! What's done is done. All we can do now is stick the kettle on, get the stream up at dawn, and hope the lucky peanuts do their job for them.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 52], "text": "Budget won't matter a damn till we see if it's in one piece."}, "note": "TheyKnow will never stop chewing his fingernails over disasters before they even happen.", "state": {"position":"It is a massive and exciting venture that is well worth backing, and rather than fretting over the cost or the risks when it is already on its way down, we should just put the kettle on and see what happens.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady, experienced perspective that pacing the floor changes nothing"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"still finds him overly anxious and determined to fret about disaster"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"welcomes his sensible no-nonsense attitude about waiting for the outcome"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Boss's opinion is reads as if written by lawyers"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Alan Boss's opinion is reads as though it were drafted by lawyers rather than scientists"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild's opinion is TheyKnow is still making mountains out of tiny till errors when the rollout is basically done"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"Planning pub visits before a single packet of scientific telemetry has even been verified seems remarkably premature.\""}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T17:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 59]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 64]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 25]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-04T21:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 38]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "If that rig hits the ground wrong, those budget cuts folks keep mentioning will get an awful lot deeper, and there will not be any science program left to justify the expense. We will see soon enou..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 54], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 54, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-05T19:16:00.000Z", "body": "Aye. Peanuts or no peanuts, we'll see soon enough what lands on the screen. Alarms set for six. See you lads on the other side of it.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 53], "text": "All we can do now is stick the kettle on, get the stream up at dawn, and hope the lucky peanuts do their job for them."}, "note": "PintOfMild has the right attitude, no use fussing till the alarm goes off.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a reasonable use of money compared to what gets wasted elsewhere, but what is done is done and we will know its worth when the telemetry arrives in the morning.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"appreciates his down-to-earth attitude and shared plan for tea in the morning"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"tired of his endless hand-wringing over budgets and doom scenarios"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his calm, experienced perspective on waiting for the hardware to do its job"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time","have a celebratory pint with PintOfMild if the landing succeeds"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev: \"No need to panic.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic: \"I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Sysop_Kev's role is moderator"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T20:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "text": "I have to head into the office later on for the dreaded evening shift, but I will definitely be popping back in here during my tea breaks to see how kookaburra99 and the Aussies are getting on. Kee..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T22:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 159]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 55], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 55, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-05T19:33:00.000Z", "body": "Hoping on lucky charms is fine for a bit of fun, my friend... but it says an awful lot when grown engineers sitting at the consoles are falling back on passing around bowls of peanuts just to calm their nerves! That tells you plenty about how much real control anyone has over this thing right now.\n\nOnce that clock runs down, nobody gets to steer, nobody gets to hit the brakes, and those computers have to sort out every inch of that descent on their own. We will know soon enough if the radio speaks up, but having to lean on blind luck is a mighty strange way to run a multi-billion-dollar show. I'll be listening.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 53], "text": "All we can do now is stick the kettle on, get the stream up at dawn, and hope the lucky peanuts do their job for them."}, "note": "They are treating this whole thing like a lark with lucky peanuts instead of realizing how helpless mission control actually is once the descent starts.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's $2.5 billion price tag is an irresponsible waste of public money gambled on an overly intricate autonomous landing sequence.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his past experience but thinks comparing this gamble to Viking ignores public accountability"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"thinks he is being overly dismissive about the financial stakes of a potential crash"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he treats serious public funding like a casual flutter with lucky charms"}],"commitments":["listen to the radio bands tonight during the landing attempt"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, RTU polling protocols communicate raw register values - binary status flags, analog-to-digital converter readings, and basic telemetry words. The polling cycle does not evaluate Julian..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T00:22:00.000Z", "text": "Official spokesmen always tell you everything is under CONTROL right up until the switch flips, my friends. Nobody wants a bank run or folks clearing out the grocery aisles before the weekend, so o..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T00:32:00.000Z", "text": "We will not have to wait on speculation or rumours much longer. In a matter of hours, Auckland and Sydney will cross midnight. The lights will remain on, the amateur sets will pick up ordinary holi..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 34]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 56], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 56, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-05T20:59:00.000Z", "body": "Give over mate, it is just a bit of harmless fun! Like wearing your lucky socks for the match, doesn't mean the lads forgot how to play football. Anyone in Leeds fancy a pint next Friday to celebrate if it sticks the landing? First round on me at the Head of Steam. Alarm set, cheers :)", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 55], "text": "it says an awful lot when grown engineers sitting at the consoles are falling back on passing around bowls of peanuts just to calm their nerves!"}, "note": "TheyKnow really would find a dark cloud behind every silver lining, exhausting bloke.", "state": {"position":"It is a massive and exciting mission worth backing, and rather than worrying about the budget or the risks while it is dropping onto Mars, we should just get the tea on and hope for the best.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"enjoys his down-to-earth, sensible camaraderie in cutting through the worry"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"finds his continued hand-wringing and doom-mongering exhausting"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his calm, seasoned outlook"}],"commitments":["get up early on Monday morning with a cuppa to watch the landing stream","buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard: \"Back in '76 with Viking, we had the very same knot in our stomachs waiting on the teleprinter.\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Spot on with that!\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 25]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-04T21:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 38]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-05T17:38:49.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-024⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 57], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 57, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-05T21:17:00.000Z", "body": "A tradition dating back to Ranger 7 in 1964 is hardly an operational vulnerability. It is simply human ritual while the software executes.\n\nNow, to keep to the practicalities: the descent begins around 05:10 UTC, with Odyssey relaying the tones. Sleep well, everyone, and set your alarms.", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow continues to treat harmless institutional folklore as evidence of technical weakness.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's scientific mission justifies the cost, but its true worth will be decided by the geological and chemical findings after landing, not pre-landing nerves.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his steady, realistic perspective on technical sequences running their course"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exasperated by his persistent hand-wringing and treating routine superstitions as technical proof of vulnerability"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"finds his easygoing optimism harmless enough, even if unscientific"}],"commitments":["wake up early on Monday morning to watch the NASA landing commentary"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic's opinion is framing systematic aerospace engineering as a gamble or reckless wager clouds the issue, and Curiosity's merit rests on delivering geochemical analysis from Gale Crater"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T00:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 57]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T08:58:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 23]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T11:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "text": "It really is out of everyone's hands now anyway, so treating it like a wasted gamble before it even touches down just feels miserable for no good reason. The money spent went into the pockets of th..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 58], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 58, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-05T22:38:00.000Z", "body": "Ritual or not, folks, it still comes down to the cold fact that nobody has their fingers on the controls once that descent starts... You can brush it off as harmless tradition, but when you have an automated sequence doing all the heavy lifting across millions of miles, any little hiccup in the timing or the sensors means game over. That is an awful thin margin to bet billions on, no matter how folks choose to pass the time in the control room. My coffee is brewed and the shack is warmed up... we will see what that carrier tone has to say soon enough.", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic always waves off real risks as simple routine when people are flying completely blind.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity remains an unjustified $2.5 billion gamble, and the reliance on superstitious rituals shows how little control anyone actually has over such an autonomous descent.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he dismisses genuine vulnerabilities as mere routine or harmless ritual"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"finds his trivializing of billions in public money through lucky charms frustrating"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"thinks he is dodging the broader issue of financial and public accountability"}],"commitments":["listen to the radio bands tonight during the landing attempt"],"shift":"He seized on the peanut ritual as further proof that NASA lacks actual control over the descent."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 30]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 34]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 47]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-05T17:38:49.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-024⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 59], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 59, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-06T02:20:00.000Z", "body": "Projector is sorted, class is buzzing, and now we just wait for that descent to kick off.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 57], "text": "the descent begins around 05:10 UTC, with Odyssey relaying the tones."}, "note": "My students are so hyped to see this stream live this afternoon.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a bold, vital investment in science that pushes human boundaries and inspires us, well worth the risk and cost.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"exhausted by his relentless doom-mongering and refusal to see the value"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"deeply respects his calm, experienced perspective"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his clear, objective explanations of the engineering"}],"commitments":["show the NASA stream to her class on Monday afternoon","grab a celebratory drink after work if the landing succeeds"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "sky crane's assessment is the least crazy way to land a rover as big as Curiosity, though it looks a little crazy"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 41]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 60], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 60, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T03:10:00.000Z", "body": "Having nobody at the controls is simply the physics of the distance, not a failing of nerve. A fourteen-minute radio delay means human hands on the stick would only guarantee a wreck. The machine must fly itself, or not at all.\n\nMy kettle has just boiled. In an hour or two the signal will arrive, and we shall see what the instruments have to say. Enjoy the broadcast with your class.\n\nWalter", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 58], "text": "it still comes down to the cold fact that nobody has their fingers on the controls once that descent starts..."}, "note": "TheyKnow will never grasp that automation is a necessity of distance, not an admission of defeat.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an ambitious and worthwhile mission, and there is nothing left to do now but wait calmly for the descent sequence to execute and the signal to arrive.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his persistent fretting over routine rituals and autonomous flight"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his cool-headed reminder of mission history and schedule"},{"member":"Sysop_Kev","feeling":"likes his no-nonsense, practical patience"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto with strong tea to watch the landing feed"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "NOC screens's status is completely flat"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's status is on final approach"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 61], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 61, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-06T03:22:00.000Z", "body": "Physics explains why you cannot steer it, Walter... but it doesn't make building a machine that complicated any less of a gamble with public money! Handing total FAITH over to black-box software is quite a leap. Sitting here watching the dials right along with you, my friend... let us see what actually comes down the pipe.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Punchcard is still rationalizing away the danger of letting an automated black box run the whole show.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity remains an unjustified multi-billion-dollar gamble resting entirely on an autonomous sequence that leaves no room for human intervention or error.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"thinks he is rationalizing away the immense risk of handing total control to automated systems"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he brushes off genuine operational vulnerabilities as harmless tradition"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he is far too flippant about public money and serious engineering dangers"}],"commitments":["listen to the radio bands tonight during the landing attempt"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 16]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 30]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 47]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 62], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 62, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T03:24:00.000Z", "body": "It is not a matter of faith, my friend, but of rigorous testing before the craft ever left the ground. Now it is simply waiting on the clock.\n\nThere are nearly two hours yet before atmospheric entry begins. Pacing the floor now will not change a single line of logic. Let us see what the telemetry tells us when Odyssey picks it up.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 61], "text": "Handing total FAITH over to black-box software is quite a leap."}, "note": "TheyKnow mistakes careful automated design for blind faith, but the telemetry will settle the matter soon enough.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an ambitious and worthwhile mission, and there is nothing to do now but wait for the autonomous descent to run and the telemetry to arrive.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"patiently dismissive of his endless fretting over automation and public spending"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"warmly wishes him well showing the landing to his class"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his steady focus on facts and flight schedules"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto with strong tea to watch the landing feed"],"shift":"Has put his kettle on and posted a calm engineering reality check to TheyKnow while settling in for the landing signal."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's opinion is astonishing piece of software and systems engineering if all the stages execute properly"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Adam Steltzner's assessment is it looks crazy, but is the result of reasoned engineering"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:56:00.000Z", "text": "The Boston hospital incident demonstrates precisely why audits are conducted; oversight worked as intended, and missing an administrative patch on a server is not an uncontrollable physical failure..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-04T16:09:00.000Z", "text": "When folks say you have to take big chances to achieve great things, well, that is fine poetry, but back here in the real world that cash could have done immense good right on the ground. Instead, ..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 63], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 63, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-06T03:42:00.000Z", "body": "Fair point on the waiting, Walter... though testing on a stand back home never quite matches the real thing when Mars takes hold. The NASA TV feed is up now on Ustream with a big crowd tuning in, so we won't have long to wait. Let's see how that Odyssey link holds.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Walter has an awful lot of confidence in lab testing holding up millions of miles away.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an excessively expensive and needlessly complicated gamble that recklessly trusts billions of dollars to autonomous software completely out of human control.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his calm demeanor but thinks he places blind faith in pre-programmed software"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he dismisses genuine operational risks as harmless tradition"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he treats serious technical hazards and public money far too casually"}],"commitments":["listen to the radio bands and monitor the carrier tones tonight during the landing attempt"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T02:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 47]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 28]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 30]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-05T04:07:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-022⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 47]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T16:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 51]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 64], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 64, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T04:04:00.000Z", "body": "The stream is steady here as well. About an hour to go now, so let us watch.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 63], "text": "The NASA TV feed is up now on Ustream with a big crowd tuning in, so we won't have long to wait."}, "note": "TheyKnow is still fretful, but at least he is watching the feed with the rest of us.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an ambitious, well-tested project, and there is nothing to do now but wait patiently for the clock and the telemetry.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"patiently reminds him that automation is engineering, not blind faith"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"warmly wishes him well showing the landing to his class"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his steady focus on facts and flight schedules"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto with strong tea to watch the landing feed"],"shift":"Reiterated to TheyKnow that software autonomy is a matter of thorough testing rather than faith, while settling in for the upcoming entry."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T16:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 58]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 65], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 65, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-06T04:11:00.000Z", "body": "Got the feed running alongside the receiver gear here... plenty of big names and brass down at JPL for the show, too. Not long now until the real test begins, so we will see how that relay behaves when Odyssey passes over. Fingers crossed for good telemetry.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I will keep watching the Odyssey carrier tones closely to see if that automated sequence actually holds together.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an overcomplicated, multi-billion-dollar gamble that places far too much trust in autonomous software when human control is impossible.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his steady patience but believes he trusts ground testing and software far too much"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"thinks he brushes off genuine operational risks as harmless tradition"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"thinks he takes serious technical hazards and public money far too lightly"}],"commitments":["listen to the radio bands and monitor the Odyssey carrier tones during the landing attempt"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "NOC screens's status is completely flat"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:37:00.000Z", "text": "Looking at network ping times from Dublin or watching city streetlamps stay on doesn't tell us how the main water treatment valves or substation relays hold up over the next twenty-four hours. A lo..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "text": "Boston Children's Hospital was finding missing patches on critical cardiac servers just this past week, so anyone acting like every loose end was neatly tied up months ago is ignoring reality. When..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2002-01-04T08:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 149]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 47]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T16:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 51]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T03:30:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-028⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 66], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 66, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T04:28:00.000Z", "body": "It is quite a gathering out in Pasadena tonight, I see. Seeing Mr. Bolden and the science folk assembled reminds me a bit of the floor back during our first big cheque-clearing conversion on the System/360 in '71. Half the bank directors turned up in their finest suits just to stare at the tape drives spinning, as if their presence might persuade the magnetic heads to behave.\n\nOf course, once the tape began to feed, the executives could do no more than the night watchman. The machine runs its routines regardless of who is in the gallery. \n\nWe have just about forty minutes now before the atmosphere takes hold out there. My mug is topped up, the stream is quiet, and the room here is peaceful. We shall soon have our answer, one byte at a time.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow seems to be settling down at last to watch the telemetry come in alongside the rest of us.", "state": {"position":"The mission is an admirable, thoroughly engineered undertaking, and now we must simply watch the live feed and see what the telemetry delivers.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"companionable vigil despite his persistent fretting"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased he is sharing the broadcast with his class"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his practical, grounded schedule updates"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto with strong tea to watch the landing feed"],"shift":"Settled in alongside the others watching the steady video feed as atmospheric entry approaches."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-27T12:56:00.000Z", "text": "It is not just about keeping clerks busy with paperwork, Punchcard... The whole economy runs on tight schedules these days, and things do not happen in a vacuum. If a regional warehouse cannot veri..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T22:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 28]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 67], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 67, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-06T04:38:00.000Z", "body": "The stream is running smooth as anything here on the big screen! Seeing all the folks packed into mission control at JPL really drives home how much hard work went into this moment. The kids have gone completely quiet watching the clock count down. Less than an hour to go now!", "quote": NONE, "note": "The whole classroom is holding its breath waiting for that first signal from Mars.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a daring, crucial leap for science that inspires people and justifies the cost and risks.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"deeply appreciates his steady wisdom and warm encouragement"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"weary of his endless skepticism and talk of blind gambles"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"grateful for his precise timeline and engineering updates"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream live with her class","grab a celebratory drink after work if the landing succeeds"],"shift":"She has set up the stream in her classroom with her excited students and is waiting calmly for the descent to start."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "automated systems: \"the automated systems elsewhere processed record volumes throughout the day\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "text": "What remains for tomorrow is not a structural emergency, but ordinary clerical reconciliation. When administrative staff arrive at their offices, they may well encounter isolated formatting quirks,..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 41]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 68], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 68, "author": member:theyknow, "at": d"2012-08-06T04:51:00.000Z", "body": "Atmosphere entry is right around the bend now... keeping my ears glued to the radio gear to see if Odyssey catches it.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Monitoring the carrier frequencies while Walter and kookaburra watch the broadcast.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an overcomplicated, multi-billion-dollar gamble that hands over far too much public money and faith to automated software that testing on Earth can never fully prove.","score":0,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"enjoys his company and calm perspective while still believing he puts too much faith in pre-flight testing"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"sees his classroom excitement as caught up in the official show"}],"commitments":["listen to the radio bands and monitor the Odyssey carrier tones during the landing attempt"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "text": "TheyKnow: \"TheyKnow's private note to self, Thu Aug 24, 2006 3:30 pm GMT\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T12:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 6]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T01:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 16]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 126]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T04:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 133]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-27T19:47:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-06T04:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 161]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-21T22:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 67]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 15]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 47]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T16:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 51]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T19:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 55]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T03:30:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-028⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 69], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 69, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T04:56:00.000Z", "body": "Watching a room full of young students go quiet over a science broadcast is a fine sight, kookaburra99. I dare say moments like this stay with a youngster for decades. I still recall gathering by the wireless as a lad, hanging on every crackle of static.\n\nDown at JPL they are passing round the lucky peanuts, I see. A harmless bit of tradition dating back to the Ranger Moon probes in '64. Back on the night shift we had our own superstitions - one fellow insisted on placing a clean punch card on top of the card reader before every monthly ledger run. It did not alter the circuits, of course, but it kept everyone calm while the machinery got on with the job.\n\nNow we are only minutes away from the top of the Martian atmosphere. Direct line of sight to Earth will drop away shortly, and everything hinges on Odyssey picking up the automated descent tones. Pacing will not help, so we just wait quietly for the signal.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "TheyKnow and I are both keeping the quiet vigil, but kookaburra99's students have the better view of history tonight.", "state": {"position":"The mission is an admirable, thoroughly engineered undertaking, and now we must simply wait calmly as the automated descent begins to see what the telemetry tells us.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"sharing a good-natured vigil despite his persistent fretting"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased his classroom is engaged and watching"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto with tea to watch the landing feed"],"shift":"Relaxing into the quiet countdown with his tea as atmospheric entry draws near."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is should wait to see what telemetry reveals once Odyssey picks it up"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-25T12:16:37.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-010⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 70], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 70, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:27:00.000Z", "body": "Atmospheric entry confirmed, seven minutes of terror officially underway now! The whole room is holding its breath.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I'm still planning on grabbing a celebratory drink after work if this landing actually comes off.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is a daring, inspiring leap for science that fully justifies the cost and risks as the landing sequence begins.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"deeply touched by his nostalgic reflections and steadying presence"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"glad he is at least watching along despite his habitual cynicism"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"grateful for his earlier engineering context"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream live with her class","grab a celebratory drink after work if the landing succeeds"],"shift":"She is thrilled to see her classroom of students captivated by the broadcast as the final descent approaches."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 41]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-05T17:38:49.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-024⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T03:30:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-028⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "Now we are only minutes away from the top of the Martian atmosphere. Direct line of sight to Earth will drop away shortly, and everything hinges on Odyssey picking up the automated descent tones. P..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 71], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 71, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:28:00.000Z", "body": "Proper sitting on the edge of the sofa here, tea gone stone cold!", "quote": NONE, "note": "Can hardly breathe watching this telemetry roll in.", "state": {"position":"We are right in the thick of the landing now, and while it is nerve-wracking, it is an incredible spectacle that is thoroughly worth getting behind.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"loves his steady, nostalgic stories that take the edge off the nerves"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"shares the excitement and anticipation of watching with the family"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"pleased to see him finally quiet down and just watch the dials"}],"commitments":["buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"],"shift":"He is caught up in the suspense of atmospheric entry alongside the others with his cuppa in hand."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Spot on with that!\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 11]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T07:46:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 21]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 25]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-04T21:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 38]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T20:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 56]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "Now we are only minutes away from the top of the Martian atmosphere. Direct line of sight to Earth will drop away shortly, and everything hinges on Odyssey picking up the automated descent tones. P..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:24:34.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-030⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 72], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 72, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "body": "The telemetry coming through the Mars Odyssey relay confirms the supersonic parachute has deployed as scheduled. That takes care of the bulk of the kinetic energy shed during atmospheric friction, but the descent sequence is by no means complete.\n\nTo keep our heads clear while the commentary runs, remember the sequence of mechanical hurdles remaining right now:\n\n1. Heat shield separation, exposing the radar and terminal guidance systems.\n2. Powered descent stage ignition on the retrorockets to brake the vehicle.\n3. The sky crane descent, lowering the rover on bridles before severed cords allow the stage to fly clear.\n\nAll of this has already occurred fourteen minutes ago in reality, so we are merely watching historical data confirm whether the onboard guidance executed autonomously without a fault. It is fascinating to watch the Odyssey link hold its lock so reliably, but we must wait for the actual touchdown telemetry before drawing any conclusions about the vehicle's integrity.\n\nIn short: excellent progress on the descent software so far, but the most delicate mechanical manoeuvre is still ahead of us.", "quote": NONE, "note": "The descent telemetry through Odyssey is immaculate so far, but the sky crane separation remains the true test.", "state": {"position":"The mission's scientific value fully justifies the investment, but its ultimate success still depends entirely on the geochemical analysis to come after surviving this critical entry sequence.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his calm, disciplined approach to letting the automated procedures run without fuss"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"notes their excitement but remains focused strictly on the technical telemetry"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"pleased to see him keeping quiet and watching the feed for once"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA landing commentary live"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "New Zealand servers's status is seeing extra traffic as people check connections"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "New Zealand's television broadcasts status is completely normal"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "TheyKnow: \"getting the kettle on\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "text": "Furthermore, RTU polling protocols communicate raw register values - binary status flags, analog-to-digital converter readings, and basic telemetry words. The polling cycle does not evaluate Julian..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T12:38:00.000Z", "text": "Attempting to dismiss these verified results by imagining hypothetical failures in rural substations is simply untenable. Primary electrical grids operate under continuous regional monitoring. A tr..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T15:37:00.000Z", "text": "Looking at network ping times from Dublin or watching city streetlamps stay on doesn't tell us how the main water treatment valves or substation relays hold up over the next twenty-four hours. A lo..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T11:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 24]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T21:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 57]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 73], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 73, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:31:00.000Z", "body": "The parachute deployment is certainly encouraging news, and seeing the Odyssey relay hold steady gives one confidence in the communications path.\n\nStill, as quasistatic rightly points out, the hardest part of the business remains ahead. Shedding velocity is one thing, but bringing the rocket stage to bear and letting down that sky crane on nylon lines is entirely untested in the field. \n\nNo sense getting ahead of ourselves just yet. We only need a few more minutes of patience while the telemetry works its way through. Let us see the wheels touch down first.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic has the right measure of things, keeping everyone grounded while the tricky sky crane sequence plays out.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's supersonic parachute deployment is encouraging progress, but we must steadily await the final telemetry on the sky crane and touchdown before celebrating.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his methodical, level-headed breakdown of the remaining steps"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"sharing a good-natured vigil despite his persistent fretting"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased his classroom is engaged and watching"}],"commitments":["staying up past 1 a.m. in Toronto with tea to watch the landing feed"],"shift":"Heartened by the successful parachute deployment while calmly keeping in mind the critical rocket descent still to come."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is should wait to see what telemetry reveals once Odyssey picks it up"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:29:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 145]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-06T03:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 62]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "Now we are only minutes away from the top of the Martian atmosphere. Direct line of sight to Earth will drop away shortly, and everything hinges on Odyssey picking up the automated descent tones. P..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:28:53.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-031⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 74], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 74, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:31:00.000Z", "body": "Stream is solid here too. Crane has to do the business now, wait and see.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 73], "text": "Let us see the wheels touch down first."}, "note": "Waiting to see the wheels touch down before I celebrate anything.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is still worth the investment compared to usual government waste, but we need to see actual touchdown telemetry on the screen before declaring it a success.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady, calm view on waiting for the actual touchdown"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear breakdown of the remaining landing stages"},{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"amused by his cold tea as the tension mounts"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream at half six Dublin time","have a celebratory pint with PintOfMild if the landing succeeds"],"shift":"He is pleased the stream and relay are holding up and the parachute deployed, but remains reserved until the sky crane actually puts wheels on the ground."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's status is on final approach"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-15T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 11]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-27T13:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 162]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T21:10:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 5]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-02T16:08:00.000Z", "text": "Trusting lines of computer code when you're completely blind across millions of miles is a dangerous game, folks... You have that fourteen minute lag, and then the whole relay hinges on an eleven-y..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T01:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T15:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 49]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T17:02:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 52]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:28:53.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-031⟩}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "text": "All of this has already occurred fourteen minutes ago in reality, so we are merely watching historical data confirm whether the onboard guidance executed autonomously without a fault. It is fascina..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 75], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 75, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:31:00.000Z", "body": "parachute opened, now here comes the scary sky crane part, fingers crossed so hard!!", "quote": NONE, "note": "I am definitely grabbing that drink after work if this crazy sky crane actually pulls it off.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity is an inspiring scientific triumph in the making, and the incredible tension of the landing proves why bold exploration is worth every effort.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his clear, orderly breakdown of the critical mechanical stages"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"warmed by his kind words about her students and his calm perspective"},{"member":"TheyKnow","feeling":"glad he is following along closely on his radio gear"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA stream live with her class","grab a celebratory drink after work if the landing succeeds"],"shift":"Watching the parachute deploy and reading quasistatic's clear breakdown has her classroom holding its breath for the sky crane sequence."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "wire queues's status is clear"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "NOC screens's status is completely flat"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"1999-12-31T09:09:00.000Z", "text": "I have to head into the office later on for the dreaded evening shift, but I will definitely be popping back in here during my tea breaks to see how kookaburra99 and the Aussies are getting on. Kee..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "text": "[quoting: \"Even when an odd glitch turns up - like those police breath-test units in Hong Kong this morning - the officers simply write the birth dates down in a notebook while the readings carry o..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2002-01-02T10:10:00.000Z", "text": "Industrial action and localized coin pinches will create isolated queues, certainly. However, the automated networks have already demonstrated their capacity to move high volumes of currency withou..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 41]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:04:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 64]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 70]}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 76], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 76, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "body": "The announcement has just come through from JPL operations: touchdown is confirmed via the Canberra tracking station. The sky crane manoeuvre executed its programmed descent sequence, and the vehicle is resting on the surface inside Gale Crater.\n\nIt is certainly an impressive achievement in autonomous systems engineering. One must credit the automated guidance routines for executing such a complex mechanical sequence without human intervention. However, a successful landing is merely the physical precondition for the actual programme. \n\nNow begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and how the internal components withstood the mechanical deceleration. Until the engineering staff can verify system health and deploy the main mast over the coming days, we have no substantive confirmation of the rover's scientific viability. A functional landing gear does not yet equal an operational analytical laboratory.", "quote": NONE, "note": "A confirmed touchdown is fine engineering, but until the instruments transmit valid baselines, celebrating is premature.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's value hinges on whether it lands intact and delivers verifiable empirical science, not on the excitement of its landing telemetry.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"pleased by his precise reminder about the 14-minute signal delay and systematic technical sequence"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"respects his calm, disciplined approach to awaiting actual touchdown data"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"weary of emotional buzzwords like seven minutes of terror and superstitious finger-crossing"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "private", "text": "OffByOne: \"OffByOne's private note to self, Wed Dec 29, 1999 3:08 pm GMT: The card terminal glitch in Britain provides concrete proof that retail software errors do not cascade into industrial para..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-06-22T17:00:00.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-005⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-03T11:17:59.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-016⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "Now we are only minutes away from the top of the Martian atmosphere. Direct line of sight to Earth will drop away shortly, and everything hinges on Odyssey picking up the automated descent tones. P..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:28:53.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-031⟩}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:31:27.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-032⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 77], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 77, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "body": "OffByOne is quite right to keep his boots on the ground. A safe landing is a grand relief and a marvellous feat of engineering, but now the real work begins.\n\nWe must wait patiently for the health checks and those first small images from Odyssey. Let the systems settle before drawing conclusions.\n\nWalter", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 76], "text": "Until the engineering staff can verify system health and deploy the main mast over the coming days, we have no substantive confirmation of the rover's scientific viability."}, "note": "OffByOne has the right attitude: celebrate the touchdown, but wait for the diagnostic printout before calling the job done.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity has touched down safely on Mars, a magnificent engineering feat, though we must now patiently await initial system health checks and data.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his sensible, measured focus on systems verification"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his steady, accurate tracking of the sequence"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"glad the students witnessed a successful landing"}],"commitments":[],"shift":"Relieved and thrilled that touchdown has been confirmed after staying up for the descent."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Punchcard's opinion is should wait to see what telemetry reveals once Odyssey picks it up"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:45:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 139]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 12]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-06T03:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 62]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 66]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "text": "All of this has already occurred fourteen minutes ago in reality, so we are merely watching historical data confirm whether the onboard guidance executed autonomously without a fault. It is fascina..."}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:31:45.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-033⟩}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 78], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 78, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "body": "The arrival of that first thumbnail from the rear hazard avoidance camera showing the wheel on the Martian surface is an immense relief. To see the sky crane deposit a metric ton of machinery into Gale Crater and clear the area safely is an outstanding engineering achievement by the JPL team, and everyone who designed that descent software deserves proper credit.\n\nAt the same time, we must maintain perspective as the celebration unfolds on the stream. A rover intact on the regolith merely provides the mobile chassis. The substantial scientific justification for this mission rests entirely upon whether the complex analytical payload survived that violent transition intact. The internal spectrometers, the sampling arm, and the geochemical laboratory inside the chassis are far more sensitive to shock and thermal stress than a ruggedised aluminium wheel.\n\nWe will not have true confirmation of the rover's scientific capability until the mast is raised, the main communication antennae are deployed, and the instruments run through their diagnostic routines over the coming sols. It is a splendid morning for aerospace engineering, but the scientific return remains entirely to be proven.", "quote": NONE, "note": "A wheel on the ground is a triumph for the JPL software team, but the mission only succeeds if the internal spectrometers survived the shock intact.", "state": {"position":"The successful touchdown is an outstanding engineering feat, but the investment is only justified if the rover's scientific instruments survived and can perform their geochemical analysis.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"strongly agrees with his measured emphasis on verifying platform health before celebrating"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his continued patience and sensible perspective as the systems settle"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"views their emotional excitement as premature until instrument viability is established"}],"commitments":["watch the NASA landing commentary live"],"shift":"Maintains that the mission is well worth doing, while turning focus from landing execution to verifying instrument integrity."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T07:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 164]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:05:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 99]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "Now we are only minutes away from the top of the Martian atmosphere. Direct line of sight to Earth will drop away shortly, and everything hinges on Odyssey picking up the automated descent tones. P..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "text": "All of this has already occurred fourteen minutes ago in reality, so we are merely watching historical data confirm whether the onboard guidance executed autonomously without a fault. It is fascina..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 72]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 79], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 79, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:39:00.000Z", "body": "my classroom erupted when that wheel photo popped up, absolutely unbelievable engineering! but quasistatic makes a really fair point, the chassis made it down fine but now we have to hold our breath to see if the delicate lab instruments survived that wild ride intact. still definitely celebrating this arvo though!", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 78], "text": "The internal spectrometers, the sampling arm, and the geochemical laboratory inside the chassis are far more sensitive to shock and thermal stress than a ruggedised aluminium wheel."}, "note": "I am definitely grabbing that celebratory drink after work now that the landing succeeded.", "state": {"position":"Touchdown is an incredible triumph of engineering and inspiration, though the full scientific return depends on whether the sensitive instruments survived to do their job.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his clear, level-headed reminder that the scientific instruments still need to be verified"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his precise assessment of the autonomous engineering"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his calm, sensible perspective on letting the systems settle"}],"commitments":["grab a celebratory drink after work now that the landing succeeded"],"shift":"The nerve-wracking sky crane landing succeeded and sent back its first image, making her thrilled by the engineering triumph while taking on board quasistatic's point that instrument verification comes next."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's opinion is astonishing piece of software and systems engineering if all the stages execute properly"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's schedule is less than an hour to go until landing"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 41]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 70]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 75]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "text": "At the same time, we must maintain perspective as the celebration unfolds on the stream. A rover intact on the regolith merely provides the mobile chassis. The substantial scientific justification ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 80], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 80, "author": member:pintofmild, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:48:00.000Z", "body": "Blimey, absolutely mental that it actually worked! That wheel picture made the early alarm worth it. Fair point though, let's see if all the clever science kit inside survived the jolt before popping open the bubbly. Still, what a result! Head of Steam next Friday is definitely on, cheers :D", "quote": NONE, "note": "They actually pulled off the landing, but now we'll see if the science gear inside made it through without breaking.", "state": {"position":"They have pulled off an incredible landing with that sky crane, though now we have to see if the delicate scientific instruments inside actually survived the trip.","score":3,"certainty":"medium","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"agrees with his sensible reminder that the science instruments still need to prove themselves"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his steady advice not to get ahead of ourselves"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"shares the pure joy and celebration of seeing that first wheel photo"}],"commitments":["buy the first round at the Head of Steam in Leeds next Friday"],"shift":"He is thrilled that the crazy landing actually worked, while readily taking on board the group's point that the onboard science kit still has to be tested."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's opinion is astonishing piece of software and systems engineering if all the stages execute properly"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "PintOfMild: \"Spot on with that!\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 171]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-05T11:32:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 178]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T11:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 56]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:17:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T12:48:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 100]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T15:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 124]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T00:36:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 17]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 25]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T12:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 33]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-04T21:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 38]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-05T13:21:00.000Z", "text": "Breaking it down into clean bullet points does not make the danger any less real, folks... Writing off the risk as mere routine procedure sounds fine in a textbook, but out there you have a thousan..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T20:59:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 56]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "text": "At the same time, we must maintain perspective as the celebration unfolds on the stream. A rover intact on the regolith merely provides the mobile chassis. The substantial scientific justification ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 81], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 81, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:49:00.000Z", "body": "Small correction: the rover mass is not quite a metric ton. The flight weight of Curiosity is approximately 899 kilograms, or 900 kilograms if one rounds conventionally. That is certainly substantially heavier than the previous rovers, but precision in mass calculations matters when discussing kinetic energy transfer.\n\nThat minor detail aside, the subsequent operational phases will require an enormous amount of patience. Transmitting a single 64 by 64 pixel thumbnail through a UHF link on Mars Odyssey merely confirms the camera optics cleared the dust cover and the basic data bus functioned during relay. The actual analytical hardware - namely the internal ovens, the gas chromatograph, and the robotic turret drill - must undergo extensive cold-starts and baseline calibrations before any geological findings can be considered valid. In the Netherlands we have a sensible saying, 'men moet de huid niet verkopen voor de beer geschoten is'. Delivering the laboratory to the surface of Gale Crater is an undeniable triumph of aerospace logistics, but until the instrument spectra return verifiable data, the mission's ultimate return on investment remains completely undecided.", "quote": NONE, "note": "quasistatic and Punchcard understand the necessary discipline of waiting for instrument telemetry, while the rest are already celebrating a simple thumbnail.", "state": {"position":"A successful landing is an impressive engineering milestone, but the mission's value remains entirely undecided until the scientific instruments are systematically tested and proven operational.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"pleased by his rigorous insistence on testing the analytical payload before declaring success"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his disciplined patience in awaiting system diagnostics"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"mildly glad she conceded the need to verify the sensitive lab instruments despite her emotional celebration"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne: \"The issue is that the proposed resolution creates a very messy precedent.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T13:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 22]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T19:14:00.000Z", "text": "I shall certainly have my kettle on well before half past five to follow the telemetry feed. One can appreciate the enormous ambition of the engineering trial while still recognising that safe touc..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "text": "All of this has already occurred fourteen minutes ago in reality, so we are merely watching historical data confirm whether the onboard guidance executed autonomously without a fault. It is fascina..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 76]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "text": "At the same time, we must maintain perspective as the celebration unfolds on the stream. A rover intact on the regolith merely provides the mobile chassis. The substantial scientific justification ..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 82], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 82, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:54:00.000Z", "body": "Point taken on the precise mass, OffByOne, and the Dutch proverb fits the instrument calibrations ahead quite nicely.", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne remains as rigorously pedantic as ever, which is precisely why I value his contributions.", "state": {"position":"A successful landing is an incredible engineering feat, but whether the mission is truly worth the expenditure depends on proving the delicate geochemical instruments inside survived and function.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his precise technical rigor and shared insistence on instrument calibration"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his grounded, patient outlook"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased they took the caution about delicate instrumentation on board"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's schedule is less than an hour to go until landing"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "quasistatic: \"We should inspect the actual wording of the redraft before making dramatic claims.\""}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T20:12:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 157]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-07-23T12:35:51.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-009⟩}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "news", "at": d"2012-08-03T11:17:59.000Z", "from": news:⟨curiosity-016⟩}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 72]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 78]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:49:00.000Z", "text": "That minor detail aside, the subsequent operational phases will require an enormous amount of patience. Transmitting a single 64 by 64 pixel thumbnail through a UHF link on Mars Odyssey merely conf..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 83], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 83, "author": member:offbyone, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:57:00.000Z", "body": "It is encouraging to see that perspective shared. With the landing complete, the public broadcast will likely dwindle down into speeches, but the real test is the engineering log over the next several sols. We shall have to wait patiently for those diagnostic readouts.", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 82], "text": "Point taken on the precise mass, OffByOne, and the Dutch proverb fits the instrument calibrations ahead quite nicely."}, "note": "quasistatic remains one of the few members capable of viewing technical operations through evidence rather than premature emotion.", "state": {"position":"A landing and a thumbnail image confirm mechanical delivery, but whether the mission is worth its expenditure remains undecided until the analytical instruments are calibrated and return scientific data.","score":2,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"pleased he gracefully accepted the mass correction and shares the focus on rigorous calibration"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"approves of his disciplined patience in awaiting telemetry"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"tolerates her enthusiasm now that she concedes the instruments must be proven"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's opinion is quasistatic and Punchcard understand the necessary discipline of waiting for instrument telemetry, while the rest are already celebrating a simple thumbnail"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "OffByOne's expectation is expects tomorrow's formal session will confirm what mathematical evidence has demanded all along"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Resolution 6B's opinion is merely an administrative delay according to OffByOne"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T14:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 91]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-02T10:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 166]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2000-01-03T09:20:00.000Z", "text": "We have already seen examples over the weekend, such as the temporary glitch with Amtrak's tracking software, where automated fallbacks and manual procedures ensured normal operations continued wit..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T22:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 67]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T20:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 141]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-04T09:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 150]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-18T13:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 47]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-19T08:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 55]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T20:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 4]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-02T05:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 76]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:49:00.000Z", "text": "That minor detail aside, the subsequent operational phases will require an enormous amount of patience. Transmitting a single 64 by 64 pixel thumbnail through a UHF link on Mars Odyssey merely conf..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 84], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 84, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T05:59:00.000Z", "body": "That Dutch proverb fits the bill nicely. A grand machine sitting on the red dust is a fine sight to behold, but a laboratory is only as good as the numbers it sends home.\n\nNow comes the slow, methodical business of checking every switch and wire. One cannot rush baseline tests or mast deployments after a journey of that distance. The flight controllers will take their time, and rightly so.\n\nAt nearly two in the morning here in Toronto, my old bones are telling me it is time to turn in. A tremendous triumph for the engineers tonight, and we can inspect the morning readouts in due course.\n\nWalter", "quote": NONE, "note": "OffByOne and quasistatic are right to keep their eyes on the instrument calibration logs rather than the fanfare.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's safe touchdown and first thumbnails are a tremendous engineering triumph, but we must patiently wait for system health checks and instrument calibrations before evaluating the mission.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"admires his level-headed insistence on waiting for diagnostic readouts"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"values his steady, sensible grasp of engineering realities"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"glad her students shared in the excitement of the landing"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:38:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 28]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-06T03:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 62]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 66]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "text": "All of this has already occurred fourteen minutes ago in reality, so we are merely watching historical data confirm whether the onboard guidance executed autonomously without a fault. It is fascina..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 77]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "text": "At the same time, we must maintain perspective as the celebration unfolds on the stream. A rover intact on the regolith merely provides the mobile chassis. The substantial scientific justification ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:49:00.000Z", "text": "That minor detail aside, the subsequent operational phases will require an enormous amount of patience. Transmitting a single 64 by 64 pixel thumbnail through a UHF link on Mars Odyssey merely conf..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 85], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 85, "author": member:quasistatic, "at": d"2012-08-06T06:01:00.000Z", "body": "Get some rest, Walter. It was well worth the early start on this side of the Atlantic, but you have certainly earned your sleep.\n\nNow that the initial excitement on the stream is winding down, the real work shifts entirely to the flight software teams and their diagnostic passes. Raising the mast and high-gain antenna will take several sols, and we cannot expect any meaningful baseline readouts from the internal instruments until those comms links are fully configured. It will be a slow, quiet week of data verification, but that is precisely how thorough engineering ought to proceed.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Walter has the right idea turning in; the diagnostic phase will be slow and requires patience.", "state": {"position":"The landing is an immense engineering triumph, but the mission's true scientific value depends on whether the delicate analytical instruments survived and function properly.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"appreciates his technical precision and shared focus on calibration"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"values his patient, methodical perspective"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"pleased they took the caution about delicate instrumentation on board"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's purpose is prepare the way for future searches and returning samples from Mars, not life detection"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity: \"The rover has separated from the descent stage\""}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's opinion is astonishing piece of software and systems engineering if all the stages execute properly"}, {"kind": "fact", "text": "Quasistatic's opinion critique is believes TheyKnow treats harmless institutional folklore as evidence of technical weakness"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T15:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 24]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-29T23:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-30T09:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 50]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T18:14:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 151]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-03T18:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 176]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-30T09:54:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 40]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-26T12:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 154]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T10:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 1]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-03T06:51:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 20]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T12:00:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 46]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:30:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 72]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "text": "At the same time, we must maintain perspective as the celebration unfolds on the stream. A rover intact on the regolith merely provides the mobile chassis. The substantial scientific justification ..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 78]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:49:00.000Z", "text": "That minor detail aside, the subsequent operational phases will require an enormous amount of patience. Transmitting a single 64 by 64 pixel thumbnail through a UHF link on Mars Odyssey merely conf..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 86], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 86, "author": member:punchcard, "at": d"2012-08-06T06:04:00.000Z", "body": "Thank you, quasistatic. I am just shutting down the machine now.\n\nBack in '79 during our big core store upgrade, we had junior analysts wanting to run production jobs the very minute the power lights came green. Our senior systems man told them straight: you do not ask a horse to pull a wagon until you have checked every buckle on the harness. That is exactly what JPL will be doing on Mars this week. The celebratory speeches are fine, but the diagnostics tell the tale.\n\nA pleasure sharing the vigil with you all tonight.\n\nWalter", "quote": {"post": post:["curiosity", 85], "text": "It will be a slow, quiet week of data verification, but that is precisely how thorough engineering ought to proceed."}, "note": "A relief to see the rover down safely, but the true test of the mission will be the instrument baselines over the coming sols.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity's touchdown is an undeniable engineering triumph, but the true scientific value will only be proven as the instruments undergo slow, methodical verification in the sols ahead.","score":4,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his precise, patient approach to instrument calibration and data"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"appreciates his steady engineering perspective and kind sign-off"},{"member":"kookaburra99","feeling":"glad her students shared in the excitement of the landing"}],"commitments":[]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "fact", "text": "Curiosity's schedule is less than an hour to go until landing"}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T15:23:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 7]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T02:11:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 130]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-29T18:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 32]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 79]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-16T01:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 13]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T21:18:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 123]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-03T20:45:00.000Z", "text": "A prolonged task always brings operational friction. Narrow transmission passes and finicky mechanical parts are the bread and butter of working with remote machinery. One expects things to go slig..."}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T08:37:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 32]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T17:55:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 35]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T19:35:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 37]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-05T14:39:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 48]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-06T03:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 62]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:28:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 66]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T04:56:00.000Z", "text": "Now we are only minutes away from the top of the Martian atmosphere. Direct line of sight to Earth will drop away shortly, and everything hinges on Odyssey picking up the automated descent tones. P..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 77]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "text": "At the same time, we must maintain perspective as the celebration unfolds on the stream. A rover intact on the regolith merely provides the mobile chassis. The substantial scientific justification ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:49:00.000Z", "text": "That minor detail aside, the subsequent operational phases will require an enormous amount of patience. Transmitting a single 64 by 64 pixel thumbnail through a UHF link on Mars Odyssey merely conf..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 87], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 87, "author": member:kookaburra99, "at": d"2012-08-06T06:14:00.000Z", "body": "The final school bell just went here in Melbourne, and honestly, trying to explain to a room of thirteen-year-olds why we now have to wait days just to see a mast raise or a laser fire was an exercise in patience in itself. They wanted full colour panoramas straight away, but Punchcard's harness analogy is spot on. \n\nIt is so easy to get caught up in the huge adrenaline rush of the touchdown and forget that this massive 900 kg contraption is essentially an automated chemistry department sitting in freezing conditions. OffByOne and quasistatic are totally right about the quiet grind ahead. The software handoffs, the power bus checks, warming up those tiny internal ovens, and testing whether the drill mechanism took any hidden mechanical stress during that cable drop are what actually make or break the mission now. The sky crane did its job brilliantly, but proving the analytical kit works is the real test. \n\nI am finally shutting down the classroom projector and heading out for that celebratory drink. Sleep well Walter, what an unforgettable afternoon to watch together.", "quote": NONE, "note": "I'm still planning on grabbing that well-earned drink after work now that the landing is done.", "state": {"position":"The landing was an unbelievable engineering feat that inspired my students, though we have to wait for the slow diagnostic checks to see if the delicate science instruments actually survived.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"admires how clearly he frames the engineering and scientific stages"},{"member":"OffByOne","feeling":"respects his meticulous focus on instrument calibrations and diagnostic readouts"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"appreciates his patient, sensible perspective and steady presence"}],"commitments":["celebrate with a drink after work this afternoon"]}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-27T08:07:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 19]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-31T03:16:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 73]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T00:01:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 120]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T05:22:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 136]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T07:21:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 144]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2001-12-31T23:43:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 77]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-02T01:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 113]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2006-08-24T03:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["pluto", 85]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-01T13:56:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 2]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T03:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 9]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-02T06:52:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 13]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-04T05:19:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 31]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"2012-08-05T01:40:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 41]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:27:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 70]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:31:00.000Z", "from": post:["curiosity", 75]}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:33:00.000Z", "text": "Now begins the systematic verification of the platform. We must see whether the hazard avoidance cameras transmit their initial telemetry through Odyssey, whether the power bus remains nominal, and..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:37:00.000Z", "text": "At the same time, we must maintain perspective as the celebration unfolds on the stream. A rover intact on the regolith merely provides the mobile chassis. The substantial scientific justification ..."}, {"kind": "fact", "at": d"2012-08-06T05:49:00.000Z", "text": "That minor detail aside, the subsequent operational phases will require an enormous amount of patience. Transmitting a single 64 by 64 pixel thumbnail through a UHF link on Mars Odyssey merely conf..."}]};
INSERT INTO post {"id": post:["curiosity", 88], "thread": thread:curiosity, "n": 88, "author": member:sysop_kev, "at": d"2012-08-06T06:22:00.000Z", "body": "Pint is definitely earned, PintOfMild. Fair play to them, the crane pulling that off without binning the lot into the gravel was worth getting up for. Stream held up grand too. Punchcard and quasistatic have the right of it though - until the instruments pass their self-tests and power up properly, the real work has barely started.", "quote": NONE, "note": "Looking forward to that pint with PintOfMild on Friday now that the wheels are down.", "state": {"position":"Curiosity pulled off an incredible landing, proving well worth the outlay, but we still need to see the instruments pass their diagnostics before calling the science mission an outright success.","score":3,"certainty":"high","towards":[{"member":"PintOfMild","feeling":"ready to join him for that celebratory pint now that it touched down safely"},{"member":"Punchcard","feeling":"agrees with his steady reminder that diagnostics and calibration come first"},{"member":"quasistatic","feeling":"respects his sensible emphasis on checking the analytical instruments"}],"commitments":["have a celebratory pint with PintOfMild at the Head of Steam"],"shift":"With visual confirmation of the wheels on the surface, he is fully satisfied the landing succeeded, though he agrees with the cautious members that testing the science kit is the next hurdle."}, "recalled": [{"kind": "private", "at": d"1999-12-26T16:09:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 10]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-27T06:50:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 18]}, {"kind": "forum", "at": d"1999-12-30T21:24:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 63]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2000-01-01T01:26:00.000Z", "from": post:["y2k", 128]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-01T16:25:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 107]}, {"kind": "private", "at": d"2002-01-03T00:20:00.000Z", "from": post:["euro", 129]}, {"kind": "private"